window.DM_BANK_8006_FULL = [{"id":"8006-foundational-001","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade teacher reviews running records and finds that several students decode single-syllable short-vowel words accurately but consistently misread longer words such as \"boat,\" \"tree,\" and \"play.\" Which spelling pattern should the teacher introduce first with these students?","options":["Vowel teams, such as the vowel pair in \"boat\"","R-controlled vowels, such as the vowel sound in \"car\"","Closed syllables, such as the vowel sound in \"cat\"","Consonant clusters, such as the /st/ at the beginning of \"stop\""],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies for common phonics patterns. The words the students are missing all contain two vowels that work together to represent one sound. Introducing vowel teams directly targets the pattern appearing in the students' errors, which is the most efficient use of instructional time.","dex":{"R-controlled vowels, such as the vowel sound in \"car\"":"Equating a high-frequency phonics pattern with the pattern that deserves priority. Relevance to the misreadings is what makes a pattern worth teaching now. R-controlled vowels are a legitimate phonics pattern, but they do not appear in the words the students are misreading. Teaching it now would address a pattern that is not yet causing difficulty while leaving the actual gap unaddressed.","Closed syllables, such as the vowel sound in \"cat\"":"Treating what students already read correctly as what they need to be taught. Re-teaching a secure pattern adds no new decoding power. Closed syllables with short vowels are exactly what these students already read accurately. Re-teaching a mastered pattern adds no new decoding power and does not address the multi-vowel patterns in the error words.","Consonant clusters, such as the /st/ at the beginning of \"stop\"":"Confusing a pattern students have already mastered with the pattern generating their errors. The error data, not the scope and sequence, should set the teaching point. Students who decode single-syllable words accurately are generally already handling initial consonant clusters successfully. This instruction would target a pattern the students have essentially mastered rather than the one producing their errors."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-002","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A fourth-grade student reads the word \"unhappy\" accurately. When asked what it means, the student says, \"It means you are happy again — the 'un' part makes it happen two times.\" Which analysis of this response is most accurate?","options":["The student has confused the prefix un- with the prefix re-.","The student has correctly identified the base word but has not yet learned any prefixes.","The student has applied a closed-syllable decoding strategy to a multisyllabic word.","The student has confused the suffix -y with the prefix un-."],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to analyze student responses to identify partial conceptions. The student correctly identifies \"happy\" as the base and correctly recognizes that the added letters form an affix, but assigns the wrong meaning to it. The meaning given, \"happen two times\" or \"again,\" is the meaning of re-, not un-. This is a partial conception: the structural analysis is sound while the semantic assignment is not.","dex":{"The student has correctly identified the base word but has not yet learned any prefixes.":"Mistaking a partial conception for the complete absence of one. Because the structural analysis is sound, instruction should refine the idea rather than start over. This analysis understates what the student demonstrated. The student did apply prefix knowledge by attributing a specific meaning to un-, which means instruction should refine an existing concept rather than introduce prefixes from scratch.","The student has applied a closed-syllable decoding strategy to a multisyllabic word.":"Confusing a decoding difficulty with a meaning difficulty. Accurate oral reading rules out syllabication as the obstacle and points to morphological meaning instead. The student read the word accurately, so decoding is not the difficulty. The error appears only when the student explains the meaning, which places the problem at the level of morphological meaning rather than syllable-based word attack.","The student has confused the suffix -y with the prefix un-.":"Misjudging where the affix boundary falls by taking a letter inside the base as the affix under discussion. The student's explicit reference to un- is the decisive evidence. The student's comment focuses entirely on \"un\" and attributes a meaning to it. The final -y in \"happy\" belongs to the base word and plays no part in the student's explanation, so the confusion is between two prefixes, not between a prefix and a suffix."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-003","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A teacher is planning instruction for students who decode closed-syllable words such as \"cat\" and \"mop\" accurately but stumble on \"baking\" and \"theme.\" Which syllable type should the teacher introduce next?","options":["Closed syllables","Vowel-consonant-e (VCe) syllables","R-controlled syllables","Open syllables"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of syllable types and the sequence in which they are taught. Both 'baking' and 'theme' follow a long-vowel, single-consonant, silent-e pattern, so teaching the VCe pattern explains why the final e is silent while the preceding vowel says its long sound.","dex":{"Closed syllables":"Recommending instruction in a skill the student has already mastered. The prompt explicitly rules this out by describing what students can already do. A closed syllable ends in a consonant and its vowel is short. The prompt states that students already read closed-syllable words accurately, so reteaching this type does not address the observed difficulty.","R-controlled syllables":"Broadening 'a vowel followed by a consonant' into a single category. Which consonant follows the vowel determines the syllable type. R-controlled syllables are those in which a vowel is followed by r, as in 'car' or 'bird.' In 'baking' and 'theme' the long vowel is followed by k and m respectively, not by r.","Open syllables":"Inferring that a long vowel always signals an open syllable. A long vowel can also arise from a VCe pattern or a vowel team. An open syllable ends in a vowel and that vowel is long, as in the first syllable of 'ta-ble.' In these words the vowel is followed by a consonant, so the pattern does not apply."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-004","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A teacher wants students to be able to determine the meaning of unfamiliar multimorphemic words such as \"unhappiness.\" Which instructional approach best supports that goal?","options":["Having students locate the word in a dictionary and copy the entry into a notebook","Having students sound out the word syllable by syllable and check their pronunciation against a picture","Guiding students to identify the prefix, base word, and suffix and then combine their meanings","Providing the definition and asking students to use the word in a written sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of morphological analysis as a word-solving strategy. Separating 'un-' (not), 'happy,' and '-ness' (state of) and recombining their meanings gives students a transferable procedure they can apply to new multimorphemic words.","dex":{"Having students locate the word in a dictionary and copy the entry into a notebook":"Treating access to a reference tool as equivalent to instruction in deriving word meaning. Dictionary lookup is a reference skill rather than an analysis strategy, and copying an entry produces only shallow contact with the word. It also interrupts reading rather than supporting it.","Having students sound out the word syllable by syllable and check their pronunciation against a picture":"Equating successful pronunciation with comprehension of meaning. The Simple View of Reading treats word recognition and language comprehension as distinct contributors. Sounding out a word addresses pronunciation, not meaning. Decoding and comprehension are separate components of reading, and a student can produce a word aloud with no idea what it means.","Providing the definition and asking students to use the word in a written sentence":"Confusing providing an answer with teaching a strategy. Instruction should build a capability that transfers to untaught words. Supplying a definition gives students the meaning of this one word but teaches no procedure. Confronted later with 'unwillingness,' they are no better equipped than before."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-005","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A teacher is planning word-analysis instruction and wants the end-of-year target to match grade-level expectations. Which decoding task is most appropriate for students at the end of first grade?","options":["Using morphological analysis to unlock the meaning of words such as \"unhappy\" and \"replay\"","Using context to confirm the pronunciation of multisyllabic content words such as \"habitat\"","Blending three phonemes to read single-syllable words such as \"cat\" and \"mop\"","Decoding two-syllable words with common long-vowel patterns such as \"paper\" and \"reptile\""],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how word-analysis expectations shift by grade. By the end of first grade, students are expected to apply their letter-sound knowledge to two-syllable words that follow regular patterns, dividing the word into syllables and reading each part before blending them into a known word.","dex":{"Using morphological analysis to unlock the meaning of words such as \"unhappy\" and \"replay\"":"Introducing morphological analysis before students can reliably decode single- and two-syllable words Morphological analysis of prefixes and suffixes is normally introduced after students reliably decode single- and two-syllable words. Asking first graders to use it as a primary decoding route sets the task well above their current word-analysis repertoire.","Using context to confirm the pronunciation of multisyllabic content words such as \"habitat\"":"Using context to generate a pronunciation as a substitute for letter-sound analysis Checking a decoded word against context is a useful verification step, but it is not itself a decoding procedure. Using context to generate pronunciation bypasses the letter-sound analysis students need to develop.","Blending three phonemes to read single-syllable words such as \"cat\" and \"mop\"":"Treating beginning-of-year CVC blending as an appropriate end-of-first-grade decoding target Blending three phonemes in CVC words is an early-first-grade target, not an exit target. Choosing it holds students below grade level and leaves them without the syllable-level strategies the later grades assume."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-006","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which statement best describes how high-frequency words such as \"said,\" \"was,\" and \"they\" should be taught?","options":["By ensuring that students recognize them instantly and automatically through repeated encounters in connected text","By having students sound them out letter by letter each time they appear","By teaching their meanings first so that students can read them with understanding","By grouping them for instruction with other words that share the same spelling pattern"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the purpose of high-frequency word instruction. These words make up a large share of early texts, so the goal is instant recognition; repeated encounters in sentences and books build the automaticity that frees attention for comprehension.","dex":{"By having students sound them out letter by letter each time they appear":"Assuming every high-frequency word should be sounded out, including those with irregular spellings Several of the most frequent English words are spelling outliers: the ai in \"said\" represents a short e sound, and the ey in \"they\" is not the expected pattern. Requiring students to blend them every time prevents the automatic recognition the words demand.","By teaching their meanings first so that students can read them with understanding":"Confusing oral word meaning with printed word recognition Students almost always know these words orally before they meet them in print. The instructional problem is mapping a familiar spoken word onto an unfamiliar printed form, not teaching a new meaning.","By grouping them for instruction with other words that share the same spelling pattern":"Applying phonics-pattern grouping logic to words selected for their frequency rather than their regularity Pattern-based grouping is the organizing logic of phonics instruction, not of high-frequency word instruction. These words are selected and taught because of how often they occur, not because they illustrate a spelling generalization."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-007","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher writes \"was\" on the board, says the word, has students repeat it, and then has each student trace the letters in a tray of sand while saying the sounds. This routine primarily supports sight word recognition through","options":["explicit instruction in the meaning of the word","systematic practice in segmenting the word into individual phonemes","repeated, multisensory exposure that links the printed form, the pronunciation, and the physical movement together","practice in using the word in a spoken sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of techniques that expand sight word recognition. The routine combines seeing the word, hearing and saying it, and forming the letters by hand, so several sensory pathways are activated for the same spelling at the same time, which strengthens retention and speeds later recognition.","dex":{"explicit instruction in the meaning of the word":"Mistaking vocabulary instruction for the orthographic mapping that sight word work requires Nothing in the routine addresses what \"was\" means; students in kindergarten use the word in speech already. The routine targets the printed form, not the definition.","systematic practice in segmenting the word into individual phonemes":"Equating phonemic segmentation practice with practice in recognizing printed words Segmenting into phonemes is an auditory phonemic awareness task, and it typically stops short of print. This routine keeps the written word in view throughout and asks students to form letters, so the emphasis is on orthographic mapping rather than on sound segmentation alone.","practice in using the word in a spoken sentence":"Treating oral sentence production as a route to printed word recognition Producing a spoken sentence exercises oral language and syntax. It gives students no concentrated look at the spelling, which is where the recognition problem lies."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-008","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade student writes \"kik\" for \"kick.\" The teacher notes that every letter the student used represents a plausible sound. Which response best builds the student's understanding of spelling conventions?","options":["Having the student practice forming the letters k, i, and c correctly","Telling the student the correct spelling and having the student copy it five times","Deferring any discussion of the spelling until the student reads more fluently","Explaining that the /k/ sound at the end of a one-syllable word after a short vowel is usually spelled ck, and having the student sort words by their final /k/ spelling"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how conventional spellings are taught. The student's attempt is phonetically defensible, so the next move is to make the convention explicit and give the student a set of examples that reveals when the language uses k and when it uses ck. Sorting turns a one-off correction into a generalizable pattern.","dex":{"Having the student practice forming the letters k, i, and c correctly":"Attributing a spelling error to letter formation rather than to an unlearned spelling convention The student's letters are legible and correctly formed; the issue is which letter represents the final sound, not how the letters are written. Handwriting practice would leave the actual error untouched.","Telling the student the correct spelling and having the student copy it five times":"Equating repeated copying of a corrected word with instruction in a spelling generalization Copying a correct model may fix this one word for this one day, but it does not tell the student why ck is used here. Without attention to the pattern, the same error reappears with \"back,\" \"pick,\" and \"duck.\"","Deferring any discussion of the spelling until the student reads more fluently":"Assuming instruction in spelling conventions should wait until fluency improves Encoding and decoding develop together, and instruction in one supports the other. Waiting for fluency to improve first delays the spelling knowledge that would support both."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-009","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher wants to track students' decoding growth from week to week. Which assessment approach best serves that purpose?","options":["An end-of-unit written test in which students state phonics rules","A brief timed measure in which students read lists of decodable nonsense words","A running record of connected text administered at the end of each grading period","A letter-name inventory administered once in the fall"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of progress monitoring in foundational skills. A short nonsense word measure is quick enough to repeat frequently, produces a comparable score each time, and forces students to apply letter-sound correspondences rather than retrieve memorized words, which makes it sensitive to decoding growth over short intervals.","dex":{"An end-of-unit written test in which students state phonics rules":"Treating the ability to state a phonics rule as evidence of the ability to apply it while reading Being able to state a rule and being able to apply it while reading are different competencies. A student may recite a generalization accurately and still not use it to read an unfamiliar word.","A running record of connected text administered at the end of each grading period":"Using a periodic diagnostic assessment as a progress-monitoring measure A running record gives rich diagnostic detail, but administering it only at the end of a grading period is far too infrequent to show week-to-week movement or to prompt a timely change in instruction.","A letter-name inventory administered once in the fall":"Confusing screening, which identifies risk, with progress monitoring, which measures response to instruction A once-a-year letter-name inventory is a screening tool used to flag risk. It cannot show whether instruction is working, because it is not repeated and it measures a skill that decoding instruction has already moved past."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-010","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher is introducing the r-controlled vowel pattern found in \"car,\" \"fork,\" and \"her.\" Which instructional sequence best reflects research on teaching phonics patterns?","options":["Having students memorize a statement of the rule before they read any words that contain it","Having students read a leveled text first and infer the pattern from the words they meet in it","Having students read words with the pattern, sort those words by the vowel-plus-r spelling, and then read them again in connected decodable text","Having students copy the pattern words and draw a picture to illustrate each meaning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies for common phonics patterns. The sequence is explicit and systematic: students meet the pattern in words, compare examples to see what is regular about it, and then apply it while reading connected text, which is where the pattern has to work automatically.","dex":{"Having students memorize a statement of the rule before they read any words that contain it":"Teaching a rule as a statement to be memorized before students have examined any examples A rule stated in the abstract before students have handled any examples is difficult to hold onto and harder still to apply while reading. Patterns are best built from examples students have already read successfully.","Having students read a leveled text first and infer the pattern from the words they meet in it":"Assuming incidental contact with words in text is enough for students to infer a phonics pattern Asking beginning readers to infer a pattern from continuous text assumes they will notice the regularity on their own while also managing everything else reading requires. Incidental contact is far less reliable than explicit attention to the pattern.","Having students copy the pattern words and draw a picture to illustrate each meaning":"Using copying and illustrating as phonics practice when the attention stays on handwriting and meaning Copying and illustrating keeps students busy with handwriting and meaning while leaving the letter-sound relationship unexamined. The objective is the spelling-to-sound mapping, and this routine never puts attention on it."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-011","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade teacher is demonstrating how to read the word \"cupcake\" by thinking aloud about syllable types. Which explanation best applies that knowledge?","options":["\"The first part ends in a consonant, so that vowel is likely short; the second part has a vowel-consonant-e pattern, so that vowel is likely long. I'll try cup-cake.\"","\"Both parts end in a consonant, so both vowels are short. I'll say cup-cak.\"","\"I'll say the sound for each letter and push them together.\"","\"Both parts have two vowels together, so I'll use the long sound in each one.\""],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how syllable-type knowledge is explained to students. The teacher names the two different syllable types in the word, states the vowel expectation each one carries, and then uses those expectations to generate a pronunciation, which is exactly the reasoning students need to hear modeled before they can do it themselves.","dex":{"\"Both parts end in a consonant, so both vowels are short. I'll say cup-cak.\"":"Applying the closed-syllable expectation to a vowel-consonant-e syllable The second syllable is not closed; it ends in a vowel-consonant-e pattern, so applying the closed-syllable expectation to it produces a nonword. This explanation overgeneralizes one syllable type across a word that contains two different ones.","\"I'll say the sound for each letter and push them together.\"":"Substituting letter-by-letter blending for the syllable-level analysis longer words require Saying each letter sound and blending may work for short single-syllable words, but it gives students nothing to use on longer words. A demonstration of syllable types should show the larger unit of analysis, not retreat to letter-by-letter blending.","\"Both parts have two vowels together, so I'll use the long sound in each one.\"":"Misreading a syllable's internal structure and treating it as a vowel team Neither syllable contains a vowel team. Describing the word this way misreads its structure and sends students to the wrong rule for the wrong reason."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-012","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A third-grade teacher wants students to understand how suffixes change the way a word works in a sentence. Which activity best targets that goal?","options":["Having students read multisyllabic words by dividing them into syllables","Having students look up base words in a dictionary and record the definitions","Having students practice reading words with the suffix -ed, focusing on its three pronunciations","Having students sort words ending in -ness, -ful, and -less according to the part of speech each suffix creates"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of morphological instruction. Sorting by the part of speech a suffix produces makes the grammatical work that suffixes do visible: -ness forms a noun, while -ful and -less form adjectives. Students see that changing the ending relocates the word within a sentence.","dex":{"Having students read multisyllabic words by dividing them into syllables":"Substituting syllabication, which serves pronunciation, for morphological analysis, which serves meaning Dividing into syllables is a decoding procedure aimed at pronunciation. It directs attention to vowel patterns rather than to the meaningful units inside the word, so it does not address how suffixes change function.","Having students look up base words in a dictionary and record the definitions":"Treating dictionary definition work as instruction in word structure Recording definitions treats each word as an isolated item to be looked up. It never asks students to compare base and affix, which is where the insight about how suffixes operate comes from.","Having students practice reading words with the suffix -ed, focusing on its three pronunciations":"Equating work on a suffix's pronunciation with work on its grammatical function The three pronunciations of -ed are a real decoding concern, but the activity targets sound, not function. Practicing pronunciation leaves untouched the question of what the suffix does to the word's role in a sentence."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-013","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A fourth-grade teacher notices that several students read multisyllabic words accurately on a word list but stall on the same words when they meet them in their science text. Which instructional moves would best address this? Select all that apply.","options":["Modeling how to break a multisyllabic word into syllables and then reread the whole word inside the sentence","Providing repeated oral reading practice with content-area sentences that contain the same words","Encouraging students to skip unknown words and rely on pictures and topic knowledge to keep meaning going","Teaching students to check a decoded word against meaning by asking whether it makes sense in the sentence","Replacing the science text with a decodable text at a lower level for all students"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how word-analysis skills transfer from word lists to connected text. The gap between list reading and text reading is an application gap. Modeling the syllable analysis inside an actual sentence, then rereading the whole word, shows students how to transfer a skill they already own into the context where it is needed.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how repeated encounters in meaningful context build recognition of words students can already decode. Repeated oral reading of content-area sentences gives students several encounters with the same words in meaningful context. Each successful retrieval in context strengthens the word's representation and reduces the hesitation the next time.  [D] The question requires an understanding of how self-monitoring supports word analysis while reading connected text. Checking a decoded attempt against sentence meaning closes the loop. Students who can produce an accurate word on a list often fail to monitor themselves in text, and this habit makes them catch a misread word instead of reading past it.","dex":{"Encouraging students to skip unknown words and rely on pictures and topic knowledge to keep meaning going":"Relying on pictures and topic knowledge as a substitute for analyzing the word itself Skipping and guessing from pictures leaves the word unidentified and gives students no way to handle the next one. It manages the surface of the reading while leaving the underlying word-analysis weakness in place, and content-area texts carry few picture supports to rely on.","Replacing the science text with a decodable text at a lower level for all students":"Assuming that lowering text difficulty removes the need to teach word analysis Swapping in easier material lowers the immediate demand but removes the very words students need to learn to handle. Grade-level content vocabulary is part of the task, and the fix is to teach the analysis, not to withdraw the text."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,3]},{"id":"8006-foundational-014","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade student reads \"the\" as \"thuh-ee\" in every sentence. Which instructional response best addresses the difficulty?","options":["Having the student practice reading a list of other words that begin with the /th/ digraph","Teaching \"the\" as an irregular high-frequency word by drawing attention to the part that does not follow the expected pattern","Asking the student to use the sentence meaning and picture to figure out the word","Providing additional phonemic awareness practice in blending two phonemes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how irregular high-frequency words are taught. The student is applying letter-sound knowledge and getting stuck on the vowel, so the response is to name the irregular part explicitly and let the student hold the rest by pattern. This is the heart-word approach: most of the word is regular, and only the tricky part needs to be learned by attention.","dex":{"Having the student practice reading a list of other words that begin with the /th/ digraph":"Attributing a high-frequency word error to general phonics weakness instead of to the specific correspondence that failed The student already produces /th/ correctly. Practicing more /th/ words drills a skill the student has, while the vowel spelling that caused the error goes unaddressed.","Asking the student to use the sentence meaning and picture to figure out the word":"Substituting context guessing for the instant recognition that high-frequency words require Guessing from context lets the student produce a word sometimes, but it does not build recognition of this spelling. The student will meet \"the\" constantly and needs to know it on sight, not infer it anew each time.","Providing additional phonemic awareness practice in blending two phonemes":"Misjudging an orthographic difficulty as a phonemic awareness deficit The student is already blending sounds in print and is doing it too thoroughly. The difficulty is knowing which spelling convention applies, which is an orthographic problem, not a phonemic awareness deficit."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-015","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A third-grade student still labors over high-frequency words such as \"because\" and \"friend\" during oral reading. The teacher has already taught the phonics patterns those words contain. Which next step best applies the principle of repeated exposure?","options":["Lengthening the oral reading passages so the student gets more total practice time","Having the student look up the words in a dictionary and copy the definitions","Embedding the words in short sentences the student rereads across several sessions until recognition is immediate","Having the student sort the words by their vowel spelling patterns"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how repeated exposure builds sight word recognition. Short sentences give the target words many quick, successful encounters spread across sessions, which is what moves a word from effortful decoding to instant recognition. The brevity matters: it lets the student meet the same word several times in one sitting without fatigue.","dex":{"Lengthening the oral reading passages so the student gets more total practice time":"Equating total reading volume with repeated exposure to specific target words Longer passages increase total reading time but scatter the target words thinly. The student meets each one once or twice per session, which is well below the number of encounters needed to make recognition automatic.","Having the student look up the words in a dictionary and copy the definitions":"Treating dictionary work on word meaning as a way to build automatic word recognition The student can almost certainly use these words in speech and knows what they mean. The breakdown is in retrieving the printed form quickly, and dictionary work leaves that untouched.","Having the student sort the words by their vowel spelling patterns":"Treating an analytic sorting task as practice in rapid word retrieval Sorting develops awareness of patterns in English spelling, which is valuable for other purposes, but it is an analytic task performed at leisure. Automatic recognition requires rapid retrieval of the whole word, and sorting does not train that."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-016","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade teacher wants students to understand the convention that /j/ is spelled g before e, i, and y but j before a, o, and u. Which activity best develops that understanding?","options":["Having students read a decodable text containing many /j/ words and then answer comprehension questions","Having students write a story that uses as many /j/ words as they can think of","Having students chorally read a list of words that begin with /j/","Having students sort \"gem,\" \"giant,\" \"gym,\" \"jog,\" and \"jump\" by the letter that spells /j/ and notice which vowel follows it"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how spelling conventions are made explicit. A word sort places contrasting examples side by side and asks students to look for what governs the choice, so they construct the generalization from evidence rather than receiving it as a statement to memorize.","dex":{"Having students read a decodable text containing many /j/ words and then answer comprehension questions":"Assuming students will infer a spelling convention from encounters in text without explicit comparison Reading words in text and then answering questions about meaning keeps attention on the message. Nothing in the task asks students to look at the spellings, so most will finish without noticing the pattern at all.","Having students write a story that uses as many /j/ words as they can think of":"Using free writing as a way to teach a spelling convention Free composition puts the focus on ideas and, in any case, pushes students toward words they can already spell. The teacher loses control of which examples appear, so the targeted contrast may never come up.","Having students chorally read a list of words that begin with /j/":"Treating choral reading practice as instruction in a spelling convention Choral reading builds fluency and confidence with pronunciation. It involves no comparison of spellings and no reflection on why a given letter was chosen."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-017","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"During a one-to-one conference, a first-grade student reads \"stop\" correctly and then says, \"This word has four letters, so it must have four sounds.\" Which teacher response best elicits and develops the student's thinking?","options":["Telling the student that English words do not always match one letter to one sound and moving on","Asking the student to say the sounds one at a time while counting on fingers, then comparing that count with the number of letters","Giving the student a list of words beginning with the /st/ blend to read","Praising the student for reading the word and then reading the next word together"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how a conference can develop a student's thinking. The student has stated a testable hypothesis, so the teacher gives the student a way to test it: produce the sounds, count them, and set that count against the letter count. The student discovers the mismatch independently, which both corrects the rule and shows the student how to check future attempts.","dex":{"Telling the student that English words do not always match one letter to one sound and moving on":"Replacing guided inquiry with direct correction, leaving the student's own hypothesis untested This response is accurate but it terminates the thinking instead of extending it. The student hears a correction to memorize rather than testing a personal hypothesis, so the underlying idea may survive alongside the new statement.","Giving the student a list of words beginning with the /st/ blend to read":"Substituting practice for conversation and leaving the student's stated reasoning unaddressed The student already read /st/ accurately, so blend practice addresses nothing the student got wrong. More importantly, it abandons the student's idea instead of engaging with it.","Praising the student for reading the word and then reading the next word together":"Using praise and reading along to avoid confronting a misconception Praise plus moving ahead leaves the mistaken rule intact and unexamined. The student will go on applying it to \"ship,\" \"that,\" and \"chop\" and misread every one of them."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-018","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade teacher is teaching students how to divide words into syllables while reading. Which sequence best supports students in applying syllabication independently?","options":["Teaching students to locate the vowel in each syllable, try the common division patterns, and then check whether the result sounds like a word they know","Having students clap out the beats in spoken words and then read the same words","Having students memorize the names of the six syllable types","Having students look up unfamiliar words in a dictionary to find the division points"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies for syllabication. A usable strategy has to be a procedure, not just a fact: find the vowel, try a plausible division, and evaluate the attempt against oral vocabulary. The final check gives students a way to self-correct when the first division does not work.","dex":{"Having students clap out the beats in spoken words and then read the same words":"Treating clapped beats in spoken words as equivalent to dividing written words into syllables Clapping beats works on spoken syllables, and it does not transfer cleanly to print because spoken syllabication and written division often disagree on consonant placement. Students need a procedure anchored to letters, not to rhythm.","Having students memorize the names of the six syllable types":"Equating knowledge of syllable-type names with having a procedure for dividing words Naming the syllable types is vocabulary learning about reading, not the ability to divide a word. A student may label a syllable correctly and still have no procedure for deciding where one syllable ends and the next begins.","Having students look up unfamiliar words in a dictionary to find the division points":"Substituting a reference tool for the independent strategy students need to learn A dictionary supplies an answer for one word at a time and is unavailable during most reading. Depending on it leaves students with no independent way to attack an unfamiliar word."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-019","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A teacher is choosing example words for a first lesson on closed syllables. Which words are appropriate examples? Select all that apply.","options":["\"picnic\"","\"paper\"","\"magnet\"","\"winter\"","\"make\""],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how to identify a closed syllable in a two-syllable word. \"Picnic\" divides as pic-nic, and both syllables end in a consonant that closes in a short vowel sound. It is a clean two-syllable example of the pattern repeated twice in one word.  [C] The question requires an understanding of what makes a syllable closed when selecting examples for instruction. \"Magnet\" divides as mag-net. Each syllable ends in a consonant, so the vowel in each is short, giving two consistent instances of the same syllable type in a single word.  [D] The question requires an understanding of how an example of a closed syllable should read once the word is divided. \"Winter\" divides as win-ter. Both syllables are closed by a consonant, so both vowels take the short or reduced sound students are being taught to expect.","dex":{"\"paper\"":"Classifying a word as a closed syllable from its syllable count alone, without checking whether the syllable ends in a consonant \"Paper\" divides as pa-per. The first syllable ends in a vowel and takes the long sound, which makes it an open syllable, the contrast case rather than an example of the target. Using it in the first lesson would blur the distinction students are just beginning to build.","\"make\"":"Treating a one-syllable vowel-consonant-e word as an example of a closed syllable \"Make\" is a single syllable with a vowel-consonant-e pattern, so its vowel is long. It belongs in a lesson on the magic e pattern, and it cannot illustrate what happens when a syllable is closed."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,2,3]},{"id":"8006-foundational-020","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade student reads \"careless\" and says, \"It means full of care.\" Which next step best addresses the misunderstanding?","options":["Having the student reread the sentence and use the picture to confirm the meaning","Having the student divide \"careless\" into syllables and blend each part","Having the student compare \"careless,\" \"careful,\" and \"hopeless\" and discuss what -less and -ful add to a base word","Having the student write a sentence using the word \"care\""],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of morphological instruction. The student has confused two suffixes with opposite meanings, so setting \"careless\" beside \"careful\" makes the contrast visible, and adding \"hopeless\" shows that the same suffix does the same work on a different base. The student constructs the meaning of -less rather than being told it.","dex":{"Having the student reread the sentence and use the picture to confirm the meaning":"Using context to confirm meaning instead of correcting the suffix meaning directly Context might steer the student to an acceptable guess this time, but it does not correct the suffix confusion. The student will attach the same wrong meaning to the next -less word that appears without a helpful picture.","Having the student divide \"careless\" into syllables and blend each part":"Using syllabication to address an error in morphological meaning Syllable division addresses pronunciation, and the student already read the word accurately. The error is in meaning, so a decoding routine leaves it completely untouched.","Having the student write a sentence using the word \"care\"":"Practicing the base word while leaving the suffix that caused the error unexamined Practicing the base word reinforces what the student already understands. The breakdown is entirely in the suffix, and this activity never asks the student to look at it."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-021","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"In a small-group lesson, a third-grade student reads \"farm-er\" with a pause between the parts and then says \"farmer.\" Which next step best extends the student's word-analysis skill?","options":["Asking the student to write \"farmer\" three times from memory","Asking the student to use \"farmer\" in a sentence to show understanding of its meaning","Asking the student to read a list of single-syllable words that begin with the same consonant blend","Asking the student to read other two-syllable words with the same closed-syllable pattern, such as \"winter\" and \"sister\""],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to move a partial success forward. The student has found the syllable boundary and applied a closed-syllable attempt to both parts. Giving several more words with the same structure consolidates the procedure at the same level of difficulty and builds the fluency that lets it become automatic.","dex":{"Asking the student to write \"farmer\" three times from memory":"Treating repeated copying and writing from memory as a way to build decoding skill Writing from memory exercises recall of one spelling. It does not give the student a repeatable procedure for words not yet practiced, so it adds nothing to word analysis.","Asking the student to use \"farmer\" in a sentence to show understanding of its meaning":"Taking work on word meaning as the next step when the difficulty is orthographic analysis The student understood the word well enough to blend it into a real word. Meaning was never the obstacle, so a comprehension-focused follow-up leaves the developing skill unpracticed.","Asking the student to read a list of single-syllable words that begin with the same consonant blend":"Reducing task difficulty instead of consolidating a newly emerging strategy Single-syllable words with the same blend are easier than what the student just did. Moving backward forfeits the chance to consolidate the two-syllable strategy at the moment it is emerging."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-022","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher is planning instruction to help students recognize high-frequency words automatically. Which practices best support that goal? Select all that apply.","options":["Introducing a small number of new words at a time and providing many chances to read them in sentences","Having students copy each new word ten times before they read it in text","Revisiting previously taught words through brief cumulative review routines","Delaying high-frequency word instruction until students have mastered all major phonics patterns","Pointing out the part of an irregular word that does not follow the expected sound-spelling pattern"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of the practice conditions that make recognition of high-frequency words automatic. Automatic recognition comes from many successful retrievals under time pressure. Introducing a few words at a time keeps the load manageable, and reading them in sentences supplies the repeated in-context encounters that build speed.  [C] The question requires an understanding of how distributed review maintains recognition of words already taught. Words that are taught once and then dropped fade back into effortful decoding. Short cumulative reviews spread retrieval practice over time, which is what turns a newly taught word into a word recognized at a glance.  [E] The question requires an understanding of how an irregular high-frequency word is stored efficiently in memory. Naming the irregular part lets students store the word efficiently: they apply what they know to the regular letters and hold only the odd part in memory. This is more effective than treating the whole word as an unanalyzable shape to be memorized.","dex":{"Having students copy each new word ten times before they read it in text":"Equating repeated copying with practice in automatic word recognition Copying directs attention to forming letters rather than to retrieving the word as a whole, and doing it before reading means the first encounter with the word is not a reading encounter. Repeated writing does not produce the rapid recognition required in text.","Delaying high-frequency word instruction until students have mastered all major phonics patterns":"Assuming high-frequency word instruction should wait until phonics patterns are mastered High-frequency words appear throughout the earliest texts students read, so waiting means students cannot read those texts at all. Phonics and high-frequency word instruction run in parallel from the start."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,2,4]},{"id":"8006-foundational-023","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher wants her students to recognize irregular high-frequency words such as \"said,\" \"was,\" and \"of\" automatically. Which instructional approach best reflects how these words are learned?","options":["Requiring students to blend the letters sound by sound each time they meet the word","Providing brief, repeated exposures in which students read the word in a phrase, say the letters as they write it, and meet it again in connected text","Delaying these words until students have mastered every major phonics pattern","Having students copy each word ten times and then take a weekly spelling test"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of instructional techniques that expand recognition of words that are not fully decodable. Irregular words are learned through orthographic mapping: repeated, attention-focused encounters that connect the letters in sequence to the pronunciation. Brief practice that combines reading, saying, and writing, spread across days and reinforced in connected text, supplies the exposures that mapping requires.","dex":{"Requiring students to blend the letters sound by sound each time they meet the word":"Assuming irregular high-frequency words can be sounded out letter by letter, which rehearses a nonword pronunciation Blending is the right strategy for decodable words, but words such as \"said\" contain letters that do not follow the expected pattern. Asking students to sound them out each time teaches them to produce a nonword and never builds automatic recognition.","Delaying these words until students have mastered every major phonics pattern":"Assuming foundational skills must proceed in a strict linear order, with every phonics pattern mastered before high-frequency words appear These words carry a large share of beginning texts, so postponing them leaves students unable to read the very material used to practice phonics. Instruction in phonics patterns and in high-frequency words has to proceed together.","Having students copy each word ten times and then take a weekly spelling test":"Equating massed copying with repeated exposure, overlooking that copying draws attention away from the letter sequence and supplies no feedback Massed copying directs attention to filling rows rather than to the letter sequence that must be mapped to memory, and a weekly test checks recall without building automaticity. Effective exposure is brief, distributed, and focused on the word's spelling-sound connection."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-024","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher puts the words \"cat,\" \"kite,\" \"back,\" and \"school\" on the board and asks students what all four have in common. This activity primarily develops students' understanding that","options":["the same sound can be spelled with different letters or letter combinations","every syllable must contain a vowel","two consonants printed together are pronounced as one blended sound","a single letter can stand for more than one sound"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how letter-sound correspondences and spelling conventions are taught. All four words contain the sound /k/, but it is spelled c, k, ck, and ch. Noticing that one phoneme can be written several ways gives students flexibility when they meet an unfamiliar spelling, which is the point of comparing the words rather than drilling one pattern in isolation.","dex":{"every syllable must contain a vowel":"Reaching for an unrelated true fact about syllables instead of reading the pattern the word set was built to show The claim about syllables is true but unrelated to what these words share. Selecting it means answering from a familiar-sounding fact instead of from the pattern the teacher built the word set to show.","two consonants printed together are pronounced as one blended sound":"Mistaking a digraph, which represents one sound, for a consonant cluster, in which both sounds are heard The final ck in \"back\" and the ch in \"school\" are digraphs representing a single sound, not clusters in which both consonants are heard. Treating them as blends would leave students expecting to produce two sounds.","a single letter can stand for more than one sound":"Confusing the two directions of letter-sound correspondence: one phoneme with several spellings versus one letter with several sounds The letter-to-sound question, such as c saying /k/ or /s/, is a different concept from the sound-to-spelling question this set poses. All four words share one sound, so the target is the spelling side of the correspondence, not the variability of one letter's pronunciation."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-025","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"At the start of second grade, Ms. Boyd asks each student to read aloud a list of pseudowords such as \"vop,\" \"sabe,\" and \"flim.\" What does this measure most directly tell her?","options":["How well students understand the grade-level passages they read independently","How well students apply letter-sound knowledge to read words they have never seen","How many words students can recognize instantly, without sounding them out","How large each student's oral vocabulary is"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of what decoding measures assess. The words are pronounceable but meaningless, so students cannot draw on memory or context and must map graphemes to phonemes. The score therefore isolates the alphabetic principle and blending, which is exactly what a teacher needs before deciding how much phonics review the class requires.","dex":{"How well students understand the grade-level passages they read independently":"Treating a decoding measure as a measure of reading comprehension, which pseudoword reading cannot assess Passage comprehension depends on language comprehension as well as word recognition, and this measure removes meaning entirely. A student can decode pseudowords well and still comprehend poorly, or the reverse.","How many words students can recognize instantly, without sounding them out":"Equating quick word reading with instant sight recognition, even though pseudowords cannot be stored in memory Sight vocabulary is measured with real words that students have previously encountered. Pseudowords cannot be stored in memory, so reading them necessarily draws on decoding rather than on instant recognition.","How large each student's oral vocabulary is":"Conflating oral vocabulary size with word-level decoding ability Oral vocabulary is a language comprehension ability that grows through talk and experience, not through print-to-sound mapping. A student with a rich vocabulary may still score low here if alphabetic decoding is not yet secure."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-026","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Nakamura is planning a second-grade lesson on r-controlled vowels. Which sequence of steps best reflects explicit, systematic phonics instruction?","options":["Naming the pattern and modeling it with a think-aloud, guiding students to blend and sort words with the pattern, then having them read those words in decodable sentences","Having students read a trade book and underline every word containing \"ar,\" \"or,\" or \"er\" so that they can discover the pattern themselves","Teaching all of the r-controlled spellings in one lesson so that students see the complete set at once","Posting the week's r-controlled words on the word wall for students to memorize before they read them in text"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how common phonics patterns are taught. Effective lessons release responsibility gradually: the teacher names the pattern and demonstrates it, students practice blending and sorting with guidance, and then they apply the pattern while reading connected text. Each step prepares the next, so students finish able to use the pattern rather than only recite it.","dex":{"Having students read a trade book and underline every word containing \"ar,\" \"or,\" or \"er\" so that they can discover the pattern themselves":"Assuming that discovering a pattern in authentic text can substitute for explicit modeling and guided practice Unguided discovery asks students to infer a rule they have not been taught, and many will underline words that simply happen to contain those letters. Authentic text is a good place to apply a pattern, but a weak place to introduce one.","Teaching all of the r-controlled spellings in one lesson so that students see the complete set at once":"Assuming that teaching an entire family of spellings at once accelerates learning rather than overloading working memory Presenting ar, or, er, ir, and ur together overloads working memory and invites confusion among spellings that sound alike. Systematic instruction secures one pattern, then contrasts it with the next.","Posting the week's r-controlled words on the word wall for students to memorize before they read them in text":"Substituting whole-word memorization for pattern instruction, which leaves students unable to decode untaught words Memorizing the posted words yields recognition of those words only. The purpose of teaching a vowel pattern is to hand students a tool for the untaught words they will meet later."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-027","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A third-grade student reads the word \"robot\" with a short o in the first syllable, pronouncing it \"rob-ot.\" Which instructional move best addresses this error?","options":["Directing the student to look for a prefix such as re- or pre- at the beginning of the word","Encouraging the student to read the word more quickly so that it comes out right","Teaching the student to try the long vowel sound when a syllable ends with a single vowel","Having the student memorize the pronunciation of \"robot\""],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how syllable types guide vowel pronunciation in longer words. The first syllable of \"robot\" is open, meaning it ends with a vowel, and open syllables usually take the long vowel sound. Teaching students to flex the vowel, trying the long sound when the short one yields no real word, gives them a strategy that transfers to every open syllable they meet.","dex":{"Directing the student to look for a prefix such as re- or pre- at the beginning of the word":"Mistaking the vowel letter of an open syllable for a prefix, confusing syllable boundaries with morpheme boundaries The opening letters form the first syllable of the base word, not a meaning-bearing prefix. Instruction that blurs morpheme boundaries with syllable boundaries leads students to invent prefixes that do not exist.","Encouraging the student to read the word more quickly so that it comes out right":"Treating increased reading rate as a remedy for inaccurate vowel selection Rate is a remedy for slow reading, not for inaccurate reading. Reading faster simply produces the wrong vowel more quickly and trains the student to skip the analysis.","Having the student memorize the pronunciation of \"robot\"":"Resorting to memorization for a fully decodable word and forgoing a transferable syllable-type strategy \"Robot\" follows a regular pattern, so memorizing it treats a teachable word as though it were irregular. The student would face the same problem again with \"tiger\" and \"music.\""}},{"id":"8006-foundational-028","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A fourth-grade teacher writes \"kindness,\" \"careless,\" and \"darkness\" on the board and asks students what the three words have in common. This activity primarily develops students' ability to","options":["identify the final consonant sound in each base word","apply the closed-syllable pattern to multisyllabic words","use context clues to work out the meaning of unfamiliar words","use the suffix -ness to recognize that each word names a quality or state"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how morphemic analysis is taught. The suffix -ness turns an adjective into a noun naming a quality or condition, so recognizing it lets students read and interpret a whole family of words at once. Comparing words that share one suffix makes the pattern visible instead of leaving each word to be learned separately.","dex":{"identify the final consonant sound in each base word":"Staying at the letter-sound level and overlooking the meaning-bearing morpheme the three words share The ending sounds of these three words are not the same, and the final consonant of each base word is not what the set was chosen to show. Attention at the sound level misses the meaningful unit the teacher selected the words for.","apply the closed-syllable pattern to multisyllabic words":"Treating a morphological task as syllabic decoding, which pronounces a word without explaining its meaning or part of speech Syllable patterns help a student pronounce a long word, but they say nothing about what the suffix contributes to meaning or to part of speech. Decoding gets the word off the page; morphology is what makes sense of it.","use context clues to work out the meaning of unfamiliar words":"Substituting context-clue strategies for morphological analysis, a workaround that fails with unfamiliar derived words Context can sometimes hint at a meaning, but it is unreliable and does not travel: the -ness pattern serves students in any text. Morphological analysis is direct instruction in meaning, whereas context is a workaround."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-029","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Before his fifth-grade class reads a science article containing \"photosynthesis,\" \"chlorophyll,\" and \"chloroplast,\" Mr. Osei wants to use those words to build vocabulary through direct instruction. Which move best integrates word analysis with that goal?","options":["Having students read the article independently and infer each term's meaning from context as they go","Teaching what photo-, chlor-, and -phyll mean, then guiding students to split the words into parts and check their meanings against the text","Giving students the definitions to copy before reading so that no instructional time is lost later","Asking each student to look up one word in the dictionary and write a sentence using it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how word analysis carries direct vocabulary instruction. Teaching the recurring Greek elements and then having students take the words apart gives them a reusable key, since photo- and chlor- reappear in \"photograph\" and \"chlorine.\" Checking the parts against the text ties the analysis to meaning instead of leaving it as a puzzle.","dex":{"Having students read the article independently and infer each term's meaning from context as they go":"Assuming vocabulary is acquired incidentally through wide reading and context, without direct instruction Context in a dense science article rarely supplies enough information to fix a precise technical meaning, and students left to infer often settle on a vague or wrong sense. Incidental learning from context is slow and uneven; these words are worth teaching directly.","Giving students the definitions to copy before reading so that no instructional time is lost later":"Equating handing students definitions with direct vocabulary instruction, skipping the active processing of word parts Handing over definitions is efficient but shallow: students receive words they have no way to unpack, so the knowledge does not extend to new words. Direct vocabulary instruction requires active processing of word parts and multiple meaningful encounters.","Asking each student to look up one word in the dictionary and write a sentence using it":"Treating dictionary lookup as vocabulary teaching, since copying isolated definitions builds no analytical skill Dictionary definitions for technical terms are often harder than the words themselves, and copying one produces no analysis. The value of this word set lies in its shared parts, which a dictionary lookup leaves invisible."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-030","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher reviews a high-frequency word list and notices that many entries, such as \"can,\" \"did,\" and \"not,\" follow predictable phonics patterns. Which decision best reflects that observation?","options":["Teach the decodable words through their letter-sound patterns and give extra attention to the irregular ones, such as \"the\" and \"said\"","Teach every word on the list as a whole shape to be memorized","Drop the decodable words from the list because students will pick them up through reading","Postpone all high-frequency word instruction until students know every consonant and short-vowel sound"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how high-frequency word instruction is differentiated. Words that follow the patterns being taught are best learned through those patterns, which reinforces the phonics lesson and shows students that decoding pays off. Words that break the patterns need the additional step of attention to the irregular letters, so sorting the list by decodability sends effort where it is actually needed.","dex":{"Teach every word on the list as a whole shape to be memorized":"Assuming every high-frequency word must be memorized as a whole shape, wasting the chance to reinforce decodable patterns Treating every word as a shape to memorize ignores the letter-sound knowledge the student already has. Many frequent words are fully decodable and are better used as practice with the patterns being taught.","Drop the decodable words from the list because students will pick them up through reading":"Assuming frequent words will be absorbed through reading alone and therefore need no explicit instruction Removing words from instruction leaves their learning to chance, and the students who struggle most with print are the least likely to absorb them incidentally. Frequent exposure in text supports instruction; it does not replace it.","Postpone all high-frequency word instruction until students know every consonant and short-vowel sound":"Assuming foundational skills must follow a strict linear sequence instead of developing in parallel High-frequency words appear throughout the very texts used to practice letter-sound knowledge, so waiting means students cannot read the practice material. Decoding instruction and high-frequency word instruction reinforce each other from the beginning."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-031","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade student consistently writes \"sed\" for \"said\" and \"wuz\" for \"was.\" Which instructional technique best addresses this pattern of errors?","options":["Accepting the spellings as a normal stage and waiting for the student to outgrow them","Providing additional phonics practice with short-vowel patterns","Pointing out the part of the word that does not sound the way it looks while the student says and writes it, then reviewing it briefly over several days","Having the student read longer decodable texts to build fluency"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how irregular high-frequency words are taught. The student is applying sound-out spelling to letters that do not follow the rule, so the fix is to make the irregular part explicit: name the part that does not match, have the student say and write the whole word while attending to that sequence, and return to it on several days so the spelling is mapped to memory.","dex":{"Accepting the spellings as a normal stage and waiting for the student to outgrow them":"Extending developmental spelling theory to the point of never correcting the conventional spelling of common words Approximations are useful evidence in the earliest stage of writing, but by second grade these very common words should be conventional. Waiting leaves a high-frequency word wrong in everything the student writes.","Providing additional phonics practice with short-vowel patterns":"Reading misspelled irregular words as a phonics deficit when the student is in fact overapplying sound-out spelling The student already knows the short-vowel patterns well enough to produce these spellings, so more phonics practice would strengthen the very habit that is causing the error. The gap is knowledge of particular irregular words, not of sound-symbol patterns.","Having the student read longer decodable texts to build fluency":"Assuming additional decoding practice will incidentally correct the spelling of irregular high-frequency words Decodable texts contain regular words by design, so more time in them provides no practice with the words being misspelled. Fluency with patterns and memory for irregular words develop through different kinds of practice."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-032","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade student spells \"back\" as \"bak\" and \"duck\" as \"duk.\" Which spelling convention should the teacher address?","options":["The letter k is used only before e, i, and y","Every final consonant sound is spelled with two letters","Doubled letters always signal that the vowel before them is short","The /k/ sound at the end of a one-syllable word with a short vowel is usually spelled ck rather than k"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the spelling conventions that govern common letter-sound correspondences. The sound /k/ has several spellings, and English uses ck rather than k immediately after a short vowel in a one-syllable word. Teaching the convention directly gives the student a rule that covers dozens of words instead of a separate correction for each.","dex":{"The letter k is used only before e, i, and y":"Merging two distinct conventions into one: the positional c-or-k rule and the final ck rule The statement about e, i, and y governs whether to write c or k, not whether k should be doubled. Applying it here would leave the student still writing \"bak.\"","Every final consonant sound is spelled with two letters":"Overgeneralizing a narrow convention into a rule that every final consonant sound is spelled with two letters Most final consonant sounds are spelled with a single letter, as in \"bat\" and \"run.\" An overgeneralized rule like this one would create new errors in words the student currently spells correctly.","Doubled letters always signal that the vowel before them is short":"Extending the floss rule for doubling final f, l, and s to all consonants Doubling f, l, and s at the end of a short-vowel word is a separate convention from the ck rule. Treating doubled letters as a universal signal of a short vowel sends the student looking for double letters English does not use."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-033","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher wants a complete picture of each student's foundational literacy development. Which assessments would provide that information? Select all that apply.","options":["A phonological awareness screener that asks students to blend and segment phonemes","A pseudoword reading measure that asks students to read pronounceable nonsense words","Weekly curriculum-based measures of oral reading fluency on connected text","A listening comprehension assessment in which students answer questions about a passage read aloud","A single end-of-unit spelling test"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of which tools assess foundational reading development. Phonemic blending and segmentation sit beneath decoding, and a screener shows whether a student is ready to benefit from phonics instruction. Without it, weak phonemic awareness is easily mistaken for carelessness.  [B] A pseudoword measure isolates the alphabetic principle: the words cannot be memorized or guessed from context, so the score reflects grapheme-phoneme mapping and blending. It is the clearest available index of whether phonics instruction is taking hold.  [C] The question requires an understanding of which tools assess foundational reading development. Repeated measures on connected text show how decoding and recognition are growing over time and whether students transfer word-level skills into reading. Their weekly rhythm makes them useful for adjusting instruction, not only for reporting.","dex":{"A listening comprehension assessment in which students answer questions about a passage read aloud":"Treating a measure of language comprehension as an assessment of foundational word-level skills Listening comprehension measures language comprehension, a strand separate from word recognition. The information is valuable, but it says nothing about a student's phonemic awareness, phonics knowledge, or decoding.","A single end-of-unit spelling test":"Treating a single summative quiz as a developmental assessment, when one snapshot shows no growth over time One unit test gives a snapshot at a single moment, mixes memorized words with untaught ones, and yields no trend data. Profiling development requires measures that are repeated and that isolate specific skills."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-foundational-034","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher models blending \"stop.\" Several students read it back as \"suh-tuh-op.\" Which instructional point best addresses the error?","options":["Telling students to say the sounds in a continuous stream without adding a vowel after each consonant","Explaining that s and t form a digraph that stands for a single sound","Having students memorize \"stop\" as a sight word","Switching to words with final clusters because those are easier to blend"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how consonant clusters are taught. The students are inserting a schwa after each consonant, which happens when sounds are produced in isolation and then placed side by side. Directing them to hold and slide the sounds together, keeping the mouth ready for the next one, produces the cluster without added vowels and transfers to every word with an initial cluster.","dex":{"Explaining that s and t form a digraph that stands for a single sound":"Mistaking a consonant cluster, in which both sounds are heard, for a digraph representing a single sound A digraph such as sh or ch represents one phoneme, but in \"stop\" both /s/ and /t/ are heard. Calling the pair a digraph would teach students to drop a sound the word actually contains.","Having students memorize \"stop\" as a sight word":"Bypassing cluster blending by memorizing individual words, which teaches one word rather than a whole class of words Memorizing one word avoids the skill these students lack. They would then need to memorize \"flag,\" \"brick,\" and every other cluster word separately, and their blending would stay incomplete.","Switching to words with final clusters because those are easier to blend":"Changing which words are taught instead of correcting the faulty blending procedure Changing the word list does not address the inserted vowels, which will appear in final clusters too. The difficulty lies in the blending procedure, not in where the cluster sits in the word."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-035","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Whitfield wants an activity that develops students' thinking about syllable types rather than their ability to label them. Which activity best achieves that goal?","options":["Having students color-code open and closed syllables on a worksheet","Having students read pairs such as \"fin\" and \"fine\" and explain why the vowel sound changes","Having students copy the six syllable types onto a classroom chart","Having students sort words by the number of syllables they contain"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how activities are evaluated by the thinking they demand. Comparing \"fin\" and \"fine\" and explaining the change obliges students to reason about the mechanism: the silent e reaches back and makes the vowel long. The activity turns the rule into something students can state and test, which is exactly the reasoning that labeling practice does not require.","dex":{"Having students color-code open and closed syllables on a worksheet":"Equating identification and labeling with understanding a mechanism, since color-coding requires no explanation Color-coding asks students to classify, and a student can classify correctly by matching a familiar pattern without being able to say why the vowel is long. The activity checks recognition of a category, not reasoning about how the category works.","Having students copy the six syllable types onto a classroom chart":"Equating the production of a classroom display with learning, as transcription demands no cognitive processing Copying definitions onto a chart is transcription. It produces a reference display but requires no engagement with how syllable types affect pronunciation.","Having students sort words by the number of syllables they contain":"Confusing counting syllables with classifying syllable types, a matter of number rather than of kind Counting syllables is a separate skill that says nothing about whether a syllable is open, closed, or vowel-consonant-e. Students could sort every word correctly and still be unable to use syllable type to decide a vowel sound."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-036","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"When a third-grade student meets the unfamiliar word \"basket,\" he says \"baseball\" and keeps reading. Which strategy instruction best addresses this behavior?","options":["Telling the student to use the illustrations to work out what the word must be","Having the student sound out only the first letter and then try a word that fits the sentence","Teaching the student to look all the way through the word, check the beginning, middle, and ending, and reread to see whether his reading makes sense","Encouraging the student to skip unfamiliar words and read on for the main idea"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how word-solving strategies are taught in the intermediate grades. The student is substituting a word that shares only the first letters, so instruction should make the whole letter sequence the object of attention and add a check: does what I said look right and make sense? Rereading turns the error into something the student can catch himself.","dex":{"Telling the student to use the illustrations to work out what the word must be":"Relying on illustrations and other non-print cues to guess words instead of analyzing letter-sound correspondences Pictures can suggest a topic but rarely identify a specific word, and they do nothing in the many texts without illustrations. Reliance on them leaves students with no way to solve words in text alone.","Having the student sound out only the first letter and then try a word that fits the sentence":"Accepting partial decoding that sounds out only the first letter and then guesses, leaving the student no way to self-check Starting with one letter and guessing produces substitutions exactly like this student's, because many words begin the same way. Partial decoding weakens accurate word solving rather than supporting it.","Encouraging the student to skip unfamiliar words and read on for the main idea":"Accepting the skipping of unknown words as a legitimate strategy, which abandons word meaning and lets errors accumulate Skipping leaves the sentence incomplete and trains the student to read past the very words that carry new information. Comprehension depends on the meanings that were skipped."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-037","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which instructional practices support students' development of automatic recognition of high-frequency words? Select all that apply.","options":["Guiding students to notice the regular letter-sound parts of the words that are decodable","Providing brief, frequent opportunities to read and write the words in phrases and sentences","Requiring students to sound out every high-frequency word letter by letter each time it appears","Introducing large sets of new words at once so that students see many of them early","Using cumulative review so that previously taught words keep appearing in later lessons"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how high-frequency word instruction connects to phonics. Many frequent words are regular, and pointing out the parts that follow the patterns gives students a way to hold on to the spelling while reinforcing the phonics lesson itself.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how practice schedules build automatic recognition. Short, frequent practice reading and writing the words in phrases and sentences supplies the repeated exposures that automatic recognition requires. Brief and frequent works better than one long session, and embedding the word in a phrase supports fluent reading as well as recognition.  [E] The question requires an understanding of how review schedules affect retention of high-frequency words. Cumulative review keeps earlier words in circulation so they do not fade once their week of instruction ends. Automatic recognition is built through distributed encounters over time, not through a single concentrated exposure.","dex":{"Requiring students to sound out every high-frequency word letter by letter each time it appears":"Assuming every high-frequency word should be sounded out letter by letter each time, which blocks automaticity and fails on irregular words Sounding a word out each time prevents the automaticity that is the entire point of high-frequency word instruction, and it fails outright with the irregular words that cannot be sounded out.","Introducing large sets of new words at once so that students see many of them early":"Assuming a massed introduction of many new words speeds progress rather than overloading memory Introducing many words together gives students more than they can hold at once and leaves little time for the repeated encounters that build memory. A steady pace with review produces stronger retention than a massed introduction."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,4]},{"id":"8006-foundational-038","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Alvarez notices that one of her kindergarten students blends unseen words accurately but rarely remembers words such as \"was\" and \"they.\" Which technique best supports this student?","options":["Providing additional phonemic awareness practice in blending and segmenting","Having the student reread the same decodable book until the reading is fluent","Having the student copy the words during independent work time","Naming the part of the word that does not sound the way it looks while the student traces and says the word, followed by several short reviews in the same week"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how multisensory techniques and repeated exposure build recognition of irregular words. The student can already decode, so the missing piece is orthographic mapping of words that break the rules. Naming the unexpected part, tracing and saying the word so several senses carry the same information, and returning to it briefly several times in one week creates the conditions for the spelling to stick.","dex":{"Providing additional phonemic awareness practice in blending and segmenting":"Attributing failure to retain high-frequency words to weak phonemic awareness when the real gap is orthographic mapping The student's blending is already accurate, so more phonemic awareness practice targets a strength rather than the difficulty. Remembering irregular words depends on mapping letters to memory, which is a different demand from hearing and manipulating sounds.","Having the student reread the same decodable book until the reading is fluent":"Assuming repeated reading of decodable texts will incidentally teach the irregular high-frequency words those texts exclude by design Decodable books exclude exactly the irregular words this student needs, so rereading them offers no practice with \"was\" or \"they.\" Repeated reading builds fluency with patterns; it does not teach irregular spellings.","Having the student copy the words during independent work time":"Treating unsupervised copying as practice, with no attention to letter sequence and no feedback to stop errors from being rehearsed Copying without attention to the letter sequence produces handwriting practice, not learning, and errors are rehearsed rather than corrected. Effective practice is brief, guided, and aimed at the part of the word that must be remembered."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-039","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Ferraro notices that one of her second-grade students writes \"plai\" for \"play\" and \"trai\" for \"tray\" during writing workshop. Which generalization would best settle the choice for him?","options":["The long a spelled ay is used at the end of a word or syllable, while ai is used elsewhere","Every long vowel sound is spelled with two vowels working together","The letters a and i together always stand for the long a sound","Vowel teams must be memorized one at a time because English spelling follows no patterns"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the spelling conventions that govern common letter-sound correspondences. Both ai and ay represent long a, but English positionally prefers ay at the end of a word or syllable and ai elsewhere. Teaching the position rule lets the student choose between two spellings of the same sound instead of guessing.","dex":{"Every long vowel sound is spelled with two vowels working together":"Overgeneralizing vowel teams as the only way to spell long vowels, ignoring silent e and open syllables Long vowels are also spelled with a silent e, as in \"cake,\" or with a single vowel in an open syllable, as in \"me.\" The overgeneralization leaves students puzzled by a large group of very common words.","The letters a and i together always stand for the long a sound":"Treating one vowel team as the fixed spelling of a sound, ignoring competing spellings and positional constraints Treating ai as the automatic choice is the error the student is already making. The point of instruction is to show that several spellings compete for the same sound and that position decides among them.","Vowel teams must be memorized one at a time because English spelling follows no patterns":"Assuming English spelling is patternless and must be memorized word by word English spelling is regular enough to teach: most correspondences follow patterns, and teaching those patterns is far more efficient than memorizing words one at a time. Surrendering to memorization removes the most useful tool students can have."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-040","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Two English learners in a second-grade class score below benchmark on a pseudoword decoding measure but perform at grade level on a listening comprehension task. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?","options":["The students have a language-based comprehension problem that calls for intensive vocabulary instruction","The students' difficulty is at the level of word recognition, so decoding instruction is warranted and listening comprehension is a strength to build on","The screening is not valid for English learners and the results should be set aside","The students should be referred for special education evaluation before any instruction is changed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how assessment data are interpreted for diverse learners. Pseudoword decoding draws little on English oral vocabulary, so a low score points to word recognition, while grade-level listening comprehension shows that language comprehension is developing normally. Together the two results locate the difficulty precisely and indicate that decoding instruction, not vocabulary intervention, is the next step.","dex":{"The students have a language-based comprehension problem that calls for intensive vocabulary instruction":"Reading the assessment results backwards, since grade-level listening comprehension rules out a language-comprehension problem The listening comprehension data contradict this reading: the students understand language at grade level. Intensive vocabulary instruction would target the one area in which they are performing well.","The screening is not valid for English learners and the results should be set aside":"Assuming standardized screeners are invalid for English learners and discarding data that are in fact interpretable Because pseudowords carry no meaning, the measure does not penalize a smaller English vocabulary the way a passage reading task can. Setting aside valid information would leave a real decoding difficulty unaddressed.","The students should be referred for special education evaluation before any instruction is changed":"Assuming a low screening score requires a special education referral before instruction is adjusted and the response monitored Screening data are meant to inform instruction first. A structured response of targeted decoding instruction with progress monitoring is the appropriate next step, and referral enters the picture only if students fail to respond."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-041","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher wants to build students' awareness of sound patterns at the word level before moving on to work with individual phonemes. Which oral activity best targets this goal?","options":["Asking students to replace the first sound in \"map\" with /t/ and say the new word","Asking students to identify the first sound they hear in the word \"sun\"","Asking students to decide whether two spoken words, such as \"cat\" and \"hat,\" end with the same sound","Asking students to blend the sounds /c/ /a/ /t/ to say a single word"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of systematic and explicit methods for teaching phonological awareness. Rhyme recognition asks students to compare the ending sounds of whole words, which develops awareness of sound patterns at the word level (onset-rime) rather than at the level of individual phonemes. Because it does not require isolating or manipulating single sounds, it is an appropriate foundation to establish before phonemic awareness work begins.","dex":{"Asking students to replace the first sound in \"map\" with /t/ and say the new word":"Confusing phoneme substitution, the most demanding kind of sound manipulation, with an entry-level awareness task, which flattens the hierarchy of skills. Phoneme substitution is one of the most advanced phonemic awareness tasks, requiring students to hold a word in memory, delete a sound, and insert another. It is far beyond a starting point for students who have not yet developed word-level awareness.","Asking students to identify the first sound they hear in the word \"sun\"":"Equating first-sound identification at the phoneme level with word-level work. Hearing one sound is not the same as comparing the sound patterns of whole words. Identifying the first sound in a word is phoneme isolation, which operates at the individual sound level rather than the word level. It belongs to the later phase of instruction the teacher is preparing for, not the foundational phase described.","Asking students to blend the sounds /c/ /a/ /t/ to say a single word":"Confusing phoneme blending with word-level phonological awareness, and so skipping the developmental sequence from whole words down to individual sounds. Blending individual phonemes to form a word is phonemic awareness, the level the teacher plans to move to later. It requires students to hold and combine separate sounds, which is more demanding than comparing whole-word endings and skips the foundational step the teacher is targeting."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-042","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A teacher gives each student three counters and says, \"Push one counter forward for every sound you hear in 'cat.'\" Which skill is this activity designed to develop?","options":["Phonics, because students are attending to the sounds that letters represent","Reading fluency, because students are practicing accurate and rapid word recognition","Reading comprehension, because students must know what the word means to complete the task","Phonemic awareness, because students are segmenting a spoken word into its individual phonemes"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how phonemic awareness is taught. Moving a counter for each sound heard is phoneme segmentation, a core phonemic awareness skill: students manipulate individual sounds in spoken words without any print involved.","dex":{"Phonics, because students are attending to the sounds that letters represent":"Treating any activity that involves speech sounds as phonics. Phonics is about print-to-sound mapping; phonemic awareness is purely auditory and can be done with the eyes closed. Phonics instruction teaches the correspondence between letters (graphemes) and sounds (phonemes), so it requires printed text. In this activity no letters appear at all, and students work entirely by listening.","Reading fluency, because students are practicing accurate and rapid word recognition":"Using fluency as a catch-all label for any difficulty observed during reading. Fluency operates at the level of connected text, not single spoken words. Fluency means reading connected text accurately, at an appropriate rate, and with prosody. This task uses no text and asks nothing about rate or expression, so it cannot build fluency.","Reading comprehension, because students must know what the word means to complete the task":"Classifying every reading-related task as comprehension. Phonemic awareness sits below word recognition and is a prerequisite for it, not a product of it. Comprehension is the construction of meaning from text. Segmenting the sounds in 'cat' happens at the sound level; a student can complete it successfully without knowing what a cat is."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-043","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher says, \"Listen: /k/ /a/ /t/ is 'cat.' Now let's change the first sound to /b/. What word do we have?\" Which phonemic awareness skill is the teacher targeting?","options":["Phoneme isolation","Phoneme substitution","Phoneme deletion","Syllable blending"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the phonemic awareness skill progression. Replacing the initial phoneme of a word to produce a new word is phoneme substitution, a more advanced manipulation skill typically taught after blending and segmenting are secure.","dex":{"Phoneme isolation":"Equating any task that names a first sound with isolation. Isolation identifies; substitution manipulates. Phoneme isolation asks students to identify the sound in a given position, as in \"What is the first sound in 'cat'?\" Here the teacher supplies the sound and asks students to swap it, which is one level harder.","Phoneme deletion":"Confusing removing a phoneme with replacing one. Both change the word, but only deletion leaves the position empty. Deletion would ask \"Say 'cat.' Now say it without /k/.\" That removes a sound entirely and leaves 'at.' This task replaces /k/ with /b/ to make 'bat,' so a sound remains in that position.","Syllable blending":"Confusing the syllable level with the phoneme level of the sound structure hierarchy. Syllable blending combines larger units of sound, as in blending 'pic' and 'nic' into 'picnic.' The word 'cat' is a single syllable, and the teacher is working at the phoneme level, below the syllable."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-044","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"During an informal assessment, a teacher asks a first grader to blend the sounds /d/ /o/ /g/. The student responds \"d... o... g... doh.\" Which statement best describes the student's difficulty?","options":["The student cannot blend individual phonemes into a word, an undeveloped phonemic awareness skill","The student has acquired the alphabetic principle but lacks sight word recognition","The student has a speech sound production disorder that interferes with decoding","The student reads with insufficient fluency to support comprehension"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to interpret informal assessment data. The student produces the phonemes in sequence but cannot fuse them into a word, which indicates a gap in blending, a foundational phonemic awareness skill that must precede successful decoding.","dex":{"The student has acquired the alphabetic principle but lacks sight word recognition":"Assuming that because a student can produce letter sounds, the alphabetic principle is in place. Producing sounds and blending them into a word are distinct achievements. Blending is precisely what the alphabetic principle requires. If the student could map letters to sounds and then fuse those sounds, the word would be read. Sight word recognition is a separate issue and is not what this response shows.","The student has a speech sound production disorder that interferes with decoding":"Attributing a cognitive-linguistic gap to a speech or motor difficulty. Clear articulation in the response is the evidence that rules this out. The student articulated /d/, /o/, and /g/ clearly, which rules out a production problem. The breakdown is not in saying the sounds but in mentally combining them.","The student reads with insufficient fluency to support comprehension":"Applying a broad label such as fluency to any observed reading difficulty, which obscures the specific skill that needs teaching. Fluency concerns reading connected text with accuracy, rate, and prosody. This student has not yet blended a single spoken word, so the difficulty lies at a more basic level than fluency."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-045","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"During a shared reading of a big book, Ms. Alvarez runs her finger beneath the words as she reads and pauses to say, \"We finished this line, so we go back to the start of the next one.\" Which concept of print is she teaching?","options":["One-to-one match, the correspondence between each spoken word and a written word","Book orientation, the difference between the front cover and the back cover","Return sweep, the movement from the end of one line of print to the beginning of the next","Letter-sound correspondence, the match between a letter and the sound it represents"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how concepts of print are taught systematically and explicitly. Naming and modeling the return sweep draws attention to directionality: English print is read left to right and top to bottom, and reading resumes at the beginning of the next line. Beginning readers often invent their own route back to the left margin or skip the move entirely, so the behavior has to be shown rather than assumed.","dex":{"One-to-one match, the correspondence between each spoken word and a written word":"Reaches for one-to-one match, another genuine concept of print, without tying the choice to the teacher behavior the stem actually names. One-to-one match is also a concept of print, but it concerns pointing to each word as it is spoken. The teacher's comment is specifically about where reading resumes at the end of a line, which is a different concept.","Book orientation, the difference between the front cover and the back cover":"Defaults to book-orientation knowledge and skips the specific move described in the stem, resuming at the start of the next line. Book orientation is taught by identifying the front cover, the title page, and where reading begins. None of those elements appears in the scenario, and none is what the teacher is pointing to.","Letter-sound correspondence, the match between a letter and the sound it represents":"Files letter-sound correspondence under concepts of print, a category that covers how text is arranged and works rather than which sounds letters stand for. Letter-sound correspondence belongs to the alphabetic principle, not to concepts of print, and it is not what the teacher's finger movement demonstrates. Nothing in the scenario connects a printed symbol to a sound."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-046","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Byrne holds up a card with the letter m and says, \"This letter stands for the sound /m/. Listen for it at the beginning of mop and mom — the same letter stands for the same sound in both.\" This lesson introduces","options":["Phonemic awareness, the understanding that spoken words can be broken into individual sounds","Concepts of print, the understanding that text carries meaning and is read left to right","Sight recognition, the understanding that some words must be learned as whole units","The alphabetic principle, the understanding that written letters systematically represent the sounds in spoken words"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the alphabetic principle in early literacy instruction. The teacher is not merely asking students to hear /m/; she is linking a sound they hear to a printed symbol and showing that the same symbol recurs across different words. That mapping insight is what later lets students decode unfamiliar words instead of memorizing them one at a time.","dex":{"Phonemic awareness, the understanding that spoken words can be broken into individual sounds":"Stops at hearing the sound and misses the point that the alphabetic principle begins only when a printed symbol is mapped onto that sound. If the lesson stopped at hearing /m/ in two words, it would be phonemic awareness, an entirely oral skill. The letter card and the statement that the letter stands for the sound move the lesson into the alphabetic principle.","Concepts of print, the understanding that text carries meaning and is read left to right":"Reads the presence of a printed letter as concepts of print instruction, a category that says nothing about what a letter represents. Concepts of print concern how written language is organized and handled, such as directionality, spacing, and the parts of a book. They say nothing about what a letter represents.","Sight recognition, the understanding that some words must be learned as whole units":"Treats quick whole-word recognition as where early reading starts, when it is really what secure alphabetic mapping eventually produces and leaves students with no strategy for unfamiliar words. Sight recognition is a by-product of secure alphabetic mapping, not its starting point. Teaching children to memorize whole words first leaves them without a strategy for words they have never seen."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-047","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher replaces an alphabetized word wall with a sound wall that groups spellings by phoneme and includes mouth pictures. Which statement best describes the primary instructional purpose of this change?","options":["To give students a reference for connecting the sounds they hear to the spellings that represent those sounds","To give students a reference for spelling high-frequency words correctly during independent writing","To surround students with print so that repeated exposure produces letter and word learning","To help students learn the alphabet in order by consulting the display each day"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how classroom materials support specific instructional goals. A sound wall is organized by phoneme rather than by initial letter, so a student who hears a sound can locate it and find the graphemes that spell it. That organization supports both segmenting words for spelling and blending sounds while reading.","dex":{"To give students a reference for spelling high-frequency words correctly during independent writing":"Conflates a sound wall with a word wall: one is organized by phoneme to support sound-spelling mapping, the other alphabetically to support looking up whole words. That is the purpose of an alphabetized word wall of high-frequency or irregular words. Students consult such a wall for whole words, not for the relationship between a sound and its spellings, which is the work a sound wall supports.","To surround students with print so that repeated exposure produces letter and word learning":"Assumes print on the walls does the teaching on its own, forgetting that a display helps only once students are shown how to use it. A print-rich room is worth having, but exposure alone does not teach sound-spelling relationships. A display supports learning only when students are taught how to use it and are referred to it during instruction.","To help students learn the alphabet in order by consulting the display each day":"Equates knowing the alphabet in order with readiness to read, though reciting letters and knowing their sounds are separate bodies of knowledge. Reciting the alphabet in order is a different skill from knowing the sounds letters represent. A sound wall is deliberately organized by phoneme, not alphabetically, precisely so that it does not reinforce alphabetical order."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-048","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Lin says to her kindergarten class, \"Say cupcake. Now say cupcake without saying cake.\" This task primarily develops students'","options":["Vocabulary, by having students analyze the meaningful parts of a compound word","Phonological awareness at the syllable level, by having students delete a syllable from a spoken word","Phonemic awareness, by having students delete an individual sound from a spoken word","Decoding, by having students take apart a printed word they have seen before"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how phonological awareness develops from larger to smaller units of sound. The unit being removed is a syllable, a large and easily heard chunk of speech, and the entire task is done by ear with no print involved. Oral deletion at this level is a strong predictor of later reading achievement and is typically taught before phoneme-level work.","dex":{"Vocabulary, by having students analyze the meaningful parts of a compound word":"Follows the meaning of the compound instead of its sound structure; the task asks students to say something, not to explain it. The word is a compound and its parts do carry meaning, but the task directs attention to the sound structure of the word, not to what its parts mean. Students are asked to say something, not to explain it.","Phonemic awareness, by having students delete an individual sound from a spoken word":"Takes any successful word-splitting as phoneme-level work, though a syllable is a much larger unit and far easier to detach than a single sound. The unit removed here is a syllable, not a single phoneme. A student who can drop cake from cupcake may still be unable to say cat without the /k/, which is the harder phoneme-level operation.","Decoding, by having students take apart a printed word they have seen before":"Hears taking a word apart as decoding, forgetting that decoding operates on printed words while this task never leaves the ear. Decoding works on printed text. This task is entirely oral, so it can neither build nor measure decoding ability, regardless of whether students have seen the word in a book."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-049","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which read-aloud practice best promotes first-grade students' oral language development?","options":["Playing an audio recording of the story while students follow along in their own copies","Having students copy definitions of story words into a vocabulary notebook after the reading","Pausing to discuss a few target words, asking open-ended questions, and having students turn and talk with a partner","Reading the story aloud expressively from beginning to end while students listen quietly"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how oral language is developed through instruction. An interactive read-aloud gives students a reason to produce language, supplies the words and sentence patterns they need, and then returns their attention to the text. Listening alone is receptive; the questions and the partner turn push students into expressive use of new vocabulary and syntax.","dex":{"Playing an audio recording of the story while students follow along in their own copies":"Mistakes following along with a recording for interaction, though no one responds to the student and nothing requires an answer. Following along in a text while a recording plays adds print tracking, but the experience stays passive. There is no interaction, no feedback, and no demand for the student to produce language.","Having students copy definitions of story words into a vocabulary notebook after the reading":"Substitutes copying definitions for oral vocabulary growth, when words take hold only through repeated use in meaningful listening and speaking. Copying definitions is a writing task that treats words as list items. It gives no practice hearing the words in connected speech or using them in a sentence of the student's own.","Reading the story aloud expressively from beginning to end while students listen quietly":"Treats hearing plenty of language as the same thing as developing it, overlooking that receptive input must be paired with chances to produce language. Expressive reading models prosody and can support comprehension, but listening is receptive only. Students who never speak are not practicing the language they are hearing, so oral production does not grow."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-050","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher is planning instruction in phoneme deletion. Which sequence reflects how the skill is best developed?","options":["Deleting the medial vowel first, because the vowel carries the most information about the identity of the word","Substituting one phoneme for another first, because substitution combines deletion and addition and is therefore more efficient","Presenting the words in writing so that students can see which letters to remove before they say the new word","Deleting the initial phoneme of a one-syllable word first, then the final phoneme, then one phoneme within a consonant cluster"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of evidence-based sequences for phonemic awareness instruction. Deletion gets harder as the phoneme's position becomes harder to hold in memory: the first sound of a word is easiest to isolate, the final sound comes next, and removing one sound from a cluster such as the /s/ in stop means reconfiguring the whole onset. Teaching in that order keeps the task within reach while raising the demand.","dex":{"Deleting the medial vowel first, because the vowel carries the most information about the identity of the word":"Orders instruction by how much information a phoneme carries rather than by how hard it is to manipulate; medial vowels yield last, not first. Medial vowels are among the last phonemes children can manipulate, not the first, because the vowel sits between two consonants that must be held in memory while it is removed.","Substituting one phoneme for another first, because substitution combines deletion and addition and is therefore more efficient":"Takes the more complex operation as the more efficient one and skips the simpler deletion work that substitution is built on. Substitution is a later, harder operation because it requires holding the target word in memory while deleting and adding at the same time. Introducing it first skips the prerequisite step and the practice that builds it.","Presenting the words in writing so that students can see which letters to remove before they say the new word":"Believes phoneme work needs visible letters to lean on, though adding print turns an oral task into spelling or decoding. Removing printed letters turns the task into a spelling or decoding activity. Phonemic awareness work is oral by definition; adding print changes both what is being taught and what the response reveals."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-051","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Screening shows that most of Ms. Adeyemi's kindergarten class can produce rhymes and blend onsets and rimes, but only three students can segment the three phonemes in a word such as ship. Which response best reflects systematic and explicit instruction?","options":["Keeping the class moving through onset-rime work while adding a daily small-group routine that models phoneme segmentation, guides practice with counters, and reviews sounds already taught","Returning the whole class to rhyming and syllable work until every student has reached mastery of those levels","Waiting to address phonemes until students begin reading, since phonemic awareness develops as a result of reading print","Providing phoneme instruction only in one-on-one tutoring sessions and leaving it out of whole-class time"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how systematic and explicit instruction responds to assessment data. Systematic instruction follows a planned sequence, so the class continues at the onset-rime level while a small group gets the additional, more demanding work those three students need. Explicit instruction supplies the modeling, guided practice, and cumulative review that make the harder level learnable rather than assumed.","dex":{"Returning the whole class to rhyming and syllable work until every student has reached mastery of those levels":"Reads holding the whole class until everyone masters a level as the careful choice, when systematic teaching advances on sequence and answers individual gaps in small groups. Holding the entire class at a level most students have already mastered postpones instruction the class is ready for, and it still does not give the three students what they are missing at the phoneme level.","Waiting to address phonemes until students begin reading, since phonemic awareness develops as a result of reading print":"Inverts the causal direction, assuming that reading print will hand students phonemic awareness rather than drawing on it as the foundation decoding requires. The causal direction runs the other way. Phonemic awareness supports learning to read, so waiting for reading to produce it inverts the relationship and leaves students without the foundation decoding requires.","Providing phoneme instruction only in one-on-one tutoring sessions and leaving it out of whole-class time":"Treats tiered support as a stand-in for core instruction instead of an addition layered on top of strong whole-class teaching. Tiered support adds to core instruction; it does not replace it. Removing phoneme work from the whole-class block leaves the class dependent on pull-out time that is usually too short to close the gap."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-052","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten student names most uppercase letters correctly but rarely identifies their lowercase forms. Which instructional approach is most effective?","options":["Setting letter work aside and continuing oral language activities until the student shows more interest in letters","Teaching both forms together, pointing out that they share a name and a sound, and having the student match and sort uppercase and lowercase pairs","Teaching uppercase letters to mastery first and then introducing the lowercase forms one at a time","Having the student print both forms of each letter daily until letter formation becomes automatic"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how print concepts and letter knowledge are taught explicitly. Presenting the two cases together is efficient because the name and the sound stay constant while only the shape changes, which reduces the memory load. Matching and sorting pairs also prepares the student for the fact that running text is mostly lowercase, with capitals reserved for special uses.","dex":{"Setting letter work aside and continuing oral language activities until the student shows more interest in letters":"Treats readiness as something to wait for rather than something teaching builds, turning a documented need into a reason to postpone. Letter knowledge is taught, not waited for. Setting it aside removes an instructional opportunity instead of responding to what the assessment showed the student needs.","Teaching uppercase letters to mastery first and then introducing the lowercase forms one at a time":"Trusts a one-case-at-a-time sequence as the safe route, though withholding lowercase delays the form students meet most often in books. Delaying lowercase forms postpones work with the case students encounter most in books, and it can produce the very confusion the sequence is meant to prevent. Both cases can be learned at once.","Having the student print both forms of each letter daily until letter formation becomes automatic":"Equates the motor act of forming a letter with knowing it, as if a student who can print a letter can necessarily name it and give its sound. Handwriting practice builds letter formation and may reinforce recognition indirectly, but motor practice alone does not guarantee that a student can name the letter or recall its sound."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-053","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Pratt's kindergartners can blend the spoken sounds /k/ /a/ /t/ to say cat, but most of them cannot read the printed word cat. Which statement best explains this pattern?","options":["They need more oral blending practice before any printed letters are introduced","They need to memorize cat as a whole word before additional words are introduced","They have phonemic awareness but have not yet connected sounds to letters, so instruction should make that mapping explicit","Their phonemic awareness is not yet secure, as the inability to read the printed word shows"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the alphabetic principle and how it is taught. Blending spoken sounds shows that students can work with phonemes by ear. Reading the printed word additionally requires knowing that those phonemes are represented by c, a, and t, and holding those mappings while blending. Explicit phonics work with letter tiles or sound boxes supplies the missing link.","dex":{"They need more oral blending practice before any printed letters are introduced":"Holds that phonemic awareness must be complete before letters appear, when pairing sounds with letters during blending is what builds the alphabetic insight. Once students can blend orally, waiting to add letters delays decoding. Pairing sounds with letters during blending is exactly what builds the alphabetic insight, and it is more effective than more oral-only practice.","They need to memorize cat as a whole word before additional words are introduced":"Offers memorizing whole words in place of explicit mapping, mistaking a small stock of recognized words for the ability to read. Memorizing individual words gives students no strategy for words they have not been taught, which is precisely the problem the alphabetic principle solves.","Their phonemic awareness is not yet secure, as the inability to read the printed word shows":"Reads the failure with print backward as weak phonemic awareness, though oral blending is intact and the gap lies in sound-to-letter mapping. The evidence points the other way: these students blend spoken sounds successfully. Their difficulty appears only when print is involved, so the gap is in mapping, not in phonemic awareness."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-054","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Hale is setting up a center where kindergarten students will work on phonological and phonemic awareness. Which of the following materials would best support that goal? Select all that apply.","options":["Sets of picture cards that students sort by rhyming word and by beginning sound","Short decodable passages containing only the letter-sound patterns taught so far","Elkonin boxes with counters that students push forward for each sound they hear","An alphabet chart that students trace to practice forming each letter","Hand mirrors that students use to notice how their mouths produce a sound"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how materials support particular instructional goals. Sorting picture cards by rhyme and by beginning sound asks students to compare the sound structure of spoken words with no print involved, which is exactly the work of phonological and phonemic awareness. The sort also lets the teacher see which contrasts a student has not yet mastered.  [C] Elkonin boxes give each phoneme a visible slot, so the abstract act of segmenting becomes something students can see, touch, and count. The counters are a temporary support; the goal is for students to internalize the count and eventually do the work mentally.  [E] Mirrors make fine distinctions between similar phonemes visible, such as where the tongue sits or whether the lips close. Articulatory feedback helps students both hear and produce contrasts they would otherwise miss, which strengthens phonemic awareness.","dex":{"Short decodable passages containing only the letter-sound patterns taught so far":"Files decodable passages under phonological awareness when they serve decoding and fluency; awareness work is oral and needs no print at all. Decodable passages are valuable, but they serve decoding and fluency practice. Students who cannot yet hear and manipulate the sounds have little to apply to the print, so the passages belong at a different center.","An alphabet chart that students trace to practice forming each letter":"Counts tracing letters as awareness practice, though knowing letter names and shapes does not mean a student can hear and manipulate phonemes. Tracing an alphabet chart practices letter formation and may build letter-name knowledge. It does not require students to attend to the sound structure of spoken words, so it does not develop phonological awareness."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,2,4]},{"id":"8006-foundational-055","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which sequence reflects the developmental progression of phonological awareness instruction in kindergarten and first grade?","options":["Individual phonemes first, because they are the smallest units and therefore the simplest to notice","Onset-rime work alone until students enter second grade, then individual phonemes","Syllables and individual phonemes taught together from the first week, since both involve breaking words apart","Awareness of words in sentences, then syllables, then onset and rime, then individual phonemes"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how phonological awareness develops from larger to smaller units of sound. Children notice words in sentences and then syllables before they can attend to the rime of a word, and they handle onsets and rimes well before they can hold three separate phonemes in mind. Instruction that follows this progression lets each level rest on the one before it.","dex":{"Individual phonemes first, because they are the smallest units and therefore the simplest to notice":"Takes smallest to mean simplest, inverting the real relation: the smaller the unit, the heavier the memory load and the harder the task. Smaller is harder, not simpler. Isolating the middle phoneme of a word requires holding the rest of the word in memory while removing one piece, a demand beginning students cannot yet meet.","Onset-rime work alone until students enter second grade, then individual phonemes":"Settles on onset-rime work as the destination rather than the bridge it is, leaving students short of the phonemic awareness decoding depends on. Onset-rime work is an important bridge, but stopping there for a year leaves students without the phonemic awareness that predicts decoding success and delays the phonics it is meant to support.","Syllables and individual phonemes taught together from the first week, since both involve breaking words apart":"Groups every kind of word-taking-apart together, ignoring that syllables and phonemes sit at different levels and that one prepares students for the other. Teaching both levels at once from the start ignores the fact that syllable-level work is what makes phoneme-level work possible. Students would be asked to manipulate units they cannot yet hear as separate."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-056","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Nakamura's first-grade student, who is learning English, follows multistep directions and answers yes-or-no questions accurately but rarely speaks in complete sentences during group discussion. Which approach best supports the student's oral language development?","options":["Keeping the content demanding while lowering the response demand, using partner turns, sentence frames, and wait time before the student speaks to the whole group","Simplifying the content so that the student needs only short, familiar phrases to participate","Calling on the student regularly for full-sentence answers in front of the group to build the habit of speaking","Referring the student for a speech and language evaluation, because the gap between understanding and speaking indicates a disorder"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how receptive and expressive language develop. Receptive language normally runs ahead of expressive language, so a student can understand far more than they can yet produce. Lowering the risk of the response through a partner turn and a sentence frame keeps the thinking demanding while making the language manageable, which is what moves a student from understanding to producing.","dex":{"Simplifying the content so that the student needs only short, familiar phrases to participate":"Mistakes watering down content for scaffolding, since easing the thinking also removes the practice with complex sentence structures the student most needs. Simplifying the content lowers the thinking along with the language, so the student gets less practice with the very structures that need developing. The aim is to keep ideas complex while supporting how they are expressed.","Calling on the student regularly for full-sentence answers in front of the group to build the habit of speaking":"Trusts repeated public calling-on to build speech, though performing without a language frame raises anxiety instead of supplying the missing pattern. Public pressure raises anxiety and offers no linguistic support for how to build the sentence. A student who lacks the pattern does not gain it by being asked to perform without it.","Referring the student for a speech and language evaluation, because the gap between understanding and speaking indicates a disorder":"Pathologizes a normal receptive-expressive gap in a developing language learner, reaching for a referral before documented instruction has had its chance. A receptive-expressive gap is typical of developing language learners, including students learning English, and does not by itself indicate a disorder. A referral should follow documented, well-supported instruction, not replace it."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-057","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Ochoa is writing a small-group lesson whose objective is phoneme substitution. She sketches four possible tasks. Which example best supports that objective?","options":["Students sort picture cards by whether the name of the picture begins with /m/","Students say mat, are told to change /m/ to /s/, and say the new word","Students blend the sounds /s/ /a/ /t/ to say sat","Students clap the three syllables in Saturday"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to evaluate an example against a specific instructional goal. Substitution is a manipulation task: the student holds the word in memory, removes one phoneme, inserts another, and produces the result. Turning mat into sat does exactly that, at the phoneme level and at the right level of difficulty for the stated objective.","dex":{"Students sort picture cards by whether the name of the picture begins with /m/":"Treats isolating and sorting initial sounds as manipulation, when recognizing a phoneme is only the prerequisite for changing one and hearing what the change produces. Sorting by initial sound asks students to isolate and compare one phoneme across words. That is a beginning-level identification task; it does not require changing a word and hearing what the change produces.","Students blend the sounds /s/ /a/ /t/ to say sat":"Accepts blending as substitution, though blending only combines sounds while substitution also requires holding the word in memory, removing one, and inserting another. Blending asks students to combine sounds into a word. It is a prerequisite for substitution rather than the same operation, because nothing is removed or replaced.","Students clap the three syllables in Saturday":"Overlooks that syllables are far larger units than phonemes, so a clapping task never reaches the level the objective names. Clapping the syllables in Saturday works at the syllable level, far above the individual phoneme, and the operation is segmentation rather than substitution."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-058","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Ferrand needs to screen the phonemic awareness of a kindergarten student who recently enrolled and whose strongest language is not English. Which approach is most appropriate?","options":["Administering the English task and treating a low score as evidence of a phonological deficit","Skipping phonemic awareness and screening letter knowledge instead, because letter names are language-neutral","Using oral phonemic awareness tasks in the student's strongest language where possible, because awareness of sound structure transfers across languages","Delaying screening until the student has enough English to complete the task in English"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how assessment can respond to students' varied language experience. Phonemic awareness is a metalinguistic insight into how speech is structured, and once acquired it transfers across languages. Screening in the student's stronger language therefore reveals what the student actually knows instead of measuring how much English they have learned.","dex":{"Administering the English task and treating a low score as evidence of a phonological deficit":"Reads a language difference as a deficit, drawing conclusions about phonemic awareness from a task that mostly measures knowledge of English sounds. A task that requires manipulating English sounds a student has barely heard measures English sound knowledge, not the underlying capacity. Treating the result as a deficit risks mistaking a language difference for a disability.","Skipping phonemic awareness and screening letter knowledge instead, because letter names are language-neutral":"Swaps in letter knowledge as a proxy, though naming letters and segmenting spoken sounds are different constructs and one cannot stand in for the other. Letter knowledge is a useful early measure, but it assesses a different construct. A student can name many letters and still be unable to segment the sounds in a spoken word, so it cannot stand in for a phonemic awareness screen.","Delaying screening until the student has enough English to complete the task in English":"Ties assessment to English proficiency, delaying instruction on the assumption that a student must master the language before anything can be measured or taught. Waiting for English proficiency stalls instruction. The purpose of screening is to decide what to teach now, and a student's development can be assessed and supported without waiting for English to catch up."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-059","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A second-grade teacher wants students to use the parts of a book purposefully when they read nonfiction. Which of the following activities would best develop that understanding? Select all that apply.","options":["Using the table of contents to identify the page on which a specific chapter begins","Reading the chapter titles aloud to practice fluent oral reading of nonfiction text","Copying the title, author, and copyright year from the title page onto a worksheet","Using the index to find the pages on which a particular animal is mentioned","Using the glossary to confirm the meaning of a term printed in bold in the text"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how concepts of print extend to the parts of a book and their functions. Consulting the table of contents to locate a chapter teaches students what that section is for and how it is organized by page number, which is knowledge they can reuse with any nonfiction book.  [D] The index is an alphabetical guide to where topics appear, so using it to find a specific animal shows students how the section differs from the table of contents and when each one is the better tool. The activity pairs the part with its function.  [E] Turning to the glossary to check a bold term links a typographic signal in the body of the text to the section that explains it. Students learn both what the symbol means and where to go for help, which is the functional understanding the teacher wants.","dex":{"Reading the chapter titles aloud to practice fluent oral reading of nonfiction text":"Assumes that reading from a part of a book teaches what the part is for, as though fluent reading of titles were the same as using them to locate information. Reading chapter titles aloud may build fluency with nonfiction vocabulary, but the student is using the words as text to read rather than as a navigation tool. Awareness of the part's function is not required.","Copying the title, author, and copyright year from the title page onto a worksheet":"Counts transcribing bibliographic details as understanding, though the task never asks when or why a reader would turn to that page. Transcribing bibliographic information is a clerical task. It can be completed without any understanding of what a title page is for or how a reader uses it."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,3,4]},{"id":"8006-foundational-060","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Mr. Duffy teaches one letter a week in alphabetical order. Students who already know letter names learn letters such as b and m easily but struggle with h and w. Which revision is best supported by what is known about the alphabetic principle?","options":["Dropping letter names from instruction and teaching only sounds, since names distract students from the alphabetic principle","Teaching only the letters whose names contain their sounds first and delaying the remaining letters until second grade","Keeping the alphabetical order but spending more weeks on each letter so that students have additional time to learn it","Teaching the letter name and sound together, pointing out where the name contains the sound, and scheduling extra distributed practice for letters such as h and w where it does not"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how letter-name knowledge supports the alphabetic principle. For many letters the name contains the sound, so the name acts as a built-in retrieval cue. For letters such as h and w the name offers no cue at all, so those letters need explicit statement of the sound plus additional, spaced practice. Teaching names and sounds together exploits the cue where it exists and compensates where it does not.","dex":{"Dropping letter names from instruction and teaching only sounds, since names distract students from the alphabetic principle":"Sets letter names against letter sounds and discards a built-in retrieval cue that helps students learn the sound for most letters. Letter names are an asset rather than a distraction. For most letters the name contains the sound, and children who know names generally learn the corresponding sounds more readily than those who do not.","Teaching only the letters whose names contain their sounds first and delaying the remaining letters until second grade":"Treats difficulty as a reason for delay, leaving holes in the set of mappings students need and shrinking the words they can decode. Delaying the harder letters until second grade leaves gaps in the very set of mappings students need for decoding. Difficulty is a reason for more practice and better support, not for postponement.","Keeping the alphabetical order but spending more weeks on each letter so that students have additional time to learn it":"Adds time instead of changing method, missing that the obstacle is an absent name-to-sound cue that calls for explicit teaching and spaced practice. More time per letter does not address the source of the difficulty, which is the absence of a name-to-sound cue. Alphabetical order also ignores the advantage of teaching letters that unlock many words early."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-061","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Barr's kindergarten sound wall displays the English phonemes with their common spellings and mouth pictures. Several of her students speak Spanish at home. Which adaptation would best use the display to support those students' phonemic awareness?","options":["Marking the phonemes that do not occur in Spanish and building in frequent targeted practice on those, while keeping the wall as a shared reference for the whole class","Removing the English phonemes that do not occur in Spanish so that the display matches the students' current sound system","Expecting the students to acquire the new English phonemes through daily read-alouds, since exposure builds awareness","Using the sound wall only with the English-speaking students and giving the Spanish-speaking students picture cards instead"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how classroom displays can be adapted for students with different language experience. Marking which phonemes are new turns the wall from a poster into an instructional plan: the teacher knows which mouth pictures to revisit and which contrasts need extra listening practice. Keeping it shared serves everyone and holds the same expectations for all students.","dex":{"Removing the English phonemes that do not occur in Spanish so that the display matches the students' current sound system":"Confuses matching the display to students' current sound system with adapting it, cutting out the very contrasts they most need to learn. Removing the difficult phonemes removes exactly what these students most need to learn. A display should organize instruction around the contrasts students have not yet acquired, not around the ones they already have.","Expecting the students to acquire the new English phonemes through daily read-alouds, since exposure builds awareness":"Expects immersion to do the work unaided, though a phoneme a student cannot yet hear must be noticed, named, and practiced. Incidental exposure in read-alouds is not enough for phonemes a student does not yet perceive. New contrasts have to be noticed, named, and practiced before they are available for reading and spelling.","Using the sound wall only with the English-speaking students and giving the Spanish-speaking students picture cards instead":"Reads differentiation as handing some students lesser materials, when it should change how a shared resource is used and how much practice it receives. Separate materials signal lower expectations and remove access to core instruction. The same wall, used deliberately and with additional practice, supports every student in the room."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-062","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Vega's students readily produce rhymes and blend onsets and rimes, but after several weeks most still cannot segment a three-phoneme word such as ship into its separate sounds. Which interpretation is most accurate?","options":["Rhyming is unrelated to reading development, so the time spent on it was wasted","Rhyme and onset-rime awareness are precursors that do not automatically produce phoneme-level awareness, so instruction should add explicit segmentation work at the phoneme level","The students likely have a phonological processing disorder and should be referred for evaluation","Continued rhyming practice will eventually produce phoneme awareness on its own"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how phonological awareness develops into phonemic awareness. Rhyme and onset-rime work builds the habit of attending to the sound structure of words, but holding three separate phonemes in sequence is a harder demand that requires its own instruction. The accurate reading of these data is that the students are ready for the next level, not that the earlier instruction failed.","dex":{"Rhyming is unrelated to reading development, so the time spent on it was wasted":"Dismisses rhyme work as wasted effort because it proved insufficient, overlooking that it lays the foundation the next step builds on. Rhyme and onset-rime awareness are well-established contributors to later reading and belong in a systematic sequence. Dropping them would remove the foundation that the next step builds on.","The students likely have a phonological processing disorder and should be referred for evaluation":"Blames stalled progress on a student deficit and skips the required step of first delivering explicit instruction at the level where students are struggling. Several weeks without progress at the phoneme level is a signal to change instruction, not evidence of a disorder. A referral is premature before students have received explicit, systematic teaching at the level where they are struggling.","Continued rhyming practice will eventually produce phoneme awareness on its own":"Expects awareness at the larger-unit level to trickle down on its own, when reaching phonemes requires explicit teaching to bridge the levels. Larger-unit awareness does not automatically generalize to smaller units. Students can rhyme fluently and still be unable to count or isolate the individual phonemes in a word."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-063","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher wants to build students' expressive language during a unit on plants. Which routine would best accomplish this goal?","options":["Giving students a page of plant words to trace and copy","Asking students to listen quietly while the teacher reads a book about how seeds travel","Having partners take turns telling how their own seedling changed, using the frame \"First ___, then ___, and now ___\"","Showing students labeled photos of roots, stems, and leaves and asking them to point to the stem"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how expressive language is developed. Expressive language grows only when students produce extended oral output themselves. The sentence frame lowers the planning load so students can spend their effort on putting a real observation into words, and the partner format guarantees every student a turn instead of only the volunteers who already speak readily.","dex":{"Giving students a page of plant words to trace and copy":"Assuming that tracing and copying words builds spoken language Option (D) is incorrect. Tracing and copying are print and handwriting tasks. They produce marks on a page rather than spoken language, so they leave the expressive channel unexercised.","Asking students to listen quietly while the teacher reads a book about how seeds travel":"Equating rich receptive input with the development of expressive output Option (A) is incorrect. A read-aloud supplies rich vocabulary and sentence structures, but it asks students to receive language, not to generate it. Rich input is a necessary companion to expressive work, not a substitute for it.","Showing students labeled photos of roots, stems, and leaves and asking them to point to the stem":"Treating pointing and recognition responses as if they forced students to generate language Option (C) is incorrect. Pointing to a named part requires students to map a word they hear onto a picture, which taps receptive vocabulary. The student produces no sentence and no idea of their own."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-064","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher is planning the order in which phonemic awareness skills will be taught across the year. Which sequence moves from less complex to more complex?","options":["Deleting a phoneme from a word → substituting a phoneme → blending individual phonemes → recognizing rhymes","Blending individual phonemes → recognizing rhymes → blending onset and rime → deleting a phoneme from a word","Recognizing rhymes → deleting a phoneme from a word → substituting a phoneme → blending onset and rime","Recognizing rhymes → blending onset and rime → blending individual phonemes → deleting a phoneme from a word"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the developmental progression within phonemic awareness. Larger sound units are easier to perceive than smaller ones. Working with whole words through rhyme comes first, then splitting words into the two chunks of onset and rime, then attending to each separate phoneme, and finally manipulating phonemes by deleting or substituting them, which adds a memory load because the student must hold the word in mind while changing it.","dex":{"Deleting a phoneme from a word → substituting a phoneme → blending individual phonemes → recognizing rhymes":"Presenting the developmental progression in reverse, as though mastering the hardest operation first would carry the easier ones along Option (B) is incorrect. This is the progression run backwards. Deletion and substitution depend on the student already being able to hear and hold individual phonemes, so starting there asks for the hardest operation before its prerequisites exist.","Blending individual phonemes → recognizing rhymes → blending onset and rime → deleting a phoneme from a word":"Placing analysis of individual phonemes ahead of word-level rhyme work Option (C) is incorrect. Individual phoneme blending is placed first, before rhyme, and onset-rime work is pushed to third. Phoneme-level work is more demanding than word-level rhyme work, so this order asks students to analyze the smallest units before they have practiced hearing the largest ones.","Recognizing rhymes → deleting a phoneme from a word → substituting a phoneme → blending onset and rime":"Assuming deletion and substitution can be taught before students isolate individual phonemes Option (D) is incorrect. This order jumps from rhyme straight to deletion and substitution and leaves individual phoneme blending and segmenting for last, even though manipulation cannot succeed until students can isolate the separate sounds in a word."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-065","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher wants a quick, informal check of whether students can segment a word into its individual phonemes. Which task best provides that information?","options":["Asking students to clap once for each part they hear in the word table","Asking students to push a counter forward for each sound they hear in the word dog","Asking students to pick the picture that rhymes with dog from two choices","Asking students to point to the letter that begins the word dog on an alphabet chart"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how an informal assessment isolates one specific skill. Moving one counter per sound in a three-phoneme word requires the student to isolate each phoneme in order, and the number of counters a child produces shows immediately whether that child is working at the phoneme level or is still responding to larger units.","dex":{"Asking students to clap once for each part they hear in the word table":"Treating syllable tapping as evidence of phoneme-level segmentation Option (A) is incorrect. Clapping the parts of table measures syllable awareness. A child can clap two beats perfectly while hearing no individual phonemes at all, so this task cannot answer the question the teacher is asking.","Asking students to pick the picture that rhymes with dog from two choices":"Accepting rhyme judgment as proof that a student can analyze a word's internal sound sequence Option (C) is incorrect. Rhyme judgment works at the level of whole words and their endings. It requires no analysis of the internal sound sequence and therefore reveals nothing about segmentation.","Asking students to point to the letter that begins the word dog on an alphabet chart":"Using a print-based letter-identification task to measure an oral listening skill Option (D) is incorrect. Pointing to a letter on an alphabet chart measures letter knowledge, which involves print. Phonemic awareness is an oral skill assessed without any print at all."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-066","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"During a shared reading of a big book, a kindergarten student points to the illustration instead of the print and begins at the bottom of the page. Which concept of print should the teacher address first?","options":["That print, not pictures, carries the message, and that English print is read top to bottom and left to right","That an uppercase letter marks the beginning of a sentence","That a book has a title page that names it","That spaces mark the boundaries between words"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the order in which concepts of print develop. Before a child can track print, the child has to know two things: that the print is what carries the story, and that it travels in a fixed direction. Pointing to pictures and starting at the bottom shows both are missing, so they are addressed first; capitalization, book parts, and word boundaries are taught once the child can follow a line of print.","dex":{"That an uppercase letter marks the beginning of a sentence":"Teaching capitalization rules before establishing that print carries meaning and travels in a fixed direction Option (B) is incorrect. Capitalization at sentence openings is a real concept of print, but it is meaningful only to a student who has already located the print and is following it. Teaching it first leaves the underlying tracking problem untouched.","That a book has a title page that names it":"Treating book-handling knowledge as the remedy for a difficulty with print directionality Option (C) is incorrect. Identifying the title page is book-handling knowledge. It tells a child how a book is organized, not where to begin on a page or which way the print moves.","That spaces mark the boundaries between words":"Introducing word-boundary awareness before a student can locate and follow a line of print Option (D) is incorrect. Word boundaries matter for matching spoken words to printed words while tracking, but a child who is not yet starting at the top of the page has no line of print to apply them to."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-067","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten student can tell the teacher that the spoken word sun has three sounds and can blend /s/ /u/ /n/ back into the word. Shown the printed word sun, the student cannot read it. This performance most strongly indicates that the student has not yet developed","options":["knowledge of the names of the letters","the ability to hear individual phonemes in spoken words","an understanding that letters stand for the sounds in spoken words","awareness that print is read from left to right"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the alphabetic principle. The student's oral performance shows that phonemic awareness is already in place, so the missing piece is the insight that the letters on the page are a code for those sounds. Without that mapping, a printed word carries no retrievable sound values, and instruction must make the letter-to-sound correspondence visible and explicit.","dex":{"knowledge of the names of the letters":"Treating letter-name knowledge as the mechanism that makes reading possible Option (D) is incorrect. Letter names help a student remember letter sounds, but reciting names is neither necessary nor sufficient for reading. A child can know every letter name and still not grasp that the letters represent the sounds in a word.","the ability to hear individual phonemes in spoken words":"Attributing a failure to read printed words to a phonemic awareness deficit Option (A) is incorrect. The student segmented and blended the sounds of sun orally, which is direct evidence that the phonemes are perceptible to this child. Adding more phonemic awareness practice would reteach a skill the student has already demonstrated.","awareness that print is read from left to right":"Explaining a breakdown in letter-sound mapping as a problem with print directionality Option (C) is incorrect. A directionality problem produces tracking errors, such as reading the right-hand page first or sweeping to the wrong margin. This student is not mis-tracking; the student is not recovering sounds from letters at all."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-068","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher is setting up the literacy environment. Which use of classroom materials would best support students' phonemic awareness?","options":["An alphabet frieze with one keyword picture for each letter","A bulletin board displaying student writing samples with illustrations","A word wall of high-frequency words arranged in alphabetical order","A sound wall that groups pictures by how each phoneme is produced in the mouth"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how classroom materials can make the sound structure of language visible. A sound wall organized by articulation directs attention to the phonemes themselves, to how the mouth forms them and how one differs from its neighbors. That keeps the work at the oral, speech level that phonemic awareness occupies instead of skipping ahead to print.","dex":{"An alphabet frieze with one keyword picture for each letter":"Equating a one-picture-per-letter alphabet frieze with instruction in phonemic awareness Option (C) is incorrect. A one-picture-per-letter alphabet frieze supports beginning letter-sound correspondence, which is useful but different. Because it shows twenty-six letters, it also risks leaving students with the impression that English has only twenty-six sounds.","A bulletin board displaying student writing samples with illustrations":"Assuming that displaying student work on a wall supports work on the sound structure of language Option (D) is incorrect. Displaying finished writing builds a print-rich room and values students' composition work, but the display itself gives no opportunity to hear, isolate, or manipulate sounds.","A word wall of high-frequency words arranged in alphabetical order":"Mistaking an alphabetically sorted word wall for a tool that trains phoneme manipulation Option (A) is incorrect. An alphabetically arranged word wall sorts words by letter name and helps students locate and spell known words. It supports sight-word reading and writing, not the ability to hear and manipulate individual sounds."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-069","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Alvarez is preparing an onset-rime lesson for her kindergarten class. Several students are English learners who are still building oral vocabulary in English. Which modification to the lesson materials would best support those students?","options":["Removing the pictures and working with spoken words only, so that students attend to sounds rather than images","Pairing each spoken word with a picture whose name is already familiar, so students can manipulate the sounds of a word they know","Replacing the spoken words with printed word cards so students can see the shared rime","Adding a written sorting worksheet for students to complete on their own after the lesson"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to modify examples and materials for particular learners. Onset-rime work is an oral task, and these students may not yet have a stable label for the word being taken apart. Pictures with familiar referents carry the vocabulary load, so the students' attention stays on the sound structure instead of on guessing what the word means.","dex":{"Removing the pictures and working with spoken words only, so that students attend to sounds rather than images":"Removing visual vocabulary supports on the assumption that images distract from auditory processing Option (A) is incorrect. Taking pictures away raises the language demand precisely for the students who need it lowered. A child who is unsure what the word means cannot manipulate its parts, so the task would end up measuring vocabulary rather than phonological awareness.","Replacing the spoken words with printed word cards so students can see the shared rime":"Converting an oral phonological task into a print-based decoding task Option (C) is incorrect. Putting the words in print shifts the task toward orthography and decoding. Onset-rime manipulation is meant to be done with the ear, and requiring print adds a demand these students are not ready for.","Adding a written sorting worksheet for students to complete on their own after the lesson":"Substituting an independent written worksheet for oral modeling and guided practice Option (D) is incorrect. An independent written sort removes both the oral response and the teacher's modeling, so students lose the feedback that tells them whether they split the word in the right place."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-070","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"During a phoneme segmentation activity, a kindergarten student correctly segments the familiar two-syllable words rabbit, pencil, and wagon but cannot segment the less familiar two-syllable words compass, falcon, and beacon. Which interpretation of this pattern best guides the teacher's next step?","options":["The student's difficulty is limited to words of two syllables, so the teacher should keep phonemic awareness tasks to single-syllable words","The student cannot yet isolate individual phonemes, so the teacher should restart segmentation instruction with shorter, one-syllable words","The student knows the second set of words too poorly in speech to hold them in mind while manipulating sounds, so the teacher should build oral familiarity with those words first","The student has not learned the letter-sound correspondences in the second set of words, so the teacher should teach those letters before returning to segmentation"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how oral language supports phonemic awareness. Phonemic awareness tasks are performed on spoken words, so a student must already know a word well enough to hold it in memory and then reflect on its sound structure. Because this student segments two-syllable words accurately whenever the words are familiar, the ability to isolate phonemes is present; what breaks down is the student's spoken knowledge of the less familiar words. The teacher's next step is to build that oral vocabulary through talk, pictures, and repeated spoken use before asking the student to take those words apart again.","dex":{"The student's difficulty is limited to words of two syllables, so the teacher should keep phonemic awareness tasks to single-syllable words":"Attributing the failures to word length when both sets of words contained the same number of syllables Option (D) is incorrect. Both sets of words have two syllables, so syllable count cannot account for the split between the words the student segmented and the words the student could not. Restricting tasks to single-syllable words would narrow the activity without addressing the vocabulary gap behind the failures.","The student cannot yet isolate individual phonemes, so the teacher should restart segmentation instruction with shorter, one-syllable words":"Treating failure on a segmentation task as proof that phonemic awareness itself is missing, without first asking whether the words used were known to the student Option (A) is incorrect. The student already segments familiar two-syllable words accurately, which is direct evidence that the ability to isolate phonemes has developed. Restarting with one-syllable words would reteach a skill the student has demonstrated while leaving the real obstacle, unfamiliar vocabulary, untouched.","The student has not learned the letter-sound correspondences in the second set of words, so the teacher should teach those letters before returning to segmentation":"Confusing phonemic awareness, an oral task done without print, with phonics instruction that depends on letter-sound correspondences Option (C) is incorrect. Segmentation is an oral task done with no print in view, so letter knowledge is not required to perform it. Letter-sound correspondences belong to phonics and decoding, and they cannot explain why the student succeeds on one set of spoken words and fails on the other."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-071","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A first-grade teacher says, \"Say bend. Now say bend again without the /b/.\" A student responds \"ben.\" This response most strongly indicates a difficulty with","options":["blending separate phonemes into a whole word","recognizing that two spoken words share the same rime","hearing the final sound in a spoken word","locating the position of a target phoneme within the sequence of sounds in a word"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of what phoneme deletion tasks reveal about a student. The student carried out the mechanics of the operation but took off the wrong phoneme, removing the final sound instead of the initial one. That pattern points to the positional analysis of the word, knowing which slot in the sound sequence the target occupies, rather than to any inability to perform the deletion itself.","dex":{"blending separate phonemes into a whole word":"Confusing phoneme deletion with the opposite operation of blending sounds into a word Option (C) is incorrect. Blending runs in the opposite direction, assembling separate sounds into a word, and nothing in this response shows a blending failure. The student produced a well-formed word.","recognizing that two spoken words share the same rime":"Explaining an error in phoneme manipulation by appealing to word-level rhyme awareness Option (D) is incorrect. Rime recognition compares the endings of whole words and is a much earlier-developing skill. It cannot explain an error made while manipulating a single phoneme inside one word.","hearing the final sound in a spoken word":"Interpreting removal of the wrong phoneme as an inability to perceive the final sound Option (A) is incorrect. The student clearly perceived the final sound; dropping it requires attending to it. The error is not that the final sound went unheard but that the student searched the word in the wrong place."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-072","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A kindergarten teacher wants an informal screening measure that will show whether students from varied language backgrounds are developing awareness of individual phonemes. Which two tasks should the teacher include? Select all that apply.","options":["Moving one block for each sound a student hears in the word ship","Telling what is left after the teacher says moon without the /m/","Clapping once for each part a student hears in the word banana","Naming as many letters of the alphabet as possible in one minute","Identifying which of two pictures rhymes with boat"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of which assessment tasks operate at the phoneme level. Pushing one block per sound requires the student to isolate each phoneme in sequence, and because the task is oral and uses concrete objects, it works as well for students whose English vocabularies are still small as it does for native speakers.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how manipulation tasks index phonemic awareness. Deleting an initial phoneme and reporting what remains is one of the strongest indicators that a student can hold a word's sound structure in mind and act on it, and it is a well-established predictor of later decoding growth.","dex":{"Clapping once for each part a student hears in the word banana":"Treating syllable tapping as evidence that phoneme-level awareness has developed Option (C) is incorrect. Clapping the parts of banana measures syllable awareness. A student can tap three beats correctly while hearing no individual phonemes, so this task cannot answer whether phonemic awareness is developing.","Naming as many letters of the alphabet as possible in one minute":"Using a print-based letter-naming fluency task to screen an oral phonemic skill Option (D) is incorrect. Rapid letter naming is a measure of alphabet knowledge and retrieval speed. It involves print and says nothing about a student's awareness of the sounds inside spoken words.","Identifying which of two pictures rhymes with boat":"Assuming that success with rhyme identification implies phoneme-level awareness Option (E) is incorrect. Rhyme identification is phonological awareness at the whole-word level and develops earlier than phonemic awareness. A student who succeeds here may still be unable to isolate a single phoneme."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-foundational-073","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A preschool teacher points to the cover of a book and says, \"This tells us the name of our book.\" Which concept of print is the teacher introducing?","options":["That a book is made of parts with distinct functions, and that the cover announces what the book is about","That print moves from left to right across the page","That each spoken word is matched by a printed word","That a lowercase letter can be matched to its uppercase form"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how book-handling knowledge is taught. Naming the cover and explaining what it does builds the idea that a book is organized into parts, each with its own job. That idea is what later lets students use the table of contents, headings, and page numbers as tools for finding information.","dex":{"That print moves from left to right across the page":"Treating an explanation of book parts as instruction in print directionality Option (B) is incorrect. Directionality is built by tracking print line by line during shared reading, not by talking about the cover. The two ideas live at different levels: one is about the page, the other is about the book as an object.","That each spoken word is matched by a printed word":"Confusing word awareness, the matching of speech to print, with knowledge of how books are built Option (C) is incorrect. Matching spoken words to printed words is word awareness, a concept demonstrated by pointing under each word as a line is read. The cover comment does not involve matching speech to print.","That a lowercase letter can be matched to its uppercase form":"Mistaking letter-form matching for an understanding of book structure Option (D) is incorrect. Matching uppercase and lowercase forms is letter-level knowledge, far more fine-grained than what the teacher is pointing at. A child can be taught letter forms without any understanding of how a book is put together."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-074","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which activity most directly demonstrates the alphabetic principle to beginning readers?","options":["Students push a letter tile into each box of a three-box chart as they say the sounds in mop, then run a finger under the tiles to blend them into the word","Students clap once for each part they hear in rabbit, then sort picture cards by how many parts the names have","Students sort picture cards by whether the names begin with the same sound","Students reread a familiar predictable text to a partner to build expression"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how the alphabetic principle is made visible to novices. Placing one letter tile per spoken sound attaches a written symbol to each phoneme the student can already hear, and sweeping a finger under the tiles to blend them shows that reading a word means recovering the sounds its letters stand for. The oral skill the student already has is thereby connected to print.","dex":{"Students clap once for each part they hear in rabbit, then sort picture cards by how many parts the names have":"Treating syllable-level phonological work as instruction in the alphabetic principle Option (B) is incorrect. Clapping and counting syllables operates entirely in speech with no letters present. It is phonological awareness at the syllable level, which sits below the alphabetic principle.","Students sort picture cards by whether the names begin with the same sound":"Mistaking a print-free phonemic awareness sort for work on letter-sound correspondence Option (C) is incorrect. Sorting pictures by initial sound is phonemic awareness, and it is deliberately done without print. Because no letters appear, nothing about the letter-to-sound relationship can be demonstrated.","Students reread a familiar predictable text to a partner to build expression":"Assuming that fluency practice with familiar text builds understanding of the alphabetic principle Option (D) is incorrect. Partner rereading builds automaticity and prosody on text the student already knows. It asks for whole-word recognition and expression rather than for analysis of individual letter-sound correspondences."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-075","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Ms. Dube is reviewing the posters in her first-grade room before the new term. She wants the displays to reflect how the English sound system actually works rather than how it happens to be spelled. Which two decisions would accomplish this? Select all that apply.","options":["Organizing the sound wall by phoneme and grouping together the different spellings that can represent each sound","Adding a card for each phoneme that shows a mouth picture of how that sound is produced","Organizing the word wall alphabetically by first letter","Labeling a poster with the twenty-six letters and one keyword picture for each letter","Posting the week's spelling words in the order in which they appear in the story"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how the phonological system differs from the orthographic system. English has considerably more phonemes than letters and many ways to spell each one, so a wall arranged by sound with its spelling variants grouped beneath it represents the sound system faithfully, whereas a letter-based arrangement quietly teaches that each letter owns exactly one sound.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how articulation defines a phoneme. A mouth picture shows the placement and manner of production, which is what actually distinguishes one sound from its neighbor. That keeps students' attention on the speech signal itself rather than on the letters used to record it.","dex":{"Organizing the word wall alphabetically by first letter":"Presenting alphabetical order as though it described the organization of the sound system Option (C) is incorrect. Alphabetical order is a convention of the writing system imposed for the sake of easy lookup. It sorts by grapheme and conveys nothing about how many sounds a word contains or how those sounds are made.","Labeling a poster with the twenty-six letters and one keyword picture for each letter":"Assuming the twenty-six letters of the alphabet correspond one to one with the sounds of English Option (D) is incorrect. Twenty-six letters with one keyword apiece implies a one-to-one match between letters and sounds. English phonemes outnumber letters, and most vowels are spelled several ways, so the poster misrepresents the system.","Posting the week's spelling words in the order in which they appear in the story":"Treating a weekly spelling list as a visual map of how sounds relate to their spellings Option (E) is incorrect. A weekly spelling list in story order is a memory aid for specific word forms. It presents no analysis of how sounds relate to the letters that spell them."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-foundational-076","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which activity primarily develops students' awareness of syllables in spoken words?","options":["Asking students to change the /k/ in cat to /h/ and say the new word","Asking students to clap once for each part they hear in the word elephant","Asking students to say which word in a set of three does not rhyme with cat","Asking students to name the first sound they hear in cat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the levels within phonological awareness. Syllables are the largest units below the whole word, and clapping one beat per part lets students feel the internal shape of a long word without requiring them to notice individual phonemes, which is a later and more demanding step.","dex":{"Asking students to change the /k/ in cat to /h/ and say the new word":"Treating phoneme substitution, the most advanced level, as a syllable-level task Option (D) is incorrect. Substitution requires manipulating individual phonemes while holding the rest of the word in memory, which is the most advanced level of phonemic awareness and far beyond syllable work.","Asking students to say which word in a set of three does not rhyme with cat":"Mistaking a rhyme oddity task for work on the internal structure of a single word Option (B) is incorrect. An odd-one-out rhyme task compares the endings of whole words. Attention goes to the rime shared by two of the words, not to how any single word is divided internally.","Asking students to name the first sound they hear in cat":"Confusing isolation of a single phoneme with awareness of syllables Option (C) is incorrect. Isolating the first sound is phoneme-level work, a finer grain than the syllable. A student who can do this has already moved past syllable awareness."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-077","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"A fourth-grade teacher notices that several students struggle to follow multi-step oral directions during science investigations. Which practice would best strengthen these students' receptive language?","options":["Asking students to write a summary of the investigation once they have finished","Making the teacher's explanations longer and more complex so that students build stamina","Asking students to listen to the directions and then tell a partner what they will do first, second, and third","Providing the directions in writing only, so students can reread them as they work"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how receptive language is strengthened. Listening is not passive absorption; asking students to hold a sequence in mind and then restate it in order forces active processing of what was heard. It also gives the teacher immediate evidence of where the sequence broke down, which is exactly what a written alternative cannot do.","dex":{"Asking students to write a summary of the investigation once they have finished":"Treating written summary produced after the fact as training in receptive language Option (C) is incorrect. Writing a summary afterward is an expressive task that asks students to produce language about meaning. It comes after comprehension has already happened and does not exercise the decoding of spoken input.","Making the teacher's explanations longer and more complex so that students build stamina":"Assuming that increasing the difficulty of oral input builds listening capacity Option (D) is incorrect. Longer and denser input overloads working memory for students who are already losing the sequence. Difficulty without support produces failure rather than growth.","Providing the directions in writing only, so students can reread them as they work":"Routing the task through print so that the listening skill needing practice is bypassed Option (B) is incorrect. Written directions route the task through reading and remove the listening practice these students need. Accommodating around a weak skill is different from developing it."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-078","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"During the first weeks of kindergarten, a teacher records which print-awareness behaviors each student can perform. Which two behaviors are concepts of print that the teacher should document? Select all that apply.","options":["Pointing to the front cover when asked where reading begins","Moving a finger under the words from left to right and returning to the left margin at the next line","Naming the sound that each letter of the alphabet makes","Blending three spoken sounds into a word","Retelling a story that was read aloud"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of what concepts of print cover. Knowing where a book begins is book-orientation knowledge, one of the earliest understandings children construct about how print works, and it can be observed directly during shared reading.  [B] The question requires an understanding of directionality and return sweep. Tracking print the right way and sweeping back to the left margin shows a child has internalized the spatial conventions of English print, which must be in place before spoken words can be matched to written ones.","dex":{"Naming the sound that each letter of the alphabet makes":"Classifying letter-sound knowledge as a concept about print Option (C) is incorrect. Producing letter sounds is alphabet knowledge, a separate strand. A child can supply a sound for every letter and still not know which end of a book to start with.","Blending three spoken sounds into a word":"Mistaking phonemic blending for an understanding of how books and pages work Option (D) is incorrect. Blending spoken sounds is phonemic awareness, an oral skill that is deliberately assessed without any print at hand. It says nothing about a child's understanding of books or pages.","Retelling a story that was read aloud":"Treating listening comprehension and retelling as print awareness Option (E) is incorrect. Retelling a read-aloud measures listening comprehension and narrative recall. The student is responding to meaning, not to how print is arranged or how a book is handled."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-foundational-079","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Why is oral phonemic segmentation considered a prerequisite for profiting from instruction in the alphabetic principle?","options":["Because students must know the names of the letters before they can learn the sounds of the letters","Because segmentation practice is what makes oral reading fluent","Because students cannot hear rhyme until they can segment words into phonemes","Because students must be able to hear the separate sounds in a word before a letter can be understood as standing for one of those sounds"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the relationship between phonemic awareness and the alphabetic principle. The alphabetic principle holds that letters map onto phonemes. For a child who does not yet perceive the separate sounds inside a spoken word, there is nothing for the letter to attach to; segmentation supplies the units that the letters then represent.","dex":{"Because students must know the names of the letters before they can learn the sounds of the letters":"Treating letter-name knowledge as the prerequisite for grasping the alphabetic principle Option (B) is incorrect. Letter names are convenient mnemonics for remembering sounds, but they are a separate strand of learning. The essential prerequisite is auditory, not the ability to recite the alphabet.","Because segmentation practice is what makes oral reading fluent":"Assigning phonemic segmentation the later role of producing fluent oral reading Option (C) is incorrect. Fluency develops through repeated reading of connected text and grows after decoding is underway. Segmentation supports the mapping of letters to sounds, which is a different contributor.","Because students cannot hear rhyme until they can segment words into phonemes":"Reversing the developmental order so that rhyme is made to depend on phoneme segmentation Option (D) is incorrect. The order is reversed: children recognize rhyme before they can segment phonemes. Rhyme is word-level and appears earlier, so it cannot depend on a later-developing skill."}},{"id":"8006-foundational-080","code":"8006","subtest":"Foundational Literacy Skills","q":"Which classroom practice makes the best use of a sound wall during daily phonics instruction?","options":["Having students produce the target phoneme and check their mouth position against the card before reading words that contain that sound","Having students copy every word on the sound wall into their notebooks each Monday","Using the sound wall as a display that students pass by during the day but never revisit","Having students put the sound cards back in alphabetical order at the end of each week"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how classroom materials become instructional tools. Referring students to the wall immediately before they read words with that sound ties the physical production of the phoneme to the letters they are about to decode, so the display does its work at the moment it can help instead of sitting behind the students as decoration.","dex":{"Having students copy every word on the sound wall into their notebooks each Monday":"Turning an oral reference tool into a transcription exercise Option (B) is incorrect. Copying turns an oral reference tool into a transcription exercise. The time goes into forming letters, which draws attention away from hearing and saying the sound.","Using the sound wall as a display that students pass by during the day but never revisit":"Assuming that posting a display is equivalent to teaching with it Option (C) is incorrect. A display that is never referred to during teaching is wallpaper. Materials influence learning through repeated, purposeful use in lessons, not through their presence in the room.","Having students put the sound cards back in alphabetical order at the end of each week":"Imposing the spelling system's alphabetical order on a wall organized by sound Option (D) is incorrect. Alphabetical order is the logic of the spelling system. Imposing it on a wall built around sounds undoes the very organization that makes the wall useful and suggests again that letters and sounds pair one to one."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-001","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade teacher is preparing students to read a science article about ecosystems. The article contains the words \"habitat,\" \"predator,\" and \"organism.\" Which instructional approach best supports students' comprehension of the article?","options":["Scheduling a vocabulary quiz on the words for the following week","Having students look up each word in a dictionary and copy the definition into a notebook","Preteaching the words with student-friendly definitions, examples, and non-examples before reading","Encouraging students to skip unfamiliar words and rely on context clues as they read"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to help students acquire common and domain-specific words through direct instruction. Domain-specific terms carry dense conceptual meaning, and preteaching them with accessible definitions plus examples and non-examples builds the background knowledge students need before they encounter the terms in text. Addressing them in advance means working memory during reading goes to comprehension rather than to decoding unfamiliar vocabulary.","dex":{"Scheduling a vocabulary quiz on the words for the following week":"Confusing assessment with instruction, as though scheduling a quiz were the same as teaching the words. A quiz is an assessment, and it is scheduled after the reading. It neither builds the knowledge needed to comprehend the article nor provides the instruction that would produce it.","Having students look up each word in a dictionary and copy the definition into a notebook":"Equating completion of a vocabulary task with construction of the concept. Copying a definition produces a page of writing, not a usable meaning. Copying dictionary definitions is a clerical task rather than concept building. Dictionary entries are often written above students' reading levels and give multiple senses without guidance about which one applies, so students can complete the task without being able to use the words while reading.","Encouraging students to skip unfamiliar words and rely on context clues as they read":"Treating context clues as a strategy that works under any conditions. Inference needs partial knowledge and redundant surrounding text, and clustered technical terms supply neither. Context clues work best when students already have partial knowledge of a word and surrounding text is rich enough to constrain its meaning. With three dense domain-specific terms appearing together, students lack both prerequisites, so skipping them leaves the central concepts of the article inaccessible."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-002","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A teacher notices that a student reads a grade-level passage word by word with little expression. Which instructional practice most directly addresses this student's need?","options":["Independent silent reading of progressively more challenging texts","Daily practice with phonics worksheets focusing on vowel patterns","Previewing the passage's vocabulary words before the student reads","Repeated reading of the same passage with feedback on phrasing and expression"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how prosody is developed. Word-by-word reading with flat expression is a prosody problem, and repeated reading of a short passage with targeted feedback on phrasing is a research-supported way to build both automaticity and expressive contour.","dex":{"Independent silent reading of progressively more challenging texts":"Assuming that more reading practice automatically improves fluency. Practice without feedback can consolidate an ineffective habit, and harder text works against automaticity. Independent silent reading generates no model and no feedback, so a student who reads word by word is likely to keep doing so. Raising text difficulty also lowers the accuracy and automaticity that fluency depends on.","Daily practice with phonics worksheets focusing on vowel patterns":"Reaching for phonics instruction to remedy any reading difficulty. Instructional responses should follow from what the evidence shows the student can already do. The student is producing the words, so decoding is not the bottleneck. Additional work on vowel patterns targets a skill the student has already demonstrated and leaves the phrasing problem untouched.","Previewing the passage's vocabulary words before the student reads":"Confusing a comprehension support with a fluency intervention. The two target different components of reading. Preteaching vocabulary removes meaning obstacles and supports comprehension, but this student's problem is one of delivery rather than of word meaning. Knowing every word would not by itself produce phrased, expressive reading."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-003","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A teacher is preparing third graders to read a science text that contains the words \"evaporate,\" \"condense,\" and \"precipitate.\" Which practice best supports their comprehension of the text?","options":["Having students copy the glossary definitions into their notebooks the day before reading","Providing direct instruction on the words with examples, student-generated examples, and discussion before reading","Telling students to rely on context clues to work out the words as they encounter them","Assigning the words as spelling homework for the week before the lesson"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how academic and domain-specific vocabulary is best taught. Explicit teaching that pairs a student-friendly definition with examples, student-generated examples, and discussion builds the concept rather than a verbal label, and preteaching frees attention for comprehension during reading.","dex":{"Having students copy the glossary definitions into their notebooks the day before reading":"Equating exposure plus transcription with learning. Reproduction of a definition is not evidence of conceptual understanding. Copying definitions is shallow contact with a word. It does not require students to connect the term to the concept, so they can finish the task and still be unable to use or recognize the idea in the text.","Telling students to rely on context clues to work out the words as they encounter them":"Overestimating context clues. Context is unreliable precisely for the technical terms that carry the main ideas of a science text. These three words are the concepts the text is built on, and informational text rarely supplies clues strong enough to derive them accurately. Guessing here produces misunderstanding of the very content being taught.","Assigning the words as spelling homework for the week before the lesson":"Treating word study as spelling study. Knowing how to write a word and knowing what it means are separate outcomes. Spelling practice builds orthographic memory, not conceptual knowledge. A student who can spell 'precipitate' may still not know what it means when it appears in the passage."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-004","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which statement best explains why vocabulary knowledge is essential to reading comprehension?","options":["Vocabulary instruction teaches students to use context clues effectively","Fluent readers decode automatically, which frees their attention to focus on word meanings","Comprehension is built from the meanings of words, so a reader who does not know the words cannot construct the meaning of the text","Students with larger vocabularies read more quickly, and greater speed produces better comprehension"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the relationship between vocabulary and comprehension. Meaning is constructed from the words on the page, so when word meanings are missing, comprehension breaks down at the word level before larger structures such as sentences or the passage as a whole can be built.","dex":{"Vocabulary instruction teaches students to use context clues effectively":"Identifying a secondary benefit of instruction as the underlying rationale for the construct itself. Using context clues is one useful strategy for handling unknown words, but it is a by-product of vocabulary work rather than the reason vocabulary matters. The core contribution of vocabulary is direct access to meaning.","Fluent readers decode automatically, which frees their attention to focus on word meanings":"Substituting a true statement about fluency for an explanation of vocabulary. Being correct in itself is not the same as being responsive to the question. This statement is true, but it explains why automatic decoding supports comprehension, not why vocabulary is necessary. It answers a different question than the one asked.","Students with larger vocabularies read more quickly, and greater speed produces better comprehension":"Treating reading speed as the mechanism that produces comprehension. Speed and comprehension are related but distinct outcomes. Rate is a marker of fluency, not a cause of understanding. A reader with a strong vocabulary who reads slowly can still comprehend well, and a fast reader who does not know the words will not."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-005","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A teacher wants to promote vocabulary development through direct instruction. Which activity best reflects that approach?","options":["Asking students to illustrate each new word in a personal picture dictionary","Posting the week's words on the word wall and quizzing students on Friday","Having students read widely on their own and look up any unfamiliar words they meet","Explicitly teaching word meanings with student-friendly definitions, examples, and multiple exposures across contexts"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of what distinguishes direct vocabulary instruction. Student-friendly definitions, worked examples, and repeated meaningful encounters across contexts are the three hallmarks of direct instruction, and together they move a word from unfamiliar to usable.","dex":{"Asking students to illustrate each new word in a personal picture dictionary":"Assuming that producing something for a word proves the meaning was taught. Drawing 'evidence' or 'compare' does not establish the concept. Illustrating works for concrete nouns and fails for the abstract academic words that most affect comprehension. It is also a production task that comes after learning, not a way of teaching meaning.","Posting the week's words on the word wall and quizzing students on Friday":"Treating display and testing as instruction. Without explicit teaching of meaning, exposure alone produces weak and slow growth. A word wall and a Friday quiz provide exposure and measurement but no explicit explanation of meaning. Students may recognize the words by Friday and still be unable to use any of them.","Having students read widely on their own and look up any unfamiliar words they meet":"Confusing incidental learning conditions with direct instruction. Indirect learning is valuable but insufficient on its own, particularly for struggling readers. Wide independent reading and self-directed lookup are valuable complements, but they are indirect routes to word learning. Students who most need vocabulary support are least likely to look words up accurately on their own."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-006","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A teacher wants students to be able to work out the meaning of an unfamiliar word they meet while reading. Which instructional practice best develops that ability?","options":["Modeling how to examine the surrounding words and sentences, the word's syntactic role, and its word parts","Encouraging students to skip unfamiliar words and keep reading for the main idea","Having students memorize a list of the text's difficult words before they read","Teaching students to consult the glossary first and use context only as a backup"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how word-solving strategies are taught. Deriving meaning draws on three sources at once: the semantic context in nearby words and sentences, the syntactic role the word plays, and morphological information from roots and affixes. Modeling all three teaches a procedure rather than an answer.","dex":{"Encouraging students to skip unfamiliar words and keep reading for the main idea":"Confusing a coping behavior with a comprehension strategy. Getting past a word is not the same as getting its meaning. Skipping unknown words is avoidance, not inference. Missed words accumulate, and each gap weakens the reader's model of the text, so comprehension erodes as the passage goes on.","Having students memorize a list of the text's difficult words before they read":"Mistaking coverage of a specific text's vocabulary for development of a transferable skill. Preteaching and memorizing the difficult words in one text helps with that text only. It gives students nothing to use when they meet an untaught word, which is the situation the question describes.","Teaching students to consult the glossary first and use context only as a backup":"Preferring an answer source over a strategy. The goal is for students to handle words nobody pre-selected for them. A glossary supplies the meaning of listed words but teaches no method, and it is useless for the many unlisted words a student will meet. Treating it as the first resort makes students dependent on it."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-007","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A second grader reads a grade-level passage accurately but slowly, one word at a time, pausing at the end of every line. Which statement best explains how this reading behavior is likely to affect comprehension?","options":["Accuracy guarantees that meaning is constructed, so comprehension is unlikely to be affected.","Effort spent identifying individual words leaves less attention available for constructing meaning from the text.","The behavior lowers comprehension only when the passage contains unfamiliar vocabulary.","Comprehension depends on the reader's background knowledge rather than on how the words are read."],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how fluency contributes to comprehension. When word recognition is not yet automatic, attention is consumed by identifying one word after another, so the beginning of the sentence is often lost before the end is reached. Accurate but labored reading therefore still leaves the reader with too little capacity left to build the meaning of the sentence and the passage.","dex":{"Accuracy guarantees that meaning is constructed, so comprehension is unlikely to be affected.":"Treating accuracy and fluency as the same thing, with rate and automaticity as optional extras Option (A) is incorrect. Accurate decoding is one component of fluent reading, but on its own it does not free attention for meaning; a reader can identify every word correctly and still fail to hold the sentence together. This option reflects the common misconception that accuracy and fluency are the same thing, with rate and automaticity treated as optional extras.","The behavior lowers comprehension only when the passage contains unfamiliar vocabulary.":"Assuming fluency only matters in difficult texts, so that word-by-word reading of familiar material needs no attention Option (C) is incorrect. Unfamiliar vocabulary is one source of comprehension difficulty, but labored word recognition limits comprehension on its own, even in texts whose words are all familiar to the reader. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency only matters in hard texts, so a student who reads familiar material word by word is seen as having no problem to address.","Comprehension depends on the reader's background knowledge rather than on how the words are read.":"Assuming background knowledge can substitute for the mechanics of reading Option (D) is incorrect. Background knowledge does support comprehension, but it works alongside fluent word recognition rather than in place of it; a knowledgeable reader who must decode every word still loses the thread of the sentence. This option reflects the common misconception that background knowledge can substitute for the mechanics of reading."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-008","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade teacher wants students to read with appropriate expression, pausing at commas and raising pitch at question marks. Which instructional routine best develops this aspect of oral reading?","options":["Having students complete daily phoneme segmentation activities with counters","Timing students for one minute on a passage and graphing their words-correct-per-minute scores","Having students chorally read a short poem that the teacher has marked for phrasing and stress, then reread it with a partner","Having students practice reading lists of words that contain the week's vowel pattern"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach students to read orally with prosody. Marking the text makes phrase boundaries and stress visible, choral reading supplies a fluent model to imitate, and partner rereading gives students several attempts at producing the same phrasing. The target of the routine is expression, not speed.","dex":{"Having students complete daily phoneme segmentation activities with counters":"Assuming any foundational routine improves oral reading, without matching the routine to the level of the difficulty Option (D) is incorrect. Segmenting phonemes with counters is phonemic awareness work that operates on spoken words, well below the level of reading sentences aloud. This option reflects the common misconception that any foundational routine will improve oral reading, without matching the routine to the level of the difficulty.","Timing students for one minute on a passage and graphing their words-correct-per-minute scores":"Treating rate as the whole of fluency, so that raising speed is assumed to produce expression Option (A) is incorrect. Timing and graphing measure how many words a student reads correctly in a minute; they do not teach a student where to pause or how to vary pitch. This option reflects the common misconception that rate is the whole of fluency, so raising speed is assumed to produce expression.","Having students practice reading lists of words that contain the week's vowel pattern":"Assuming word-level decoding practice transfers on its own to expressive reading of connected text Option (C) is incorrect. Reading word lists that follow a pattern builds accuracy with specific spellings, but the words are read one at a time with no phrase boundaries to observe. This option reflects the common misconception that word-level decoding practice transfers on its own to expressive reading of connected text."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-009","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"After the flood, the basement was uninhabitable.\n\nA fourth-grade teacher asks students to determine the literal meaning of \"uninhabitable\" in the sentence above. Which teacher move best combines syntactic and morphological analysis?","options":["Asking students what part of speech the word is in the sentence, then what the base \"inhabit\" and the suffix \"-able\" contribute to its meaning","Asking students to reread the sentence and tell how they think the family felt about the basement","Asking students to list other words they know that begin with the letters \"un\"","Asking students to substitute a familiar word and check whether the sentence still makes sense"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how syntax and morphology work together to establish a word's literal meaning. Recognizing the word as a predicate adjective tells students that it describes the basement, and analyzing un- + inhabit + -able shows that the word means \"not able to be lived in.\" The two sources of information converge on the same literal meaning instead of leaving students with a vague impression.","dex":{"Asking students to reread the sentence and tell how they think the family felt about the basement":"Treating the inference of feeling or tone as the same task as determining a word's literal meaning Option (B) is incorrect. Asking how the family felt directs students toward tone, connotation, or inference about characters, none of which is the literal meaning of the word. This option reflects the common misconception that inferring a feeling from a text is the same task as determining what an unfamiliar word denotes.","Asking students to list other words they know that begin with the letters \"un\"":"Treating recognition of a familiar affix as complete morphological analysis Option (C) is incorrect. Listing other words that begin with the same letters draws attention to one prefix while leaving the base word and the suffix unexamined, and it never connects the word to its role in the sentence. This option reflects the common misconception that spotting a familiar affix counts as complete morphological analysis.","Asking students to substitute a familiar word and check whether the sentence still makes sense":"Assuming that a substitution which still sounds right proves the literal meaning has been established Option (D) is incorrect. Substituting a familiar word can approximate meaning when the surrounding text supplies strong clues, but it bypasses the structural analysis that lets students work out words in sparse contexts, and a substitution that merely sounds plausible is not evidence of the literal meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that a sentence that still sounds right proves the meaning has been established."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-010","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A reading specialist is designing fluency instruction for a second grader who reads accurately but laboriously. Which set of activities best addresses the skills that support fluent reading at the phoneme, word, and passage levels?","options":["Echo reading of pattern books, choral reading of poems, and partner retelling of stories","Word sorts by onset and rime, weekly spelling tests, and written responses to literature","Vocabulary previews, silent independent reading, and comprehension strategy lessons","Phoneme blending drills, timed practice with high-frequency word lists, and repeated oral reading of short decodable passages"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach strategies that support fluent reading at each level of text. Phoneme blending makes word recognition efficient enough to support accuracy, timed word-list practice builds the automatic retrieval that raises rate, and repeated reading of connected text lets students integrate those words into phrases. Leaving out any one level means the others are practiced without the support they need.","dex":{"Echo reading of pattern books, choral reading of poems, and partner retelling of stories":"Assuming repeated reading of connected text is sufficient fluency instruction at every level Option (B) is incorrect. Echo reading, choral reading, and retelling are passage-level routines; none of them gives the student systematic work at the phoneme or word level, where word recognition is still effortful. This option reflects the common misconception that reading connected text repeatedly is sufficient fluency instruction at every level.","Word sorts by onset and rime, weekly spelling tests, and written responses to literature":"Treating word study routines such as sorting as interchangeable with fluency practice Option (C) is incorrect. Word sorts and spelling tests build orthographic and encoding knowledge, and written responses address comprehension, but none of these asks students to read connected text fluently. This option reflects the common misconception that word study routines such as sorting are interchangeable with fluency practice.","Vocabulary previews, silent independent reading, and comprehension strategy lessons":"Assuming that more time with text in any form will produce fluency Option (D) is incorrect. Vocabulary and comprehension instruction support meaning but do not build the automaticity this student lacks, and silent reading gives the teacher no evidence of how the text is actually being read. This option reflects the common misconception that more time with text, in any form, will produce fluency."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-011","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fifth-grade teacher notices that many students write long run-on sentences in their science reports. Which instructional approach best uses written language structure to address the problem?","options":["Providing daily sentence-combining practice using mentor sentences taken from the class read-aloud","Assigning grammar worksheets that require students to label parts of speech in isolated sentences","Requiring students to recopy a published paragraph in their best handwriting each morning","Having students memorize the definition of a complete sentence and write it out weekly"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach written language structure in the context of real writing. Mentor sentences give students models of mature syntax, and combining short sentences into longer ones forces them to decide where one clause ends and the next begins and which mark signals that boundary. The work happens inside meaningful text rather than in isolation, so it transfers to their own writing.","dex":{"Assigning grammar worksheets that require students to label parts of speech in isolated sentences":"Assuming that knowing grammar terminology produces control of syntax in one's own writing Option (B) is incorrect. Labeling parts of speech in sentences someone else wrote is identification practice; it does not require students to make the boundary decisions that produce a run-on in the first place. This option reflects the common misconception that knowing grammar terminology produces control of syntax in one's own writing.","Requiring students to recopy a published paragraph in their best handwriting each morning":"Assuming written language conventions are learned through transcription Option (C) is incorrect. Copying text addresses handwriting and attention to printed form, and because the model is already punctuated correctly, it asks students to make no decisions about sentence boundaries. This option reflects the common misconception that written language conventions are learned through transcription.","Having students memorize the definition of a complete sentence and write it out weekly":"Treating verbal recall of a rule as the same as applying that rule while composing Option (D) is incorrect. A student can state the definition of a complete sentence and still not apply it while composing, because the difficulty lies in applying the concept under the demands of writing. This option reflects the common misconception that verbal recall of a rule is the same as control of that rule in performance."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-012","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade teacher wants a quick, informal check on whether the English learners in the class understand the key vocabulary of a science unit the class is about to begin. Which approach best serves this purpose?","options":["Administering a standardized, norm-referenced vocabulary assessment to the whole class","Asking each student to use a target word in a sentence, act it out, or point to a picture that shows it","Recording how many words each student reads correctly in one minute from a grade-level passage","Averaging the class scores on the multiple-choice quiz given at the end of the unit"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of informal assessment that accounts for students' varied experience and language. Allowing a response in words, gesture, or images separates what a student knows about a concept from how much English the student can produce, which is exactly the distinction that matters before instruction begins. The check is also quick enough to change tomorrow's lesson.","dex":{"Administering a standardized, norm-referenced vocabulary assessment to the whole class":"Assuming a more standardized instrument is automatically a more useful one for a quick classroom check Option (A) is incorrect. A standardized assessment is a formal measure that returns scores on a delay, and its language load often understates what English learners know about the underlying concepts. This option reflects the common misconception that a more standardized instrument is automatically a more useful one.","Recording how many words each student reads correctly in one minute from a grade-level passage":"Using oral reading fluency data as a proxy for vocabulary knowledge Option (C) is incorrect. Words correct per minute measures how automatically a student reads printed words, not whether the student knows what those words mean; a student can read a term aloud perfectly and have no idea what it denotes. This option reflects the common misconception that oral reading fluency data serve as a proxy for vocabulary knowledge.","Averaging the class scores on the multiple-choice quiz given at the end of the unit":"Assuming any vocabulary score can serve as information for planning instruction Option (D) is incorrect. An end-of-unit quiz is summative; its results arrive after the unit is taught and can no longer shape the instruction students needed. This option reflects the common misconception that any score on a vocabulary task functions as information for planning."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-013","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Ms. Alvarez listens to a first grader read aloud from a leveled book. The student pauses to sound out several unfamiliar words, self-corrects most of those attempts, reads high-frequency words such as \"said\" and \"they\" automatically, and reads the characters' dialogue in the same flat tone as the narration. Which instructional focus is best supported by this observation?","options":["A phonological awareness screening before any further oral reading work","Additional decoding practice with the vowel patterns that appeared in the student's miscues","Explicit instruction in phrasing and expression, using teacher modeling of dialogue and short, predictable texts","Sight-word instruction for the high-frequency words the student has not yet mastered"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to evaluate an oral reading sample to identify an instructional focus. The student's self-corrections show that decoding strategies are working, and automatic reading of high-frequency words shows that word recognition is developing normally; the one need the sample reveals is prosody. Modeling dialogue and practicing with predictable text lets the student rehearse phrasing on material light enough to free attention for expression.","dex":{"A phonological awareness screening before any further oral reading work":"Assuming difficulty in one area of reading signals a gap in the earliest foundational skills Option (D) is incorrect. A student who sounds out words and self-corrects has already moved well past the phonological awareness skills a screening would test, and pausing reading altogether would remove the practice the student actually needs. This option reflects the common misconception that difficulty in one area of reading signals a gap in the earliest foundational skills.","Additional decoding practice with the vowel patterns that appeared in the student's miscues":"Treating every miscue as a deficit that requires reteaching the pattern Option (A) is incorrect. The student worked through those words and corrected them without help, which is evidence that decoding is being monitored and is improving, not that it has failed. This option reflects the common misconception that any miscue is a deficit requiring reteaching of the pattern.","Sight-word instruction for the high-frequency words the student has not yet mastered":"Assuming high-frequency word instruction is a safe default response to any oral reading difficulty Option (C) is incorrect. The high-frequency words named in the observation are already read automatically, so instruction there would target a strength rather than a need. This option reflects the common misconception that high-frequency word instruction is a safe default response to any oral reading difficulty."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-014","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Before a fifth-grade class reads a chapter about ecosystems, the teacher preteaches the words \"compete,\" \"limited,\" \"stable,\" \"habitat,\" \"food chain,\" and \"energy.\" Which statement best explains the value of this preteaching?","options":["It guarantees that students will spell the unit's vocabulary correctly in their science writing.","It substitutes for the background knowledge that students from varied backgrounds may not yet have.","It removes any need for students to use context clues when they meet unfamiliar words later.","It makes key word meanings available in advance, so attention during reading can go to the ideas those words carry."],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of why preteaching vocabulary supports comprehension. When the meaning of a key word is already secure, the reader does not have to stop and work it out, so the word can do its job as a carrier of the text's ideas instead of becoming an obstacle to them. Preteaching is most valuable for the words the text depends on.","dex":{"It guarantees that students will spell the unit's vocabulary correctly in their science writing.":"Judging vocabulary work by the accuracy of the written form rather than by knowledge of meaning Option (C) is incorrect. Spelling is a separate outcome that requires its own practice, and a word can be spelled correctly while its meaning remains unclear. This option reflects the common misconception that vocabulary work should be judged by the accuracy of the written form.","It substitutes for the background knowledge that students from varied backgrounds may not yet have.":"Treating vocabulary instruction as a substitute for background knowledge Option (D) is incorrect. Vocabulary and background knowledge complement each other; a list of definitions cannot supply the experiences, concepts, and discourse that students from varied backgrounds may be missing. This option reflects the common misconception that one can stand in for the other.","It removes any need for students to use context clues when they meet unfamiliar words later.":"Assuming a pretaught word list makes strategy instruction unnecessary Option (A) is incorrect. Preteaching covers only the handful of words the teacher selects, and students will keep meeting unfamiliar words for the rest of their reading lives. This option reflects the common misconception that a pretaught list makes strategy instruction unnecessary."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-015","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fourth-grade teacher explains to colleagues why fluent reading matters for comprehension. Which statements accurately describe how fluency and vocabulary support comprehension? Select all that apply.","options":["Automatic word recognition frees attention that can then be used to construct meaning.","Phrasing and pausing during oral reading show whether a reader is grouping words into meaningful units.","A reader's rate, measured in words correct per minute, is the single strongest predictor of how well that reader will comprehend a passage.","Fluency and comprehension develop independently of each other, so instruction in one has no effect on the other."],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how fluency supports comprehension. When words are recognized without effort, the attention that would have gone to identifying them becomes available for holding ideas and connecting them across sentences, which is where comprehension actually happens.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how fluency supports comprehension. A reader who pauses at clause boundaries and varies pitch is demonstrating that the print has been parsed into syntactic units, and that same parsing is what turns a string of words into a meaningful sentence.","dex":{"A reader's rate, measured in words correct per minute, is the single strongest predictor of how well that reader will comprehend a passage.":"Treating a correlation with comprehension as making rate its cause and its best single indicator Option (C) is incorrect. Rate correlates with comprehension, but a fast reader who skips words or ignores meaning can comprehend poorly, while a deliberate reader can comprehend well. This option reflects the common misconception that a correlation makes rate the cause of comprehension and the best single indicator of it.","Fluency and comprehension develop independently of each other, so instruction in one has no effect on the other.":"Assuming fluency and comprehension develop on separate tracks with no reciprocal influence Option (D) is incorrect. The two develop in a reciprocal relationship: easier word recognition leaves more capacity for meaning, and stronger comprehension supports phrasing and word solving. This option reflects the common misconception that the strands of reading development run on separate tracks."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-fluency-016","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A first grader reads slowly but accurately, sounding out almost every word. Which instructional routine best addresses this student's needs?","options":["Providing daily repeated reading of short texts at the student's independent level, with brief teacher feedback","Adding more time for sounding out unfamiliar words in isolation from word cards","Moving the student to harder texts to build reading stamina","Assigning a longer list of decodable words to practice at home each night"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach reading that is both accurate and efficient. Rereading text the student can already handle with high accuracy gives the same words several exposures in meaningful context, so recognition becomes automatic instead of being rebuilt sound by sound each time, and the teacher's feedback keeps errors from being practiced.","dex":{"Adding more time for sounding out unfamiliar words in isolation from word cards":"Assuming more of the practice that produced accuracy will also produce fluency Option (B) is incorrect. Practicing words one at a time on cards exercises decoding in isolation and rarely transfers to the pace and phrasing required by connected text. This option reflects the common misconception that more of the practice that produced accuracy will also produce fluency.","Moving the student to harder texts to build reading stamina":"Assuming more challenging material is always the appropriate next step Option (C) is incorrect. Harder text pushes the student back into word-by-word decoding and erodes the accuracy that is already present, which is the opposite of what is needed. This option reflects the common misconception that more challenging material is always the appropriate next step.","Assigning a longer list of decodable words to practice at home each night":"Assuming quantity of practice substitutes for the quality of the instructional routine Option (D) is incorrect. A longer homework list increases the volume of practice without adding the modeling, feedback, and rereading that make practice productive. This option reflects the common misconception that quantity of practice substitutes for the quality of the instructional routine."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-017","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"The biologist needed a specimen, so she collected one from the marsh and carried it back to the laboratory.\n\nA student reading the sentence above does not know what \"specimen\" means. Which teacher question best uses the sentence's syntax and context to establish the word's literal meaning?","options":["How should the sentence be read aloud to show how the biologist felt?","What does the word \"one\" refer to, and what does the sentence say the biologist did with it?","What is the Latin root of \"specimen,\" and what did that root originally mean?","Which meaning of \"specimen\" is listed first in the dictionary?"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how syntax and context establish a literal meaning. The pronoun \"one\" stands in for \"specimen,\" which tells students the word names a thing that can be gathered, and the verbs \"collected\" and \"carried\" together with the marsh setting narrow that thing to a sample taken away for study. The inference is drawn from the sentence itself rather than from an outside source.","dex":{"How should the sentence be read aloud to show how the biologist felt?":"Treating expressive oral reading as a route to word meaning Option (D) is incorrect. Reading aloud with feeling addresses prosody and the character's attitude, neither of which identifies what the unknown word denotes. This option reflects the common misconception that expressive reading is itself a route to word meaning.","What is the Latin root of \"specimen,\" and what did that root originally mean?":"Treating etymology as a replacement for contextual analysis Option (B) is incorrect. Root study can support word learning, but the question here is which meaning this sentence uses, and knowing the history of a root does not tell a student which sense applies in context. This option reflects the common misconception that etymology can replace contextual analysis.","Which meaning of \"specimen\" is listed first in the dictionary?":"Assuming a dictionary's first listed definition is the sense the author intended Option (C) is incorrect. Dictionary senses are ordered by convention, not by which one fits the passage, and taking the first entry skips the step of testing the meaning against the sentence. This option reflects the common misconception that a dictionary's first definition is the one the author intended."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-018","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Several second graders read multisyllabic words one syllable at a time, which breaks the flow of their oral reading. Which strategy best supports fluent reading at the word level?","options":["Providing phoneme segmentation practice with counters during small-group time","Teaching students to preview the text and set a purpose before reading","Providing daily practice reading high-frequency words and common word parts until they are recognized at a single glance","Having the class chorally read a poem once each morning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach word-level strategies that support fluent reading. When frequent words and common syllables are retrieved as whole units instead of assembled piece by piece, the reader's attention stays with the sentence, and the stopping and starting that fragments phrasing disappears.","dex":{"Providing phoneme segmentation practice with counters during small-group time":"Assuming a lower-level routine is the safer choice whenever reading sounds labored Option (C) is incorrect. Students who can sound out multisyllabic words have already demonstrated the segmentation that counters would rehearse, so the difficulty is retrieval speed rather than phonemic analysis. This option reflects the common misconception that a lower-level routine is the safer choice whenever reading sounds labored.","Teaching students to preview the text and set a purpose before reading":"Assuming prereading habits can compensate for slow word recognition during reading Option (D) is incorrect. Previewing and setting a purpose are comprehension routines that help readers plan their reading; they do nothing to speed up the recognition of the words on the page. This option reflects the common misconception that good reading habits before reading can compensate for slow word recognition during it.","Having the class chorally read a poem once each morning":"Assuming any supported reading of connected text builds the underlying word recognition Option (B) is incorrect. Choral reading of a poem gives whole-text practice but offers no focused work on the words and word parts the students are assembling slowly, so the source of the choppiness is never addressed. This option reflects the common misconception that any supported reading of connected text builds the underlying word recognition."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-019","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A first grader writes the following in a writing journal: \"i lik to play with my dog. he is big and brown and he can run fast\"\n\nMs. Chen uses the writing sample above to place the student on a developmental writing continuum. The sample shows control of simple sentence structure and phonetically plausible spellings of unfamiliar words, but inconsistent capitalization and no end punctuation. Which classification is best supported by this evidence?","options":["A fluent writer who is ready to work on varied sentence structure and precise word choice","A student whose writing suggests a language disorder and who should be referred for evaluation","A prephonemic writer who needs phonemic awareness instruction before any composing","A developing writer who has command of simple sentence structure and is ready for instruction in sentence-level conventions"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to classify a student's level of literacy development from a writing sample. Two connected sentences and phonetically plausible spellings show that the student has moved well past single words and is using letters to represent the sounds in words. The missing capitalization and end punctuation are the conventions that come next for a writer at this stage, so instruction should target sentence-level conventions while the message remains the priority.","dex":{"A fluent writer who is ready to work on varied sentence structure and precise word choice":"Assuming a writer who communicates a clear message is ready for stylistic revision Option (C) is incorrect. The sample contains two simple sentences with no variation and no end punctuation, which is well short of the control a fluent writer shows; asking this student to revise for craft would skip the conventions still missing. This option reflects the common misconception that a writer who communicates a clear message is ready for stylistic work.","A student whose writing suggests a language disorder and who should be referred for evaluation":"Treating developmental features of early writing as signs of a language disorder Option (D) is incorrect. The features in the sample are ordinary characteristics of a first grader at this point in development, and a single writing sample is not evidence on which to base a referral. This option reflects the common misconception that developmental patterns in early writing signal a disorder.","A prephonemic writer who needs phonemic awareness instruction before any composing":"Treating any invented spelling as evidence of the earliest stage of writing development Option (A) is incorrect. Spellings such as \"lik\" represent every essential sound in the word, which is exactly what a writer in a prephonemic stage cannot yet do. This option reflects the common misconception that any invented spelling indicates the earliest stage of writing development."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-020","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade teacher wants a complete picture of each student's fluency development, including students with disabilities and students who are learning English. Which assessment practices best serve that purpose? Select all that apply.","options":["Administering curriculum-based oral reading fluency probes several times during the year and graphing each student's growth","Taking running records during small-group instruction to note accuracy, self-correction, and phrasing","Using one standardized end-of-year reading score as the sole measure of each student's fluency","Collecting a single words-correct-per-minute score in September and using it to set instructional groups for the rest of the year","Asking students to rate their own reading confidence weekly and using those ratings in place of reading data"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of formal fluency assessment used for progress monitoring. Repeated probes on comparable passages show growth over time rather than a single snapshot, and graphing each student's own trend lets a teacher see whether instruction is working for that student, which matters when students enter with very different starting points.  [B] The question requires an understanding of informal fluency assessment gathered during instruction. A running record captures what a timed score cannot: which cues the student uses, whether errors are self-corrected, and how the text is phrased. This qualitative evidence is especially useful for students whose scores may be depressed by language load or by a disability that affects rate but not comprehension.","dex":{"Using one standardized end-of-year reading score as the sole measure of each student's fluency":"Treating a single broad standardized score as a fuller picture of an individual student's fluency Option (C) is incorrect. One global score cannot distinguish weak word recognition from weak language comprehension, and it arrives too late to change instruction for that student. This option reflects the common misconception that a broader, more standardized score is a fuller picture of an individual's fluency.","Collecting a single words-correct-per-minute score in September and using it to set instructional groups for the rest of the year":"Treating fluency as a fixed attribute that one measurement can establish Option (D) is incorrect. A single early score reflects one passage on one day and says nothing about how quickly a student is progressing, so groups set from it quickly become inaccurate. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency is a fixed attribute that can be measured once and filed.","Asking students to rate their own reading confidence weekly and using those ratings in place of reading data":"Treating learner self-perception as interchangeable with measured reading performance Option (E) is incorrect. Self-ratings describe how students feel about reading; they do not show how accurately or fluently students actually read, and substituting them for reading data removes the evidence instruction depends on. This option reflects the common misconception that learner perception is interchangeable with measured performance."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-fluency-021","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"During a unit on the water cycle, a fourth-grade teacher wants students to learn the domain-specific words \"evaporation,\" \"condensation,\" and \"precipitation.\" Which plan best combines direct instruction with independent learning?","options":["Explaining each term with examples and non-examples, then having students independently build a word map with a student-friendly definition, a sketch, and one use of the word","Writing the terms on a chart and having students copy the definitions into their notebooks","Telling students to look up any words they do not know while they read the chapter","Posting a list of the terms and telling students they will be quizzed on Friday"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how direct instruction and independent learning work together to build vocabulary. The teacher's explanation with examples and non-examples establishes the boundaries of each concept, and the word map then requires each student to process the word again alone, linking a definition, an image, and a use. Domain-specific terms need both the teacher's framing and the student's own processing.","dex":{"Writing the terms on a chart and having students copy the definitions into their notebooks":"Assuming exposure to a definition produces knowledge of a word Option (B) is incorrect. Copying definitions moves the words from one surface to another without requiring students to work out what they mean, so the terms remain unconnected to the concepts. This option reflects the common misconception that exposure to a definition produces knowledge of a word.","Telling students to look up any words they do not know while they read the chapter":"Assuming domain-specific vocabulary is picked up incidentally through reading without direct teaching Option (C) is incorrect. Leaving students to look words up on their own assumes they will notice the unknown word, have a usable source, and process what they find, none of which can be counted on. This option reflects the common misconception that vocabulary is picked up incidentally through reading and needs no direct teaching.","Posting a list of the terms and telling students they will be quizzed on Friday":"Assuming the prospect of a test can stand in for teaching word meanings Option (D) is incorrect. Announcing a quiz creates a reason to study but provides no instruction on what the words mean, leaving students to invent meanings or memorize strings of letters. This option reflects the common misconception that the prospect of a test can stand in for teaching."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-022","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fifth-grade teacher has fifteen minutes before the class reads a new selection and must decide how to use that time. Which choice best reflects research on vocabulary and comprehension?","options":["Having students memorize and recite dictionary definitions for the ten hardest words","Preteaching three key words in depth, using examples, non-examples, and discussion","Preteaching every unfamiliar word in the selection with a brief dictionary definition","Skipping preteaching so students can infer the meanings from context as they read"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to implement strategies that extend students' academic vocabulary. Depth of processing is what makes a word available during reading: examples and non-examples fix the boundaries of the concept, and discussion ties the word to what students already know. Three words learned well will carry the passage; ten words named once will not.","dex":{"Having students memorize and recite dictionary definitions for the ten hardest words":"Treating accurate recall of a definition as the goal of vocabulary instruction Option (D) is incorrect. Reciting a definition shows that the wording was memorized, not that the meaning can be applied when the word appears in a sentence. This option reflects the common misconception that accurate recall of a definition is the goal of vocabulary instruction.","Preteaching every unfamiliar word in the selection with a brief dictionary definition":"Equating the number of words covered with the amount of vocabulary learned Option (B) is incorrect. Hearing a brief definition once gives the student little to retrieve later, so the word is unlikely to be available at the moment it is needed in the text. This option reflects the common misconception that the number of words covered equals the amount of vocabulary learned.","Skipping preteaching so students can infer the meanings from context as they read":"Assuming wide reading alone builds the vocabulary comprehension requires Option (C) is incorrect. Context is often too thin to determine a meaning, and a student who lacks the word usually lacks the comprehension that would make the inference possible. This option reflects the common misconception that wide reading alone builds the vocabulary comprehension requires."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-023","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fifth grader reads a science passage quickly, accurately, and with good expression, but cannot answer questions about what the passage means. Which conclusion is best supported by this pattern?","options":["The student needs repeated reading practice to raise the reading rate further","The student simply lacks background knowledge, so vocabulary and language work would add nothing","Word recognition is working, so the limiting factor is likely on the meaning side, and the student needs support with word meanings and with the ideas the text presents","Decoding is the student's primary weakness and should be retaught from the beginning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how fluency and vocabulary together support comprehension. Accurate, well-paced, expressive reading shows that word recognition is no longer consuming attention. When comprehension still fails, the constraint lies on the meaning side: the meanings of the words, the syntax that carries the relations, or the concepts behind them. That is where instruction should go.","dex":{"The student needs repeated reading practice to raise the reading rate further":"Assuming each further gain in reading rate produces a gain in understanding Option (C) is incorrect. The student's rate is already adequate, and reading faster does not attach meanings to words the student does not know. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency can be improved indefinitely and that each gain in rate produces a gain in understanding.","The student simply lacks background knowledge, so vocabulary and language work would add nothing":"Treating limited background knowledge as a complete explanation that rules out vocabulary and language instruction Option (D) is incorrect. Limited background knowledge may well contribute, but it does not rule out vocabulary and language comprehension needs, and the two are typically addressed together rather than one being discarded. This option reflects the common misconception that background knowledge is a complete explanation that makes other instruction unnecessary.","Decoding is the student's primary weakness and should be retaught from the beginning":"Attributing any comprehension failure to decoding Option (B) is incorrect. The student reads accurately at a good pace, which is direct evidence that decoding is not the obstacle; reteaching it would spend time on a strength. This option reflects the common misconception that any comprehension failure traces back to decoding."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-024","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A second-grade teacher wants students to become more accurate when they read aloud. Which instructional practice best develops that accuracy?","options":["Timing students for one minute on a passage and graphing the number of words read correctly","Increasing the amount of time students spend reading silently each day","Supplying the word immediately whenever a student hesitates for more than a second","Teaching students to check whether what they read makes sense, sounds right, and looks right, and to go back and self-correct"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach students to read orally with accuracy. The three checks give students a concrete way to notice that something is wrong, and the return-and-correct step turns that noticing into a repair. Accuracy improves because students are monitoring their own reading instead of waiting to be told a word was missed.","dex":{"Timing students for one minute on a passage and graphing the number of words read correctly":"Treating the measurement of a skill as a way of teaching it Option (B) is incorrect. Timing and graphing produce evidence about how many words a student reads correctly; they do not teach a student what to do when a word does not match. This option reflects the common misconception that measuring a skill is a way of teaching it.","Increasing the amount of time students spend reading silently each day":"Assuming additional silent reading time improves oral reading accuracy Option (C) is incorrect. Silent reading builds reading volume and stamina, but it gives no feedback on the words a student says aloud and no practice in catching one's own errors. This option reflects the common misconception that any additional reading time improves oral reading accuracy.","Supplying the word immediately whenever a student hesitates for more than a second":"Treating the removal of hesitation as the same as building accuracy Option (D) is incorrect. Supplying the word removes the student's chance to detect and solve the mismatch, so the error is never noticed and the same difficulty will recur. This option reflects the common misconception that keeping reading moving smoothly is the same as building accuracy."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-025","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which sequence of steps should a teacher model when showing students how word parts and sentence context work together to yield a meaning?","options":["Having students look up the word in a dictionary, copy the definition, and illustrate it","Having students identify the base word and any affixes, predict a meaning, and then check that prediction against the sentence the word appears in","Having students read the sentence aloud several times and choose the meaning that sounds most natural","Having students divide the word into syllables, read each syllable, and blend them to pronounce the word"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how morphological analysis and context work together. Separating a word into a base and its affixes produces a candidate meaning, and the surrounding sentence is then used to confirm, narrow, or reject that candidate. Teaching the two steps in sequence shows students that word parts generate a hypothesis while context tests it, rather than treating either source of information as sufficient on its own.","dex":{"Having students look up the word in a dictionary, copy the definition, and illustrate it":"Confusing the product of a strategy with the strategy itself; a dictionary supplies an answer rather than a transferable way to derive meaning Looking up a definition produces the meaning but bypasses the strategy the students are supposed to learn. Copying and illustrating a definition gives students no practice with word parts and no practice with the surrounding text, so they are no better equipped when they meet an unfamiliar word in a setting where no dictionary is available.","Having students read the sentence aloud several times and choose the meaning that sounds most natural":"Treating whether a substitute sounds natural in the sentence as evidence of its meaning; plausibility tests syntax and rough sense, not definition Choosing the meaning that sounds most natural measures whether a substitute fits the sentence, not whether it matches the word's actual meaning. Several unrelated meanings can be inserted into a sentence and still sound perfectly acceptable, so auditory plausibility is not evidence of a correct inference.","Having students divide the word into syllables, read each syllable, and blend them to pronounce the word":"Confusing decoding with determining meaning; syllabication serves pronunciation and frequently cuts across morpheme boundaries Dividing a word into syllables and blending it addresses pronunciation, not meaning. Syllable division follows spoken vowel patterns and frequently cuts across meaningful word parts, so it can even work against the morphological analysis the task calls for."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-026","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which activity best supports the development of fluent reading at the word level?","options":["Having students sort words by their vowel pattern and read each column aloud","Having students reread a short passage with a partner until they can read it smoothly","Having students rapidly read lists of high-frequency words and short phrases drawn from the week's text and note any word they hesitate on","Having students mark where punctuation signals a pause before they read a passage aloud"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of fluency instruction at the word level. Word-level fluency means retrieving individual words and short phrases automatically so that attention is not spent on identification. Rapid practice on the words and phrases a student will meet in connected text, combined with attention to the specific words that cause hesitation, builds that automatic retrieval directly.","dex":{"Having students sort words by their vowel pattern and read each column aloud":"Treating word study of spelling patterns as fluency practice; analyzing word form slows recognition rather than automatizing it Sorting words by vowel pattern is a word study routine that supports decoding and spelling. Reading sorted columns draws attention to spelling patterns rather than to instant retrieval of the whole word, and that analytic attention slows recognition rather than accelerating it.","Having students reread a short passage with a partner until they can read it smoothly":"Confusing passage-level fluency practice with word-level practice; rereading connected text meets target words only incidentally Rereading a connected passage targets fluency at the passage level. It has value, but it exercises individual words only incidentally rather than giving concentrated practice on the particular words the student is not yet retrieving automatically.","Having students mark where punctuation signals a pause before they read a passage aloud":"Treating prosody work as though it built automatic word recognition; pauses and stress operate on phrasing, not on retrieval speed for single words Marking punctuation addresses prosody, the expressive shaping of connected text. That work belongs to phrasing at the sentence and passage level, not to the rapid recognition of individual words."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-027","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which practice best teaches sentence structure by working with students' own writing?","options":["Having students memorize a list of coordinating conjunctions and recite it at the start of each writing block","Having students copy well-written sentences from a published book into a notebook for reference","Having students complete daily worksheets in which they correct run-on sentences presented in isolation","Having students take two short sentences from their own drafts and combine them with a conjunction, then discuss how the new sentence changes the meaning"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how written language structure is best taught. Combining sentences the student has already written forces a real syntactic decision: the student must choose a connector that expresses the intended relationship between two ideas. Discussing the result ties the grammatical structure to meaning, which is what makes a convention worth knowing.","dex":{"Having students memorize a list of coordinating conjunctions and recite it at the start of each writing block":"Equating naming a term with knowing how to use it; the value of a conjunction lies in the logical relation it expresses Reciting a list of conjunctions supplies labels for connectors but no practice in choosing among them. Knowing the names tells a writer nothing about which relationship, such as addition, contrast, or cause, a particular sentence needs.","Having students copy well-written sentences from a published book into a notebook for reference":"Equating exposure to models with command of structure; copying is receptive and requires no syntactic decision by the student Reading mentor sentences exposes students to good models, but copying them is receptive work. Unless the student produces and revises an original structure, exposure alone does not build control over sentence structure.","Having students complete daily worksheets in which they correct run-on sentences presented in isolation":"Equating rule drill with grammatical competence; editing isolated sentences transfers poorly to students' own writing Editing isolated sentences drills a rule outside of any communicative purpose. Students can learn to insert a period or a comma without ever deciding how two ideas relate to each other, so the skill rarely shows up in their own drafts."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-028","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A comprehensive picture of students' reading fluency requires which of the following assessment practices? Select all that apply.","options":["Using the same timed oral reading probe three times a year as the only source of fluency data","Collecting running records that capture accuracy, self-corrections, and phrasing during oral reading","Pairing fluency data with a brief retell or a few comprehension questions about the passage the student read","Comparing each student's reading rate with a single grade-level benchmark without considering the difficulty of the text used","Gathering vocabulary evidence from both a formal measure and observations of students' word use in discussion and writing"],"answer":1,"explain":"[B] The question requires an understanding of how fluency is assessed comprehensively. Running records document not simply whether a word was read correctly but how the student handled difficulty, whether the error preserved meaning, and whether the student self-corrected. That detail separates a student who is monitoring for meaning from one who is calling words without comprehension.  [C] Fluency matters because it serves comprehension, so a rate figure cannot be interpreted until it is read alongside what the student took from the text. A student can produce a respectable rate on a passage and still recall almost nothing of it, which changes entirely what the data mean.  [D/E 排除] The stem asks specifically about fluency. Rate-only practices (A, D) omit accuracy, phrasing, and comprehension context. Option E, while sound as vocabulary-assessment practice, addresses language comprehension rather than fluency, so it does not belong in a comprehensive picture of fluency.","dex":{"Using the same timed oral reading probe three times a year as the only source of fluency data":"Treating occasional testing as progress monitoring; three annual data points cannot show change, and rate alone omits prosody and comprehension Three data points a year are too sparse to guide instruction, and a rate-only probe says nothing about phrasing or about understanding. Progress monitoring depends on frequent, short measurements, not on occasional snapshots taken months apart.","Comparing each student's reading rate with a single grade-level benchmark without considering the difficulty of the text used":"Applying a benchmark without reference to text difficulty; rate is interpretable only alongside text level and accuracy Rate is interpretable only in relation to the difficulty of the text and to the accuracy the student achieved on it. A benchmark applied without reference to text level will mark a student who reads an instructional-level passage well as behind, and a student who struggles through a frustration-level passage as average.","Gathering vocabulary evidence from both a formal measure and observations of students' word use in discussion and writing":"Answering a different question: sound vocabulary-assessment practice, but vocabulary is a language-comprehension strand, not a component of fluency (accuracy, rate, prosody). It does not contribute to a comprehensive picture of reading fluency."},"multi":true,"answers":[1,2]},{"id":"8006-fluency-029","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which approach best combines direct instruction in content-area vocabulary with support for students' independent word learning?","options":["Teaching the unit's key terms directly with examples and non-examples, and teaching students to apply word-part and context strategies when they meet new terms on their own","Having students read the chapter silently and list the words whose meanings they do not know","Providing student-friendly definitions of the unit's terms and having students copy them into a glossary","Assigning each student one term to look up and report to the class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how direct instruction and independent word learning support each other. Explicit teaching of the terms a class needs immediately builds the knowledge base for the unit, while instruction in word-part and context strategies gives students a way to handle the many terms no teacher can preteach. Together they address both the words at hand and the words still ahead.","dex":{"Having students read the chapter silently and list the words whose meanings they do not know":"Assuming wide reading alone will close vocabulary gaps; untaught meanings remain unavailable in text, especially for struggling readers Asking students to list unknown words identifies the problem without treating it. Independent reading leaves the meanings untaught, and the students who most need the words are the least able to recover their meanings from context on their own.","Providing student-friendly definitions of the unit's terms and having students copy them into a glossary":"Equating recording a definition with knowing a word; copying produces a reference list, not deep processing of meaning Copying definitions produces a reference page, but recording a definition is not the same as knowing a word. Students get no practice using the terms in their own sentences and no strategy for the many words that never appear in the glossary.","Assigning each student one term to look up and report to the class":"Treating divided lookup as collaborative word learning; each student studies one term and practices lookup rather than independent inference while reading Dividing the words among students leaves each child responsible for one term and merely an audience for the rest, so exposure to the full set is shallow and uneven. It also practices lookup rather than the independent analysis students need while they are reading."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-030","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which statement best describes the primary purpose of preteaching key vocabulary before students read a content-area text?","options":["It reduces the number of unfamiliar words in the text so that attention is available for constructing meaning rather than for stopping at words","It ensures that students will be able to spell the academic terms correctly on the unit assessment","It gives students a written list of definitions to consult while they read","It allows the teacher to address the vocabulary standards before moving on to comprehension"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of why robust vocabulary knowledge matters for comprehension. Each unknown word is a small interruption, and enough interruptions exhaust the attention a reader has available for building the larger message. Preteaching removes those interruptions in advance, so effort goes to ideas rather than to words.","dex":{"It ensures that students will be able to spell the academic terms correctly on the unit assessment":"Treating spelling, a by-product of vocabulary work, as its purpose; preteaching serves comprehension, not performance on a spelling measure Correct spelling may follow from repeated work with a word, but it is not the reason for preteaching. The purpose is comprehension of the text that is about to be read, not accuracy on a spelling measure at the end of the unit.","It gives students a written list of definitions to consult while they read":"Equating a written list of definitions with preteaching; the goal is meaning available in memory during reading, not a reference to consult A list of definitions is a reference, and readers almost never stop mid-sentence to consult one. The point of preteaching is to make meanings available in memory during reading, which requires far more than handing students a list.","It allows the teacher to address the vocabulary standards before moving on to comprehension":"Treating instructional sequence as a matter of coverage; preteaching precedes reading because the upcoming text itself requires those words Coverage is a scheduling concern, not a rationale. Preteaching is sequenced before reading precisely because the words will be needed for the text that follows, not because the standards have to be cleared first."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-031","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade student reads a grade-level passage aloud accurately and at an acceptable rate, but in a flat, word-by-word monotone with pauses that ignore punctuation. When asked what the passage was about, the student recounts several details but cannot state the main idea. Which interpretation of this performance is most accurate?","options":["The student's decoding is automated, so the difficulty lies entirely in vocabulary knowledge","The student reads accurately but without prosody, and the absence of phrasing limits the grouping of words into meaningful units that supports comprehension","The student's comprehension is limited by oral language proficiency rather than by any aspect of reading","The student needs additional phonics instruction to decode multisyllabic words"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how fluency contributes to comprehension. Accuracy and rate are present, so the missing element is prosody: pausing at phrase boundaries and using stress and intonation to signal how words group. Without that phrasing the student processes the text as a string of separate words, which makes the relationships among ideas, and therefore the main idea, much harder to construct.","dex":{"The student's decoding is automated, so the difficulty lies entirely in vocabulary knowledge":"Separating the components of fluency so that once accuracy and rate are adequate the difficulty must be vocabulary; prosody itself supports comprehension Accurate, appropriately paced reading does not rule out a vocabulary problem, but the evidence here points elsewhere. The flat phrasing and the detail-level recall are fluency symptoms, and treating them as a vocabulary gap would leave the phrasing difficulty untouched.","The student's comprehension is limited by oral language proficiency rather than by any aspect of reading":"Attributing a comprehension difficulty to language background without evidence, which recasts a teachable fluency problem as a fixed language factor Nothing in the description identifies this student as an English learner, and oral language proficiency is only one of several possible sources of a comprehension difficulty. Attributing the problem to language background without evidence risks withholding instruction the student actually needs.","The student needs additional phonics instruction to decode multisyllabic words":"Attributing every reading difficulty to decoding; the student already read accurately, so phonics is not the needed next step The student read the passage accurately, which is direct evidence that decoding is working on this text. More phonics instruction would target a skill the student has already demonstrated, leaving the actual difficulty in phrasing unaddressed."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-032","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which activity best develops students' prosody in oral reading?","options":["Having students sound out each word containing the week's spelling pattern before reading the text","Having students listen to a recording of the story without following along in the printed text","Having students rehearse and reread a short passage, using punctuation to decide where to pause and which words to stress","Having students read lists of high-frequency words against a timer and chart their speed"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how prosody is taught. Prosody is the reader's use of phrasing, stress, and intonation to express meaning, and punctuation is the visible map of that phrasing. Asking students to rehearse a short passage and make deliberate decisions about pauses and stress turns expressive reading into something they can practice and revise rather than something they either have or lack.","dex":{"Having students sound out each word containing the week's spelling pattern before reading the text":"Equating word identification with expressive reading; decoding is a prerequisite for prosody, not prosody itself Sounding out words before reading is a decoding routine. It prepares students to identify words, but accurate identification is only the first requirement of expressive reading, not the expressive part itself.","Having students listen to a recording of the story without following along in the printed text":"Treating listening to a model as oral reading practice; without the text in view and the student reading aloud, a model rarely transfers Listening to a fluent model has some value, but without the text in front of them students cannot connect the expression they hear to the print that produced it, and they do no reading of their own.","Having students read lists of high-frequency words against a timer and chart their speed":"Equating reading fast with reading expressively; timed word lists contain no phrase boundaries and no intonation Timed word-list reading builds automatic retrieval of individual words. It contains no phrasing or expression component, because the items are read in isolation from one another and there is no connected text to phrase."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-033","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which of the following practices belong in instruction that helps students derive word meanings from morphology and from surrounding text? Select all that apply.","options":["Teaching students to identify the part of speech an unfamiliar word occupies and the role it plays in the sentence before considering possible meanings","Teaching students to look for appositives, restatements, and signal words such as but or unlike that structure the surrounding text","Teaching students to separate a word into a base and its affixes, combine the parts, and then check the result against the sentence","Teaching students to substitute any word or phrase that makes the sentence sound acceptable to them","Teaching students to skip unfamiliar words and rely on what they already know about the topic to fill in the gaps"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of the sources of information available for word learning. Syntax tells the reader what kind of word this is and what job it is doing in the sentence, which rules out most candidate meanings before any guessing begins. This narrows the search to meanings of the right grammatical type.  [B] Texts regularly supply meaning information through appositives, restatements, examples, and contrast signals. Teaching students to recognize these structures gives them a specific place to look, rather than leaving them to infer from a general impression of the passage.  [C] The question requires an understanding of how morphological analysis and context are used together. Morphology yields a candidate meaning from the word's own parts, and the sentence then serves as a check on that candidate. Teaching the two steps together shows students that word parts propose a meaning while context confirms, narrows, or corrects it.","dex":{"Teaching students to substitute any word or phrase that makes the sentence sound acceptable to them":"Using whether a substitute sounds acceptable as the test of its meaning; acceptability checks syntax and rough sense, not the specific definition Acceptability in the sentence is a very weak test. Many unrelated meanings fit a sentence's grammar and its general sense, so a substitute that sounds perfectly fine can be entirely wrong. Students need evidence from the text or from the word's parts, not a feeling about how the sentence reads.","Teaching students to skip unfamiliar words and rely on what they already know about the topic to fill in the gaps":"Treating background knowledge as a substitute for determining a word's meaning; skipping the word leaves a gap exactly where meaning is needed Background knowledge helps a reader make sense of a text, but it cannot supply the meaning of a particular unfamiliar word, and skipping words leaves the sentence incomplete at exactly the point where the unknown meaning matters. Direct vocabulary instruction is needed in addition to wide reading."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-fluency-034","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which combination best represents research-supported fluency instruction across the phoneme, word, and passage levels?","options":["Phoneme level: rhyming games; word level: weekly spelling tests; passage level: silent sustained reading","Phoneme level: letter-name drills; word level: dictionary lookups; passage level: students reading one sentence each around the circle during a first reading","Phoneme level: oral repetition of sentences; word level: memorizing vocabulary definitions; passage level: independent reading of chapter books with no rereading","Phoneme level: blending and segmenting practice; word level: timed practice on high-frequency words and phrases; passage level: repeated reading of short texts with feedback"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how fluency is developed at more than one level of text. Phoneme-level work secures the sound structure that supports accurate word identification, word and phrase practice builds the automatic retrieval that keeps attention free, and repeated reading of connected text with feedback joins the two and adds phrasing. Each level feeds the next, which is why effective instruction schedules all three.","dex":{"Phoneme level: rhyming games; word level: weekly spelling tests; passage level: silent sustained reading":"Assembling unrelated literacy activities into a fluency program; rhyming, spelling tests, and sustained silent reading build neither automaticity nor prosody in oral reading Rhyming games are phonological awareness activities that belong earlier in development, spelling tests measure encoding rather than reading fluency, and silent sustained reading provides no feedback and no reason to reread. The three activities do not add up to fluency instruction.","Phoneme level: letter-name drills; word level: dictionary lookups; passage level: students reading one sentence each around the circle during a first reading":"Treating a familiar classroom routine as effective fluency instruction; round-robin reading gives little practice time and offers neither repetition nor feedback Letter-name drills do not address the sound manipulation that underlies accurate word reading, dictionary lookups interrupt reading rather than building automaticity, and round-robin reading gives each student very little actual reading time and no chance to rehearse.","Phoneme level: oral repetition of sentences; word level: memorizing vocabulary definitions; passage level: independent reading of chapter books with no rereading":"Assuming volume of reading produces fluency; wide reading without rereading or feedback does not improve rate or phrasing Repeating sentences by ear does not develop the sound-level analysis that supports decoding, and independent reading with no rereading and no feedback leaves rate and phrasing unpracticed. Fluent reading is built through supported, repeated oral reading rather than through volume alone."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-035","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which practice best teaches capitalization and end punctuation by building on what students already do when they speak?","options":["Having students read their own writing aloud and mark the places where their voice comes to a full stop","Giving students a worksheet of sentences to punctuate before they continue drafting","Having students copy a published paragraph and circle the capital letters and periods the author used","Telling students to place a period at the end of every line they write"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how written conventions can be taught through students' own language. A reader's voice already pauses at sentence boundaries, so reading aloud makes those boundaries audible. Marking where the voice stops and then adding the capital letter and the end mark connects a convention to a meaning signal the student can already produce, which is why the point transfers to independent writing.","dex":{"Giving students a worksheet of sentences to punctuate before they continue drafting":"Equating correct worksheet answers with command of conventions; practice on someone else's sentences transfers poorly to the student's own drafts Worksheets present someone else's sentences, so the student applies a rule to text whose intended phrasing is not their own. What is practiced is performance on isolated items, and it rarely appears in the student's drafts.","Having students copy a published paragraph and circle the capital letters and periods the author used":"Equating noticing conventions in mentor text with applying them; copying builds awareness but requires no decision about where a sentence ends Noticing conventions in published text builds awareness, but copying remains receptive work. The student observes that capitals and periods appear without ever deciding where a sentence should end in their own writing.","Telling students to place a period at the end of every line they write":"Substituting physical line position for a syntactic judgment; line ends are not sentence boundaries, so the rule systematically creates errors Line breaks have nothing to do with sentence boundaries, so this rule would place periods mid-sentence as often as at the end. It replaces a meaning-based decision with a mechanical one and produces more errors than it fixes."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-036","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which approach gives the most complete picture of the vocabulary knowledge of young students whose out-of-school experiences with books differ widely?","options":["A standardized, individually administered picture vocabulary test given once in the fall","A standardized receptive vocabulary measure combined with observations of students' word use during read-aloud discussions, play, and classroom routines","Asking families to report the number of words their child knows","Counting the number of different words students produce in a single writing sample"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how vocabulary is assessed fairly across varied experience. A formal measure supplies a comparable score, while observations of spontaneous word use show whether a word is available for the student's own purposes. Using both guards against the risk that one formal measure mainly reflects familiarity with testing situations and school-register words rather than the student's actual vocabulary.","dex":{"A standardized, individually administered picture vocabulary test given once in the fall":"Treating one standardized administration as a complete picture of vocabulary; a single score shows no growth and can mistake task unfamiliarity for limited word knowledge One administration of one formal measure is a narrow sample. Picture vocabulary tests depend on particular words and a particular response format, so a low score may reflect unfamiliarity with the task or with the pictured items rather than limited vocabulary, and a single fall score shows nothing about growth.","Asking families to report the number of words their child knows":"Treating family estimates as assessment data; such reports lack a shared standard and cannot be compared across students Families cannot count the words a child knows, and respondents differ widely in what they take the question to mean and in which situations they have observed the child. The result is impressionistic and cannot be compared across students.","Counting the number of different words students produce in a single writing sample":"Treating the words in a single writing sample as the full measure of vocabulary; spelling ability and topic choice sharply limit what young children can show A single writing sample limits students to the words they can spell and to the topic they happened to choose, so it systematically under-represents what young children know. It is useful as one piece of evidence but inadequate as the whole picture."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-037","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"During a science discussion, a fifth-grade student says, 'The water evaporated, so it disappeared and it is gone forever.' Which teacher response best targets the particular concept in need of attention?","options":["'Can someone else give us a better sentence using evaporated?'","'That is a good try, but disappeared is the wrong word. Please say your sentence again using evaporated.'","'You are right that the water is no longer in the cup. Where did it go, and what form is it in now?'","'Let us look up evaporate in the dictionary, and then everyone can copy the definition.'"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to respond to student talk so that the response targets the concept at issue. The student has used the target word correctly and has one part of the idea, that the water is no longer visible, but has attached the everyday meaning of disappearance to it. Asking where the water went and what form it is in now presses directly on the missing piece, that matter changes state rather than ceasing to exist, while building on what the student already has right.","dex":{"'Can someone else give us a better sentence using evaporated?'":"Equating hearing a better answer from a classmate with revising one's own thinking; the original speaker never examines their own reasoning Handing the question to another student bypasses the student who needs the help. The original speaker hears a better sentence without ever examining the assumption behind their own.","'That is a good try, but disappeared is the wrong word. Please say your sentence again using evaporated.'":"Mistaking a conceptual error for a word-choice error; a student can restate the term and still hold the original misunderstanding This response treats the difficulty as a word choice problem, but the student already used evaporated appropriately. Asking for the word again leaves the underlying idea, that the water still exists in another form, completely unaddressed.","'Let us look up evaporate in the dictionary, and then everyone can copy the definition.'":"Assuming an authoritative definition corrects a misconception; a definition the student never reconciles with their own idea does not replace it A dictionary definition supplies authoritative wording, but copying it does not require the student to reconcile the definition with what they just said. A student can write the definition and continue to believe that the water is simply gone."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-038","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A teacher selects the words sufficient, reluctant, and vivid for intensive vocabulary instruction instead of dinosaur, meteor, and fossil. Which rationale best justifies this selection?","options":["The selected words are longer and more difficult to decode, so students need more help reading them","The words not selected are too abstract for students at this grade level to understand","The selected words appear more often on standardized tests than the words not selected","The selected words are general academic words that appear across many subjects and text types, so teaching them yields returns well beyond the current unit"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how words are selected for instruction. Mature, general-purpose words such as sufficient and reluctant recur across content areas and across the texts students will meet for years, so instruction in them pays off repeatedly. Words tied to a single unit are better taught as part of learning that content and need far less extended treatment.","dex":{"The selected words are longer and more difficult to decode, so students need more help reading them":"Selecting words for instruction by length or decoding difficulty; decoding ease and vocabulary value are separate matters, and many short words most need teaching Decoding difficulty and vocabulary value are different matters. Word length says nothing about whether a word is worth teaching for meaning, and many short words carry the meanings that cause readers the most trouble.","The words not selected are too abstract for students at this grade level to understand":"Assuming words are passed over because they are too abstract; they are passed over because they are unit-specific and taught through the content itself The words not selected are concrete and are more readily taught through the unit's pictures, diagrams, and hands-on work. The reason for passing them over is that they are unit-specific, not that they are beyond the students.","The selected words appear more often on standardized tests than the words not selected":"Ranking words for instruction by test frequency rather than by their usefulness across texts and subjects Test frequency is not a principled basis for selecting words, and a list built this way would shift unpredictably from year to year. Selection should rest on how useful a word is across the texts and subjects students read."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-039","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which statement best explains how reading fluency contributes to comprehension?","options":["Fluent readers read aloud with expression, which lets the teacher confirm that they have understood the text","Accurate, automatic word recognition frees attention that would otherwise go to identifying words, so it can go to constructing meaning","Fluent readers read more text in the same amount of time, and the greater volume of reading produces better comprehension","Fluent readers tend to know more words, and it is that vocabulary knowledge rather than fluency that produces comprehension"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of the mechanism connecting fluency and comprehension. Attention is limited: a reader who must stop and work out each word spends capacity on identification and has little left for connecting ideas. When word recognition becomes accurate and automatic, that capacity is released for the work of comprehension.","dex":{"Fluent readers read aloud with expression, which lets the teacher confirm that they have understood the text":"Treating an observable indicator as the mechanism at work; prosody is a visible sign of fluency, not the reason fluency aids comprehension Expression is one component of fluency and a useful signal to a teacher, but a signal is not a mechanism. This statement describes how fluency can be observed rather than how it helps the reader understand.","Fluent readers read more text in the same amount of time, and the greater volume of reading produces better comprehension":"Treating volume of text covered as the route by which fluency helps; quantity does not guarantee understanding, whereas released attention does Volume of reading matters, but the quantity of text covered is not what makes fluency valuable. A reader can move through a great deal of text quickly while understanding very little, so speed in itself does not produce comprehension.","Fluent readers tend to know more words, and it is that vocabulary knowledge rather than fluency that produces comprehension":"Explaining away the construct the question asks about by citing a related one; even known words must be recognized automatically for attention to reach meaning Vocabulary and fluency are related but separate contributors, and this statement explains away the one the question asks about. Fluency supports comprehension even when the reader already knows the words, because those words still have to be recognized automatically for attention to reach meaning."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-040","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which practice is most likely to improve the oral reading rate of students who read slowly and laboriously, without sacrificing comprehension?","options":["Having students reread a short passage at their instructional level several times while receiving feedback, working toward a goal for words read correctly per minute","Having students read new and increasingly difficult texts aloud each day in order to build stamina","Encouraging students to read as quickly as they can and to skip words they do not know","Having students listen to an audio recording of the text once before the class discusses it"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how rate is developed. Repeated reading of a text at the student's instructional level lets each successive reading go a little more smoothly, and the feedback together with a goal for words read correctly per minute keeps accuracy from being traded away for speed. Because the goal counts only words read correctly, pushing for a higher number cannot reward sloppy reading.","dex":{"Having students read new and increasingly difficult texts aloud each day in order to build stamina":"Treating increased text difficulty as the way to build rate; automaticity comes from repeated reading of appropriate text, not from ever-harder new text Reading new and harder texts adds challenge rather than the repetition that builds automaticity. A student who is already laborious will spend the lesson decoding, and the rate at which that student reads unfamiliar text will not improve.","Encouraging students to read as quickly as they can and to skip words they do not know":"Equating reading fast with reading well; skipping words raises the number while sacrificing accuracy and comprehension Skipping unknown words raises a rate figure while removing the accuracy and the meaning-checking that make rate worth measuring in the first place. It also teaches a habit that leaves the student understanding less, which is the opposite of the goal.","Having students listen to an audio recording of the text once before the class discusses it":"Treating a single listening experience as reading practice; without the student rereading aloud and receiving feedback, rate does not improve Hearing a fluent model once gives students no repeated practice reading the text themselves and no feedback on their own performance. Exposure is not practice."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-041","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which statement best explains why context clues alone are often insufficient for determining the literal meaning of an unfamiliar word?","options":["Context clues work only for general academic words, not for words specific to a subject","Once a reader can decode a word, the surrounding context is no longer needed","Context typically narrows a word to a general sense or category, while the word's parts and reference sources are needed for a precise meaning","Authors rarely provide context clues, so readers cannot count on finding them"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of what each source of information can and cannot supply. Context constrains meaning to a plausible range, so the reader can usually tell roughly what kind of thing a word refers to and whether it is favorable or unfavorable, but context seldom pins the meaning down exactly. Morphological analysis and a dictionary or glossary are what close that gap, which is why instruction teaches students to treat context as one source among several.","dex":{"Context clues work only for general academic words, not for words specific to a subject":"Treating word tiers as the condition for whether context clues work; tier determines instructional time, not whether context helps Context clues operate the same way for subject-specific and general words, and content texts often provide the strongest clues of all because they define their terms explicitly. The tier a word belongs to affects how much instructional time it deserves, not whether context can help.","Once a reader can decode a word, the surrounding context is no longer needed":"Equating decoding a word with knowing its meaning; decoding yields a pronunciation, and meaning still requires context, word parts, or a reference source Decoding yields a pronunciation, not a meaning, and many words a student can decode are entirely unknown in their spoken form. Context remains useful after a word is decoded, and decoding alone never establishes what a word means.","Authors rarely provide context clues, so readers cannot count on finding them":"Asserting that context clues are rarely present; the real limitation is that context narrows meaning without pinning it down The premise is false. Authors and textbooks regularly supply restatements, appositives, examples, and contrast signals, and students can be taught to find them. The limitation of context is that it constrains meaning without fixing it precisely, not that it is absent."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-042","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which approach uses teacher modeling to build phrasing and accuracy in students' oral reading?","options":["Assigning students to read the passage independently several times before the class discusses it","Having students look up the unfamiliar words in the passage before they read it aloud","Having students take turns reading one sentence each around the circle during a first reading of new text","Reading a passage aloud with phrasing and expression and then having students reread the same sentences immediately afterward"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how modeling supports fluency. Hearing a passage read with appropriate phrasing gives students a concrete example of how the print should sound, and immediately rereading the same sentences lets them reproduce that model while it is fresh. This imitative reading builds accuracy and phrasing together, because students are matching a target rather than inventing one.","dex":{"Assigning students to read the passage independently several times before the class discusses it":"Equating repeated independent reading with guided repeated oral reading; repetition without a model or feedback consolidates current habits Independent rereading without a model or feedback leaves students rehearsing whatever they already do, including any word-by-word or inaccurate habits. Practice by itself consolidates current performance; it does not show students what better reading sounds like.","Having students look up the unfamiliar words in the passage before they read it aloud":"Substituting vocabulary preparation for fluency instruction; looking up words addresses meaning, not automaticity or phrasing in oral reading Looking up words before reading addresses vocabulary, which is worthwhile in its own right, but it does not model phrasing and gives students no oral reading practice. It also interrupts the flow of the passage rather than building fluency through that flow.","Having students take turns reading one sentence each around the circle during a first reading of new text":"Treating round-robin reading as fluency practice; each student reads very little unfamiliar text, with no model and no chance to rehearse Taking one-sentence turns during a first reading gives each student very little reading time and no chance to rehearse, and the text is unfamiliar, so there is no model to draw on. The routine distributes turns rather than developing fluency."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-043","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade teacher writes two sentences on the board: \"The dog chased the cat.\" and \"The cat chased the dog.\" Which instructional point is most important for the teacher to make explicit?","options":["The two sentences contain the same words, so they carry the same meaning.","English word order signals who performs the action, so changing word order changes the meaning.","Both sentences are written in the passive voice, which is why they look so similar.","Changing word order is a matter of personal style and does not affect what a reader understands."],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how the structure of written language carries meaning. In English, position in the sentence signals grammatical role: the noun before the verb is the doer and the noun after it is the receiver. Making this explicit teaches students to use syntax, not just word meanings, to work out who did what in a sentence.","dex":{"The two sentences contain the same words, so they carry the same meaning.":"Treating sentence meaning as the sum of individual word meanings, so that syntax contributes nothing and word order needs no teaching. Option (A) is incorrect. The two sentences share every word yet describe opposite events, which shows that meaning is not simply the sum of word meanings. This option reflects the common misconception that sentence meaning is built by adding up dictionary definitions. A teacher who holds it would skip instruction in word order and leave students without a way to interpret the relations in a sentence.","Both sentences are written in the passive voice, which is why they look so similar.":"Overextending the label \"passive voice\" to any sentence about an action, rather than checking for the be-verb plus past participle pattern. Option (C) is incorrect. Both sentences use active verbs with the subject performing the action; the passive voice would require a form of be plus a past participle, as in \"The cat was chased by the dog.\" This option reflects the common misconception that any sentence describing an action is passive. Mislabeling the structure would send the teacher to the wrong grammar lesson entirely.","Changing word order is a matter of personal style and does not affect what a reader understands.":"Treating grammar as a matter of style or preference, so that structural choices are seen as optional rather than meaning-bearing. Option (D) is incorrect. Word order here changes what the sentence means, so it functions as a grammatical signal rather than a stylistic preference. This option reflects the common misconception that syntax is decorative and that word choice alone determines comprehension. Students who believe this have no reason to revisit a sentence's structure when a sentence confuses them."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-044","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A second-grade teacher wants an informal measure of her students' depth of word knowledge rather than their ability to supply a definition. Which assessment best serves this purpose?","options":["A multiple-choice test asking students to match each word to its dictionary definition","A timed list of words that students read aloud as quickly as they can","A task in which students connect a word to their own experience and give examples and non-examples","A spelling test covering the week's vocabulary words"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how informal assessment can capture depth of word knowledge. Asking students to connect a word to their own experience and to supply examples and non-examples reveals whether they know a word's boundaries and uses, not merely whether they can recite a definition. Because students draw on their own experiences, the task also surfaces knowledge that multilingual learners and students from varied backgrounds may not display on a standard definition test.","dex":{"A multiple-choice test asking students to match each word to its dictionary definition":"Equating the ability to recognize or recite a definition with full knowledge of a word, ignoring whether the student can use it or judge where it does not apply. Option (A) is incorrect. Matching a word to a dictionary definition tests whether a student can recognize a memorized phrase, which is a shallow level of word knowledge. This option reflects the common misconception that knowing a definition is the same as knowing a word. A student can select the right definition and still be unable to use the word or to say when it does not apply.","A timed list of words that students read aloud as quickly as they can":"Using a fluency index such as naming speed as though it were evidence about word meaning, conflating two separate constructs. Option (B) is incorrect. Timed oral reading of a word list measures how quickly and accurately students name words, which is a fluency measure. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency and vocabulary can be measured with the same instrument. Naming a word quickly tells the teacher nothing about what the student understands it to mean.","A spelling test covering the week's vocabulary words":"Treating accuracy of written form as evidence of semantic knowledge, as though spelling and meaning were the same dimension of word knowledge. Option (D) is incorrect. A spelling test checks whether a student can reproduce the written form of a word, which says nothing about the meanings attached to it. This option reflects the common misconception that correct written form indicates full word knowledge. Orthographic form is one dimension of knowing a word, and it can be intact while meaning is missing."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-045","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fourth-grade teacher is selecting words from a science text for direct vocabulary instruction. Which set of words most warrants that instruction?","options":["Highly technical terms that the text defines once, such as metamorphosis","Proper nouns naming the scientists mentioned in the passage","Words the students already use in everyday conversation, such as water and animal","General academic words that appear across subjects, such as observe, evidence, and conclude"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how words are selected for direct instruction. General academic words such as observe, evidence, and conclude appear across content areas and carry the reasoning of a text, so teaching them pays off in many later readings. They are also unlikely to be picked up from a single exposure, which is precisely what makes direct instruction worthwhile rather than incidental.","dex":{"Highly technical terms that the text defines once, such as metamorphosis":"Ranking words for instruction by apparent difficulty instead of by their usefulness across texts and subjects. Option (C) is incorrect. A term like metamorphosis is tied to one topic, is typically defined by the text itself, and has limited value beyond that topic. This option reflects the common misconception that the hardest-sounding words deserve the most instructional time. Selection should be driven by how far a word travels across texts, not by how difficult it sounds.","Proper nouns naming the scientists mentioned in the passage":"Treating unfamiliar proper nouns as vocabulary to be taught, when they are one-time labels with no transfer value. Option (D) is incorrect. Proper nouns name particular people or places and are learned as labels, not as concepts that transfer to new reading. This option reflects the common misconception that any unfamiliar item in a passage is a vocabulary target. Time spent on proper nouns does not build the network of meanings that comprehension depends on.","Words the students already use in everyday conversation, such as water and animal":"Choosing words by how recognizable they are, so that instruction repeats what students already own instead of adding what they lack. Option (A) is incorrect. Words such as water and animal are already part of most fourth graders' oral vocabularies, so time spent on them displaces words the students do not yet have. This option reflects the common misconception that familiar words are the safest and therefore the best teaching targets. Conversational familiarity is not the same as control of a word's uses in academic text."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-046","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Before her fifth graders read a chapter about ecosystems, a teacher plans to preteach a small set of words. Which preteaching approach best supports their comprehension of the passage?","options":["Giving student-friendly definitions with examples, then discussing how each word is used in the specific passage","Reading the chapter aloud once while students raise their hands at words they do not know","Having students copy the dictionary definition of each word into their notebooks","Having students look up each word on their own and write one sentence using it"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how preteaching vocabulary supports comprehension. A student-friendly definition gives students a usable meaning, and connecting the word immediately to the sentence where it will appear turns that meaning into something they can apply while reading. Removing the word-level bottleneck in advance frees attention for the ideas the chapter builds.","dex":{"Reading the chapter aloud once while students raise their hands at words they do not know":"Treating the identification of unknown words as though it were instruction in their meanings. Option (D) is incorrect. Reading the chapter aloud once lets students notice which words they do not know, but noticing is not learning. This option reflects the common misconception that identifying unknown words is the same as teaching them. Without meanings supplied beforehand, students who stumble on those words lose the thread of the passage.","Having students copy the dictionary definition of each word into their notebooks":"Equating transcription of a definition with vocabulary instruction, and assuming that having the words written down means the meanings were learned. Option (A) is incorrect. Copying dictionary definitions produces little engagement with meaning and gives students no experience using the word in the passage. This option reflects the common misconception that exposure to a written definition constitutes vocabulary instruction. Definitions are often abstract or list several senses, and copying them does not tell a student which sense applies here.","Having students look up each word on their own and write one sentence using it":"Assuming students can independently select the correct sense from a dictionary entry, when choosing among senses is itself the hard part. Option (B) is incorrect. Looking words up independently leaves students to choose among multiple senses without guidance, and they frequently pick the first or shortest entry. This option reflects the common misconception that independent dictionary work is an efficient substitute for teacher-directed preteaching. Independence is a long-term goal, but it is not the way to prepare a class for one specific difficult text."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-047","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Ms. Reed's third graders read the same grade-level passage. Devin reads 104 words correct per minute with smooth phrasing but answers one of five comprehension questions correctly. Marisol reads 61 words correct per minute, stopping often to work out words, and answers four of five correctly. Which interpretation of this pattern is most accurate?","options":["Automatic word recognition is sufficient for comprehension, so Devin's low score must reflect careless answering.","Automatic word recognition frees attention for meaning but does not supply the meaning itself, so comprehension also depends on the knowledge and language a reader brings to the text.","Reading rate determines how much of a text is retained, so Marisol's slower reading should have left her remembering less than Devin.","Comprehension is built almost entirely from background knowledge, so how automatically a student reads has little to do with it."],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how fluency contributes to comprehension. Automatic word recognition takes the word-level bottleneck out of reading so that attention can go to meaning, and that is the whole reason fluency matters. What the freed attention then operates on is the reader's language, vocabulary, and knowledge of the topic. Devin's smooth reading shows the bridge is in place, not that he crossed it, and Marisol's slow but thoughtful reading shows that a reader can construct meaning even while word recognition is still effortful. For instruction the practical point is that fluency is necessary without being sufficient.","dex":{"Automatic word recognition is sufficient for comprehension, so Devin's low score must reflect careless answering.":"Equating fluent, automatic reading with comprehension, so that a smooth performance is accepted as proof that meaning was constructed. Option (A) is incorrect. Fluent word recognition releases attention for meaning; it does not guarantee that meaning gets built, and a student can read a passage beautifully and take almost nothing from it. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency is sufficient for comprehension. A teacher who holds it will hunt for carelessness or a behavior problem instead of examining Devin's vocabulary and language comprehension, which is where his difficulty actually sits.","Reading rate determines how much of a text is retained, so Marisol's slower reading should have left her remembering less than Devin.":"Treating reading rate as the cause of retention rather than as a symptom of how automatically the words were recognized. Option (C) is incorrect. Marisol reads more slowly and comprehends more, which is direct evidence against rate as the cause of retention; rate reports how automatically the words were recognized, not how much was understood. This option reflects the common misconception that speed produces comprehension. Acting on it would push Devin to read faster when what he needs is work on the meanings he is not getting from the page.","Comprehension is built almost entirely from background knowledge, so how automatically a student reads has little to do with it.":"Treating background knowledge as the whole of comprehension, so that fluent word recognition is dismissed as unrelated to understanding. Option (D) is incorrect. Background knowledge strongly shapes comprehension, but it operates on meanings the reader still has to get off the page, and a reader who stalls on every other word cannot bring that knowledge to bear. This option reflects the common misconception that one contributor to comprehension makes the others irrelevant. Fluency and vocabulary each remove a different bottleneck, and instruction has to address whichever one is blocking a particular reader."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-048","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third grader reads a grade-level passage at 116 words correct per minute with 98 percent accuracy, but in a flat, even voice that runs straight through commas and periods, and he cannot retell what the passage said. Which interpretation of this performance is most accurate?","options":["Accuracy is still too low to support comprehension, so instruction should return to decoding individual words.","The student is fluent, because both rate and accuracy are at or above the benchmark for the grade.","Word recognition is automatic but the phrasing that groups words into meaning is missing, so the reading sounds smooth while producing little comprehension.","The student read too fast to take in the meaning, and asking him to slow down would restore his comprehension."],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of what each component of oral reading fluency contributes. Rate and accuracy report that word recognition has become automatic; prosody is the component that shows whether the reader is grouping words into meaningful units, and its absence here is what leaves the student with nothing to retell. Instruction therefore has to give him a phrased model to imitate on text he can already read accurately, rather than more practice on the two components that are already in place.","dex":{"Accuracy is still too low to support comprehension, so instruction should return to decoding individual words.":"Attributing any comprehension failure to weak decoding, even when accuracy shows that word recognition is already secure. Option (D) is incorrect. At 98 percent accuracy the student is reading the words that are on the page, so decoding is not what is holding his comprehension back. This option reflects the common misconception that every comprehension failure traces back to weak word recognition. Returning to decoding would reteach a skill this student has already demonstrated and would leave the missing component untouched.","The student is fluent, because both rate and accuracy are at or above the benchmark for the grade.":"Equating fluency with its two countable indicators, so that prosody drops out of the construct entirely. Option (A) is incorrect. Rate and accuracy are the two components that are easiest to count, but fluency also includes prosody, and this student's reading shows exactly what is missing when that component is left out. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency is fully described by a words-correct-per-minute score. Benchmarks screen for risk; they do not establish that the reader is building meaning.","The student read too fast to take in the meaning, and asking him to slow down would restore his comprehension.":"Assuming that slowing the reading down will produce comprehension, when rate is not what is keeping the reader from the meaning. Option (C) is incorrect. Rate is not the problem here: reading the same flat phrasing more slowly would produce the same empty result, because slowing down does not group words into meaningful units. This option reflects the common misconception that rate is the lever to pull whenever comprehension fails. What has to change is phrasing, and phrasing is taught through a model and rereading, not through pacing."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-049","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Ms. Alvarez's third graders stop at the sentence \"The arid desert received almost no rain all year.\" She wants to show them how to work out the meaning of \"arid\" by combining context, syntax, and word-structure knowledge. Which teacher move best models that process?","options":["Having the students sound out arid syllable by syllable before rereading the sentence","Having the students skip the word, finish the sentence, and put in a word that sounds right","Telling the students that arid means \"dry\" and having them write the definition three times","Asking what the sentence says happens to the desert, what part of speech arid must be, and whether any word parts look familiar, then checking the candidate meaning against the sentence"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how systematic and explicit instruction is delivered for word-solving. By asking what the sentence reports, what part of speech the word must be, and whether any word parts are familiar, the teacher models a routine that draws on context, syntax, and morphology together and then verifies the candidate meaning against the sentence. The questioning makes the thinking visible, so students can run the same routine on the next unfamiliar word without her.","dex":{"Having the students sound out arid syllable by syllable before rereading the sentence":"Treating successful decoding as if it yielded meaning, when it yields only pronunciation. Option (C) is incorrect. Sounding out arid helps students pronounce the word, but pronunciation does not deliver meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that decoding a word and understanding it are a single achievement. Students who can read a word aloud accurately may still have no idea what it means.","Having the students skip the word, finish the sentence, and put in a word that sounds right":"Treating an unverified plausible substitution as equivalent to a checked inference from context and word structure. Option (D) is incorrect. Substituting a word that sounds right may produce a plausible reading, but plausibility is not evidence, and students have no way to tell whether their guess matches the author's meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that any context-based guess counts as strategic word learning. Without checking the guess against context, syntax, and word parts, the strategy is unreliable.","Telling the students that arid means \"dry\" and having them write the definition three times":"Confusing the delivery of an answer with the teaching of a procedure, so that explicit instruction is reduced to telling. Option (A) is incorrect. Supplying the definition ends the reasoning before it begins and leaves students with nothing to use on the next unfamiliar word. This option reflects the common misconception that telling a word's meaning is teaching a strategy. A definition is a product; the transferable thing is the procedure for arriving at it."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-050","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A third-grade teacher is deciding whether a student's fluency problem should be addressed at the phoneme level, the word level, or the passage level. The student reads a grade-level passage accurately but in short bursts, and the stops come almost entirely on words of two or three syllables. Given a list of one-syllable high-frequency words, the student reads quickly and without hesitation. Which instructional decision is best supported by this evidence?","options":["Work at the word level on common syllable patterns and word parts, because the stops inside longer words are what break the student's reading into fragments.","Work at the passage level on phrasing and expression, because the student already reads accurately and mainly needs a model to imitate.","Increase the amount of timed repeated reading, because raising rate will eventually pull the slower recognition of longer words along with it.","Start at the phoneme level with blending and segmenting, because slow oral reading shows that phonemic awareness is still weak."],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how instruction is matched to the level at which reading breaks down. The student already retrieves one-syllable high-frequency words in one step, so the breakdown sits inside longer words, and teaching common syllable patterns and frequent word parts is what makes those words retrievable the same way. Once the longer words come automatically the stops disappear, and phrasing finally has continuous text to work on, which is why the word level is the right place to start.","dex":{"Work at the passage level on phrasing and expression, because the student already reads accurately and mainly needs a model to imitate.":"Reading accuracy as automaticity, so that passage-level phrasing work is prescribed while recognition of individual words is still effortful. Option (C) is incorrect. The accuracy here is accuracy bought one word at a time, and phrasing cannot be layered over reading that keeps stopping inside words. This option reflects the common misconception that accurate reading is the same as automatic reading. A model of phrased reading is valuable once the words can be retrieved without effort, and pushing it earlier just gives the student a target he cannot reach.","Increase the amount of timed repeated reading, because raising rate will eventually pull the slower recognition of longer words along with it.":"Applying timed repeated reading as a universal fluency remedy, without first identifying which level is causing the breakdown. Option (D) is incorrect. Repeated reading is a sound passage-level routine, but timing the student over words he must still work out practices the stop-and-start pattern instead of removing its cause. This option reflects the common misconception that a favored fluency routine applies at every level of the problem. Matching the routine to the level where reading actually breaks down is what makes the practice pay off.","Start at the phoneme level with blending and segmenting, because slow oral reading shows that phonemic awareness is still weak.":"Tracing slow oral reading to a phonemic awareness deficit even when the student decodes accurately, and defaulting to the lowest level of instruction. Option (A) is incorrect. The student reads the words accurately, which means the sounds are being blended successfully; phonemic awareness work would treat a difficulty this student does not have. This option reflects the common misconception that any slow oral reading points back to a phonemic awareness deficit. Reaching for the lowest level of the hierarchy spends instructional time that this student needs at the word level."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-051","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A second-grade teacher notices that many students leave out capitalization and end punctuation in their own writing even though they complete workbook pages on those rules correctly. Which instructional change best addresses the gap?","options":["Assigning additional workbook pages on capitalization and punctuation for homework each night","Having students revise sentences taken from their own writing using a class editing checklist","Having students copy a published paragraph to practice neat handwriting","Reviewing the capitalization and punctuation rules on the board before each writing period"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how the conventions of written language are taught. Revising a sentence from their own writing, using a checklist the class has built, asks students to make an editing decision in a meaningful context instead of recalling a rule in isolation. Because the sentence is theirs, the correction is tied to a real communicative purpose, and that connection is what carries over into later writing.","dex":{"Assigning additional workbook pages on capitalization and punctuation for homework each night":"Attributing a failure to apply knowledge to insufficient practice quantity, rather than to a mismatch between practice format and application context. Option (A) is incorrect. More workbook pages supply more practice in the very format that has already failed to transfer. This option reflects the common misconception that a transfer problem is a practice-volume problem. Students who can complete the pages but not apply the conventions in their own writing need a different kind of task, not more of the same one.","Having students copy a published paragraph to practice neat handwriting":"Confusing the conventions of written language with neat handwriting, so that correct-looking transcription is mistaken for editing competence. Option (C) is incorrect. Copying a model gives students correct forms to look at but requires no decision from them, so nothing changes in their own writing. This option reflects the common misconception that conventions are a handwriting or transcription skill. Conventions involve editing choices, and copying removes the need to make any.","Reviewing the capitalization and punctuation rules on the board before each writing period":"Assuming that stating a rule again will produce the ability to use it, when the missing piece is guided application. Option (D) is incorrect. Restating the rules adds nothing for students who already know them well enough to pass the workbook pages. This option reflects the common misconception that knowing a rule is the same as applying it while composing. The gap here is application, and application is built through guided practice in editing real sentences."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-052","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fourth-grade teacher is assembling fluency data for a class that includes several multilingual learners and several students receiving reading intervention. Which practices together give the most complete picture of each student's fluency? Select all that apply.","options":["Recording words correct per minute on a one-minute cold read of a grade-level passage","Rating phrasing and expression with a prosody rubric during oral reading","Checking whether the student can retell the passage or answer questions about it","Ranking students against one another to identify the strongest readers in the class","Using a single timed reading taken in the fall as the only fluency measure of the year"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how formal and informal measures combine to describe fluency. Options (A), (B), and (C) are correct. Words correct per minute on a cold read gives a comparable index of accuracy and rate that can be tracked over time.  [B] A prosody rubric captures phrasing and expression, which a rate score misses entirely; two students can earn the same words correct per minute while one reads in meaningful phrases and the other reads word by word.  [C] A retell or a set of questions shows whether the accurate, well-phrased reading is actually producing meaning. Used together, the three separate the student who reads smoothly without understanding from the student who reads slowly but comprehends well.","dex":{"Ranking students against one another to identify the strongest readers in the class":"Treating assessment as a sorting device that ranks students against each other, rather than as a source of information about what each student needs. Option (D) is incorrect. Ranking students orders them against one another but describes no individual's instructional needs, and it says nothing about what to teach next. This option reflects the common misconception that the purpose of fluency assessment is to sort students. Within-class comparisons do not tell a teacher how to plan instruction.","Using a single timed reading taken in the fall as the only fluency measure of the year":"Treating one administration of a formal measure as complete documentation of a student's fluency, when growth requires repeated measures. Option (E) is incorrect. One timed reading in the fall is a single snapshot that is sensitive to the passage chosen and to how the student happened to read that day, and it cannot show growth. This option reflects the common misconception that a single administration of a formal measure is sufficient documentation. Progress monitoring requires repeated measures collected over time."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-fluency-053","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"To encourage independent word learning, a fourth-grade teacher has students keep personal word-collection notebooks during independent reading. Which additional step most strengthens the practice?","options":["Requiring students to collect a set number of words each week","Having students copy the dictionary entry for each word they collect","Building in regular sharing and discussion so collected words get further encounters in new contexts","Quizzing students every Friday on the words in their notebooks"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how direct instruction and independent learning work together in vocabulary development. Independent collection gives students ownership of the words, but words are learned through repeated and varied encounters, and scheduled sharing and discussion supply those encounters. It also gives the teacher a chance to correct meanings students have guessed wrong, which is what turns a private list into durable knowledge.","dex":{"Requiring students to collect a set number of words each week":"Measuring vocabulary learning by the quantity of words collected rather than by the quality of the encounters with each one. Option (A) is incorrect. A weekly quota pushes students to fill pages with easy or incidental words instead of noticing the words they actually need. This option reflects the common misconception that the number of words gathered determines how much is learned. Depth of processing matters more than the count of entries.","Having students copy the dictionary entry for each word they collect":"Equating recording a word in a notebook with acquiring its meaning, treating the notebook as the product rather than as a prompt for later encounters. Option (B) is incorrect. Copying a dictionary entry is transcription, and entries typically list several senses without indicating which one fits the student's text. This option reflects the common misconception that recording a definition is equivalent to learning a word. Writing words down does not produce the multiple meaningful encounters that word learning requires.","Quizzing students every Friday on the words in their notebooks":"Treating testing as a substitute for instruction, as though measuring retention would produce the learning being measured. Option (D) is incorrect. A weekly quiz measures what students remember but does nothing to create the encounters through which they would learn the words in the first place. This option reflects the common misconception that testing can substitute for teaching. Assessment reports on learning; it does not generate it."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-054","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Which practice most effectively builds students' academic and domain-specific vocabulary over the course of a unit?","options":["Posting the unit's words on a word wall at the start of the unit","Having students look up unfamiliar words on their own as they meet them while reading","Introducing each target word once, with a clear definition, before students read","Providing multiple encounters with each target word across speaking, listening, reading, and writing"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how vocabulary knowledge is built and why it matters for comprehension. Word knowledge deepens across encounters, and moving a word through talk, listening, reading, and writing forces students to meet it in different contexts and to use it actively rather than merely recognize it. That is what turns a word a student has heard into a word the student can read and think with, which is what comprehension runs on.","dex":{"Posting the unit's words on a word wall at the start of the unit":"Confusing the display of words with the teaching of words, treating visibility as if it produced processing. Option (C) is incorrect. A word wall displays the words and reminds students of them, but display alone does not require anyone to process meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that making words visible is the same as teaching them. Environmental print supports instruction that is already happening; it does not deliver that instruction.","Having students look up unfamiliar words on their own as they meet them while reading":"Assuming that vocabulary is acquired incidentally through wide reading, so that direct instruction of academic and domain-specific words is unnecessary. Option (D) is incorrect. Left on their own, students often skip unfamiliar words, choose the wrong sense from a dictionary, or give up on the text. This option reflects the common misconception that wide reading by itself builds the academic vocabulary students need. Incidental learning from context is slow and unreliable, and it is least reliable for the students who start with the smallest vocabularies.","Introducing each target word once, with a clear definition, before students read":"Treating preteaching as a one-time event of giving a definition, when one exposure cannot build durable word knowledge. Option (A) is incorrect. A single clear introduction is a useful beginning, but one exposure rarely produces a word a student can access while reading independently. This option reflects the common misconception that preteaching vocabulary consists of defining a word once. Preteaching opens a word's instructional life; it does not complete it."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-055","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Ms. Ortiz is choosing a short informational text about how seeds travel for her second graders to read and reread. She wants the text and its features to support both fluency development and vocabulary development. Which features should she look for when evaluating the text? Select all that apply.","options":["Sentence structures with punctuation and phrasing cues that invite expressive reading","Repeated use of a few important academic and domain-specific words across the text","Illustrations and labels that reinforce the meanings of key words","Long, embedded sentences with few visual supports so that students rely on print alone","Many unfamiliar words in every paragraph to maximize the number of new words encountered"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how the features of a text support particular instructional goals. Options (A), (B), and (C) are correct. Sentences that cue natural phrasing give students something concrete to imitate during rereading, and rereading with a model is where prosody is built.  [B] Repeating a small set of academic and domain-specific words gives students several encounters with the same words in varied contexts, which is how word meanings deepen, and it keeps the vocabulary load small enough that attention can go to meaning.  [C] Labels and illustrations tied to the key words give students a second route to meaning while their reading is still slow, so vocabulary can grow at the same time that fluency practice is happening.","dex":{"Long, embedded sentences with few visual supports so that students rely on print alone":"Equating text difficulty with instructional value, so that removing supports is mistaken for raising rigor. Option (D) is incorrect. Long embedded sentences with little support consume the attention a developing reader needs for recognizing words, leaving nothing for phrasing or for meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that more difficult text produces more growth. Texts chosen for fluency practice should be readable enough that students can work on how they read, not only on what the words are.","Many unfamiliar words in every paragraph to maximize the number of new words encountered":"Counting unfamiliar words on the page as vocabulary learned, when high density prevents both decoding and comprehension. Option (E) is incorrect. A heavy load of unfamiliar words breaks down decoding and meaning construction at the same time, so students finish the passage having learned few words and understood little. This option reflects the common misconception that the number of new words on the page equals the amount of vocabulary learned. Density of unfamiliar words works against the learning it is supposed to produce."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-fluency-056","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A first grader reads a familiar passage quickly but often substitutes words that fit the meaning, such as reading \"house\" for \"home.\" Which instructional response best addresses this pattern?","options":["Teaching the student to check whether the word read matches the letters on the page before moving on","Moving the student to a harder text so that more challenging words build vocabulary","Having the student work on expression, since the substitutions show that meaning is being monitored","Encouraging the student to read a little faster so that the substitutions disrupt the meaning less"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how accuracy is taught within oral reading fluency. Substitutions that make sense show that the student is predicting from meaning rather than working through the print, so the remedy is to make the print the check: the student compares what was said against the letters and self-corrects. Teaching that checking routine builds accurate word recognition, which has to be in place before rate or expression can be worked on productively.","dex":{"Moving the student to a harder text so that more challenging words build vocabulary":"Attributing meaningful substitutions to a vocabulary gap and responding with harder text, which increases the guessing the teacher wants to reduce. Option (C) is incorrect. Harder text multiplies the number of words the student does not know and therefore the number of guesses the student will make. This option reflects the common misconception that substitutions come from a vocabulary gap and should be answered with more advanced text. The difficulty here is checking the print, and adding difficulty will not teach that skill.","Having the student work on expression, since the substitutions show that meaning is being monitored":"Reading meaningful substitutions as evidence of comprehension to be praised, rather than as evidence of inattention to print. Option (D) is incorrect. Expression work is appropriate once the student is reading the words that are actually on the page; practicing prosody on substituted text builds fluency over the wrong words. This option reflects the common misconception that meaningful substitutions are a sign of comprehension to be encouraged. They are a sign that the student is not attending closely to print.","Encouraging the student to read a little faster so that the substitutions disrupt the meaning less":"Treating rate as the primary target of fluency instruction and pushing speed before the student reads the words accurately. Option (A) is incorrect. More speed leaves the student less time to look at the letters and typically produces more guessing, not less. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency means reading quickly and that rate should be pushed first. Accuracy is the foundation of fluency, and pushing rate before accuracy entrenches the guessing habit."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-057","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fourth grader reads the sentence \"The scientist's hypothesis was not supported, so she revised it.\" Asked what hypothesis means, the student says, \"It's what you think will happen before you test it, because she had to change it after the test.\" Which best describes the strategy the student used?","options":["Morphological analysis, because the student broke the word into its Greek roots","Context and syntax, because the student used the sentence's cause-and-effect structure to infer a meaning","Prior knowledge, because the student already knew about experiments and ignored the text","Substitution, because the student put in a word that sounded right without checking it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how readers infer the meanings of unfamiliar words. The student reads the cause-and-effect relation across the two clauses, notices that the hypothesis was revised after the test, and concludes that it must be the idea formed beforehand. The explanation cites evidence from the sentence, which is what distinguishes a strategic inference from a guess.","dex":{"Morphological analysis, because the student broke the word into its Greek roots":"Assuming that any correct inference about an academic word came from analyzing its word parts, regardless of the evidence the student actually cited. Option (A) is incorrect. The student's explanation never refers to word parts, roots, or affixes, so morphology took no part in the reasoning. This option reflects the common misconception that a successful inference about an academic word must have been morphological. Words of Greek or Latin origin invite that assumption, but the evidence this student used came from the sentence.","Prior knowledge, because the student already knew about experiments and ignored the text":"Attributing a text-based inference to background knowledge whenever the topic is familiar, which hides the strategy the student actually used. Option (C) is incorrect. The student explicitly grounds the inference in the sentence rather than in outside knowledge, pointing to what had to be revised after the test. This option reflects the common misconception that a sensible inference about a familiar topic must have come from background knowledge. Attributing it to prior knowledge would tell the teacher that no strategy instruction is needed, which the evidence does not support.","Substitution, because the student put in a word that sounded right without checking it":"Dismissing every context-based inference as an unverified guess, and missing the difference between substituting and checking. Option (D) is incorrect. A substitution becomes a guess only when the reader does not check it, and this student supplies a reason tied to the text and produces a meaning consistent with it. This option reflects the common misconception that any inference is merely a plausible substitution. Checking the candidate meaning against the surrounding text is exactly what makes the strategy work."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-058","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A second grader reads grade-level text slowly and word by word, with flat phrasing. Which instructional strategies are appropriate for supporting this student's fluency? Select all that apply.","options":["Repeated reading of a short passage until it can be read accurately and with phrasing","Teacher modeling of phrased reading followed by the student's echo reading","Practice with high-frequency words and common word parts to build automatic recognition","Moving the student to more difficult text so the student meets a wider range of words","Having the student read silently for longer stretches instead of reading aloud with feedback"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how fluency is taught at the phoneme, word, and passage levels. Options (A), (B), and (C) are correct. Repeated reading builds the automatized recognition and phrasing that word-by-word reading lacks, and using a short passage keeps the task within reach.  [B] A model followed by echo reading gives the student a concrete target for phrasing at the passage level, which is the level where flat, word-by-word reading shows up most clearly.  [C] Practice with high-frequency words and common word parts attacks the word-level recognition problem that is producing the stops in the first place, so it addresses the cause rather than the symptom.","dex":{"Moving the student to more difficult text so the student meets a wider range of words":"Assuming that harder text speeds fluency growth, when unreadable text reinforces the very habits being treated. Option (D) is incorrect. Harder text increases the number of words the student must work out, which slows reading further and reinforces word-by-word habits. This option reflects the common misconception that challenging text accelerates fluency growth. Fluency develops on text the student can read with support, not on text that defeats the strategies being taught.","Having the student read silently for longer stretches instead of reading aloud with feedback":"Treating accumulated independent silent reading time as the engine of fluency growth, when feedback on oral reading is what makes change possible. Option (E) is incorrect. Silent reading removes the model, the feedback, and the chance to hear and adjust phrasing, and it leaves the teacher with no information about how the student is reading. This option reflects the common misconception that fluency is built by accumulating independent reading time alone. Oral reading with a model and feedback is what makes fluency visible and therefore teachable."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-fluency-059","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"A fourth-grade teacher writes these sentences on the board: \"The wind blew.\" \"The wind was cold.\" \"The wind rattled the windows.\" She asks students to combine them into a single sentence. What does this activity primarily develop?","options":["Students' ability to read aloud with appropriate expression","Students' ability to decode unfamiliar multisyllabic words","Students' understanding of how sentence structure can be manipulated to combine ideas","Students' knowledge of irregular plural spellings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how the structure of written language is taught. To combine the sentences, students must decide which idea becomes the main clause, which are reduced to modifiers, and what punctuation joins them, so the task puts syntax into their hands rather than in front of them as a rule to memorize. Because the meaning is already built, they can concentrate on the structural choices.","dex":{"Students' ability to read aloud with appropriate expression":"Labeling any activity involving sentences as fluency practice, when the task asks students to construct structure rather than to perform it. Option (D) is incorrect. Students may eventually read their combined sentences aloud more smoothly, but expression is a by-product rather than the object of the lesson. This option reflects the common misconception that any sentence-level activity counts as fluency practice. The work students do here is on constructing structure, not on performing it.","Students' ability to decode unfamiliar multisyllabic words":"Treating any sentence-level task as decoding practice, and missing that the work here is on structure rather than on sounding out words. Option (A) is incorrect. All three sentences are made of words the students can already read, so no decoding is required. This option reflects the common misconception that any work with printed sentences is decoding work. The activity operates on how words are arranged, not on how individual words are sounded out.","Students' knowledge of irregular plural spellings":"Reducing written language structure to spelling and conventions, so that syntactic work is overlooked entirely. Option (C) is incorrect. Plural spellings are incidental here, and none of the sentences turns on them. This option reflects the common misconception that instruction in written language is mainly a matter of spelling and other conventions. Conventions are one part of written structure, but this task is about how clauses are built and joined."}},{"id":"8006-fluency-060","code":"8006","subtest":"Fluency and Vocabulary","q":"Several third graders who are multilingual learners use words such as \"harbor\" and \"current\" correctly when they talk about their own neighborhoods, but they do not select those words correctly on a standardized vocabulary check. What is the most reasonable interpretation of this pattern?","options":["The students are guessing, because conversational use is not evidence of word knowledge.","The check is invalid and should be removed from the students' records.","The students have a vocabulary deficit that requires intensive remediation.","The check may not be capturing knowledge the students demonstrate in other settings, so the teacher should gather additional evidence."],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how assessment information is interpreted for students whose experiences differ. Correct use in conversation is genuine evidence of word knowledge, so when one measure disagrees with it, the reasonable reading is that the measure samples a narrower slice of what the students know. Gathering further evidence through observation, discussion, and classroom tasks before drawing conclusions is what keeps a single score from being mistaken for the whole picture.","dex":{"The students are guessing, because conversational use is not evidence of word knowledge.":"Treating only decontextualized test performance as valid evidence, and rejecting appropriate spontaneous use as mere guessing. Option (C) is incorrect. Using a word appropriately in spontaneous speech about a familiar topic is among the strongest available evidence of word knowledge. This option reflects the common misconception that only decontextualized test performance counts as knowing a word. Dismissing the conversational evidence would discard the most informative data the teacher has.","The check is invalid and should be removed from the students' records.":"Jumping from \"this one measure looks wrong here\" to \"the measure is invalid,\" when the right response is to add evidence rather than delete it. Option (D) is incorrect. A measure can be limited for one purpose without being worthless; the check may still contribute useful information when combined with other sources. This option reflects the common misconception that a measure showing a discrepancy must be entirely invalid. The appropriate response is to triangulate with further evidence, not to discard a data source.","The students have a vocabulary deficit that requires intensive remediation.":"Treating a low score on a single measure as proof of a deficit within the student, regardless of contradicting evidence from other settings. Option (A) is incorrect. Calling this a deficit would require evidence that the students lack the words, and their spontaneous conversational use is evidence against it. This option reflects the common misconception that a low score on one measure settles the question of what a student knows. Reading a difference in performance across settings as a deficit inside the student is exactly the error to avoid."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-001","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"After reading a short informational article, a fourth-grade student writes, \"The article is about penguins and how they stay warm. It says they huddle together and have a layer of fat.\" Which next step best helps the student strengthen this summary?","options":["Asking the student to locate a sentence in the article that supports the claim about huddling","Asking the student to copy the article's section headings into the summary","Asking the student to rewrite the summary using more vivid adjectives","Asking the student to read a second article about penguins and compare the two"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to support students in identifying central ideas and key details while citing specific evidence. The student has produced a reasonable central idea and two supporting details, so the next instructional move is to connect those details back to the text. Having the student locate and cite the sentence that supports the huddling claim develops the habit of grounding a summary in evidence rather than recollection.","dex":{"Asking the student to copy the article's section headings into the summary":"Confusing a structural feature such as a heading with textual evidence. Words lifted from the article are not automatically support for a particular claim. Section headings are structural features that label the organization of a text. They are not evidence for a specific claim, so copying them adds labels without strengthening the link between the student's summary and the content that supports it.","Asking the student to rewrite the summary using more vivid adjectives":"Confusing a matter of style with a matter of evidence, treating weak expression as the problem when the missing support is. The summary's weakness is the absence of textual support, not a lack of vivid language. Adding adjectives changes the style while leaving the actual instructional gap untouched.","Asking the student to read a second article about penguins and compare the two":"Confusing extension into a new skill with consolidation of the one still developing. The student has not yet secured the step this task would skip past. Comparing two texts is a worthwhile skill, but it introduces a new and more complex task instead of consolidating the one the student is still developing. It moves past citing evidence rather than strengthening it."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-002","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A teacher is planning a unit on opinion writing for third-grade students. Which of the following instructional components should be included in the unit? Select all that apply.","options":["Teaching students to state a clear opinion about a topic","Teaching students to support the opinion with reasons","Teaching students to organize the piece as a chronological retelling of personal events","Teaching students to describe a setting using sensory details","Teaching students to link reasons to the opinion using words such as \"because\""],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] A clear, stated position is the defining feature of opinion writing. Without an identifiable opinion, a piece cannot function as opinion writing regardless of how well the rest is executed, so teaching students to state one explicitly is a core component of the unit.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how to teach the common writing genres. Reasons are what distinguish opinion writing from a bare statement of preference, because a claim without support gives the reader no basis for accepting it. Instruction in generating and selecting reasons that actually connect to the stated opinion is therefore central to the genre, not an optional embellishment.  [E] Linking words such as \"because\" and \"for example\" make the relationship between the opinion and its reasons visible to the reader. Teaching students to use them is a standard component of opinion writing instruction.","dex":{"Teaching students to organize the piece as a chronological retelling of personal events":"Confusing the time-order structure of narrative with the claim-and-support structure of opinion writing. Each genre is held together by its own organizing principle. A chronological retelling of personal events is the structure of narrative writing. Its organizing principle is time sequence rather than claim and support, so it belongs to a different genre and would not serve the purposes of an opinion unit.","Teaching students to describe a setting using sensory details":"Confusing the aim of description, which is to let a reader picture a scene, with the aim of opinion writing, which is to win agreement. Strong writing elements do not transfer across genres unchanged. Describing a setting with sensory details is characteristic of descriptive writing. Its purpose is to help a reader imagine a scene, not to persuade a reader of a position, so it does not belong in a unit on opinion writing."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,4]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-003","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A teacher wants students to monitor their own comprehension while they read independently. Which practice most directly develops that ability?","options":["Having students complete a comprehension quiz after they finish the text","Teaching students to notice when meaning breaks down and to apply a fix-up strategy such as rereading","Asking students to summarize each paragraph after the teacher reads it aloud","Providing graphic organizers for students to complete while reading"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of metacognitive monitoring. Monitoring means noticing that comprehension has failed, identifying where and why, and applying a repair such as rereading or slowing down. Teaching this sequence explicitly is what makes students able to do it on their own.","dex":{"Having students complete a comprehension quiz after they finish the text":"Confusing assessment of the product with instruction in the process. A test locates the problem after the fact; monitoring prevents it. A quiz measures comprehension after reading ends. It tells the teacher whether understanding occurred but teaches the student nothing about what to do at the moment understanding fails.","Asking students to summarize each paragraph after the teacher reads it aloud":"Treating any comprehension strategy as equivalent to monitoring. Monitoring is specifically about detecting and repairing breakdown. Summarizing is a comprehension strategy, not a monitoring one, and because the teacher is reading aloud, students are not practicing the noticing that happens during their own silent reading.","Providing graphic organizers for students to complete while reading":"Mistaking a scaffold for the target skill. The goal is for students to monitor internally once the support is removed. A graphic organizer is an external scaffold that supports understanding while it is in place. It does not teach students to detect their own breakdowns, and the support disappears when the organizer does."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-004","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A teacher structures writing workshop so that students plan, draft, revise, and then edit each piece. Which principle best justifies this sequence?","options":["Assessing each stage gives the teacher more evidence of student progress","Editing is most effective when it is done last, after all content decisions are settled","Writing is a recursive process in which revision at the level of ideas should precede attention to surface conventions","Students should master sentence-level conventions before they attempt to compose longer texts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of writing as a recursive process. Reworking ideas, organization, and elaboration comes before proofreading, because polishing sentences that may be cut or reorganized wastes effort that belongs to revision first.","dex":{"Assessing each stage gives the teacher more evidence of student progress":"Justifying an instructional design on administrative grounds. The rationale should concern student learning. Collecting more evidence is an assessment convenience, not a principle of writing instruction, and it says nothing about what helps students write better.","Editing is most effective when it is done last, after all content decisions are settled":"Offering a restatement of the practice in place of the principle behind it. A justification has to say why the order works. This describes the sequence without giving the reason for it. Saying that editing comes last restates the arrangement rather than explaining why ideas must be settled before conventions are addressed.","Students should master sentence-level conventions before they attempt to compose longer texts":"Assuming skills must be mastered sequentially before authentic use. Writing development is recursive, and students write extended text from the start. Delaying composition until conventions are secure holds back writing development. Students learn conventions more meaningfully while composing real texts than in isolation from them."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-005","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A teacher wants to help students see that decoding and encoding are reciprocal skills. Which activity best demonstrates that connection?","options":["Having students practice spelling lists of words that follow the week's phonics pattern","Having students reread the same text three times to build fluency","Having students sort pictures by their initial sound","Having students read a decodable text and then write sentences using words from that text"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how decoding and encoding reinforce one another. Reading words from the text and then writing those same words moves students in both directions over identical spellings, making the print-to-sound and sound-to-print routes practice each other.","dex":{"Having students practice spelling lists of words that follow the week's phonics pattern":"Treating one half of a reciprocal relationship as if it were the whole. Encoding practice alone does not demonstrate that the two processes support each other. Spelling words that follow a pattern exercises encoding, but with no reading component the connection is never made explicit. Students may spell the words correctly and still not link them to the words they read.","Having students reread the same text three times to build fluency":"Selecting a fluency routine for a task that requires linking reading to writing. Fluency practice works within one direction only. Rereading targets automaticity and prosody in oral reading. It involves no writing and therefore cannot show students anything about encoding.","Having students sort pictures by their initial sound":"Choosing an activity at the wrong level of the literacy hierarchy. The target connection requires both reading and writing of real words. Sorting pictures by initial sound is a phonemic awareness task. It sits below decoding and well below encoding, so it cannot illustrate a relationship between them."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-006","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A teacher plans to use one grade-level informational text with a class that includes struggling readers, on-level readers, and English learners. Which approach best supports comprehension for all of them?","options":["Keeping the same text and goal for everyone while varying the scaffolding, such as graphic organizers, pretaught vocabulary, and guided discussion","Assigning a simplified version of the text to the struggling readers and the English learners","Reading the text aloud to the whole class before students read it independently","Letting students who find the text difficult skip it and answer the questions orally instead"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of differentiation in comprehension instruction. Differentiation adjusts the support, not the destination: all students work with the same grade-level text and the same comprehension goal, while scaffolds such as organizers, pretaught vocabulary, and guided discussion are varied to match each group's needs.","dex":{"Assigning a simplified version of the text to the struggling readers and the English learners":"Treating differentiation as changing the text instead of changing the support. Lowering the material lowers the ceiling on what students can reach. Substituting an easier text removes students from grade-level content and vocabulary. Over time this widens rather than closes the gap, because the students who most need exposure to academic language get the least of it.","Reading the text aloud to the whole class before students read it independently":"Assuming a whole-class routine counts as differentiation. Differentiation means varying instruction by need, not delivering one thing to all. A single read-aloud is one uniform support applied to everyone. It does not respond to the differing needs of the three groups and addresses only decoding and fluency, not the comprehension demands of the text.","Letting students who find the text difficult skip it and answer the questions orally instead":"Confusing lowering the task with supporting the learner. Removing the challenge prevents growth instead of enabling it. Removing the text removes the practice students need. Answering orally tests background knowledge rather than reading, so students never build the ability to handle grade-level text themselves."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-007","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A kindergarten teacher reads aloud from grade-level stories every day and pauses to ask students questions about the characters and the order of events. Which statement best explains why this practice supports reading comprehension?","options":["Listening comprehension is a separate strand that matters only before students can decode, after which it no longer contributes to reading","Listening comprehension and reading comprehension draw on largely the same meaning-making processes, so building understanding through read-alouds develops comprehension that transfers to print once decoding is in place","Hearing stories read aloud exposes students to vocabulary they cannot yet decode, and that exposure makes decoding those words easier later on","Listening carefully trains students to track print from left to right, and accurate tracking is the mechanism that produces comprehension"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how listening comprehension supports reading comprehension. Comprehending spoken language and comprehending written language call on the same underlying processes: constructing meaning, making inferences, and tracking how ideas connect. Because young students can understand language well beyond what they can decode, read-alouds let them practice those meaning-making processes at a higher level than their decoding allows, and that comprehension capacity is what they bring to text later.","dex":{"Listening comprehension is a separate strand that matters only before students can decode, after which it no longer contributes to reading":"Treats listening comprehension as a preschool-only scaffold that drops away once decoding is in place, missing that language comprehension remains the ceiling on reading comprehension. This reverses the relationship. Listening comprehension continues to support reading comprehension well after decoding is established, because once word recognition is automatic, comprehension of language becomes the main constraint on how well a student understands text. Read-alouds are therefore useful across grade levels, not only in the primary grades.","Hearing stories read aloud exposes students to vocabulary they cannot yet decode, and that exposure makes decoding those words easier later on":"Credits read-alouds with making words easier to decode, when the real payoff is to meaning and background knowledge; word recognition runs on a separate mechanism. Hearing rich vocabulary during read-alouds is genuinely valuable, but this option locates the benefit in decoding rather than in comprehension. Encountering a word in speech does not teach a child how to map its print form to its sounds; the lasting contribution is to meaning and background knowledge, which is a different mechanism entirely.","Listening carefully trains students to track print from left to right, and accurate tracking is the mechanism that produces comprehension":"Points to a visible print behavior, tracking left to right, as the source of comprehension, swapping an observable routine for the meaning construction that actually produces it. Tracking print is a concept about print that read-alouds can support, but it is not the mechanism by which listening builds comprehension. A student can track print perfectly and still construct no meaning from it, which shows that tracking is not what produces understanding."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-008","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A third-grade teacher stops during a read-aloud and asks, \"What do you think Marisol will do next, and what in the story makes you think that?\" Which comprehension strategy is the teacher primarily developing?","options":["Summarizing the events so far in order to identify the central idea","Activating prior knowledge by connecting the story to personal experience","Making predictions and supporting them with evidence drawn from the text","Monitoring comprehension by noticing where understanding has broken down"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how questioning develops comprehension strategies. The teacher's question has two parts, and the second part is the important one: students must do more than guess what happens next, they must point to something in the text that justifies the guess. That pairing of a forward-looking claim with textual support is what distinguishes prediction as a strategy from simple guessing.","dex":{"Summarizing the events so far in order to identify the central idea":"Seizes on the one thing summarizing and prediction share, deciding what matters, and lets that resemblance hide the fact that the two run in opposite directions. Summarizing condenses what has already happened into a main idea, which is a retrospective move. Asking what a character will do next points ahead into unread text, so the two strategies run in opposite directions even though both require identifying what matters.","Activating prior knowledge by connecting the story to personal experience":"Reads the required support as background knowledge or personal experience, sliding past the stem's restriction that the evidence come from the story itself. Connecting to personal experience draws on knowledge the reader brings to the text. The teacher's question explicitly asks for support located in the story, which is a different source of evidence. A prediction justified by personal experience rather than by text evidence is not what this question calls for.","Monitoring comprehension by noticing where understanding has broken down":"Collapses prediction into monitoring, overlooking that one projects forward into unread text while the other turns inward to catch a failure already underway. Monitoring is a look-backward, inward strategy: the reader notices that meaning has failed and applies a fix. The teacher's question is look-forward and asks for textual evidence, so it does not require students to notice a breakdown in their own understanding."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-009","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher asks students to rewrite a scene from a story using the voice of a different character. Which understanding of literary devices does this activity best develop?","options":["Figurative language such as simile and metaphor creates vivid images that make description more interesting","Setting establishes when and where events occur and can be changed without altering a character's motivation","Character development is shown mainly through physical description rather than through what a character thinks","Point of view determines which events and thoughts the reader has access to, so changing the narrator changes the meaning of the scene"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how point of view functions as a literary device. Because a narrator can report only what that character perceives, knows, and cares about, the same events told from another position will include different facts and carry different weight. Rewriting the scene forces students to discover this directly: the events stay put while the meaning shifts, which is exactly the point the activity is designed to teach.","dex":{"Figurative language such as simile and metaphor creates vivid images that make description more interesting":"Hears a change of narrator as a matter of wording and imagery, missing that point of view is a structural determinant of what the reader can know. Figurative language is a real literary device, but it is not what changes when the narrator changes. A student could rewrite the scene from another character's perspective using plain literal language throughout, and the exercise would still work, which shows that imagery is not the operative device here.","Setting establishes when and where events occur and can be changed without altering a character's motivation":"Relegates setting to inert backdrop, ignoring how strongly where and when events unfold constrains what a character can want or do. Setting is a structural element, but the activity does not relocate or redate the scene. More importantly, the claim that setting can be changed without affecting motivation is itself questionable, since where and when events occur routinely shapes what characters want and can do.","Character development is shown mainly through physical description rather than through what a character thinks":"Narrows character development to physical description, overlooking a character's thoughts, speech, and choices, the routes authors chiefly rely on to reveal character. Physical description is the least reliable route to character development, and the activity asks students to work with a character's voice and thoughts. Rewriting from another position requires attending to what that character notices and believes, which is internal rather than descriptive."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-010","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"While teaching a play, a teacher has students examine stage directions and dialogue to track how one character changes from the opening scene to the final scene. This activity primarily helps students understand that","options":["in drama, character development is conveyed mainly through what characters say and do, and that development carries much of the work's meaning","stage directions tell actors where to stand and have little bearing on a reader's interpretation of the play","setting in drama is established by the scenery rather than by what the characters say","plays are written to be performed, so their meaning cannot be studied by reading them"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how structural elements of a genre contribute to meaning. Drama has no narrator who can report a character's thoughts, so change over time must be carried entirely by dialogue and by the actions recorded in stage directions. Tracking a character across scenes shows students that these elements are not decoration: they are the only evidence the genre provides, and therefore the place where meaning is made.","dex":{"stage directions tell actors where to stand and have little bearing on a reader's interpretation of the play":"Dismisses stage directions as technical notes for actors, discarding the only printed cue a reader has for tone, gesture, and silence. Stage directions do guide performance, but they also record tone, gesture, and silence, all of which a reader must interpret. Treating them as technical instructions for actors strips the reader of evidence that the genre deliberately supplies.","setting in drama is established by the scenery rather than by what the characters say":"Equates setting with scenery, a production decision, rather than with the verbal cues in dialogue and stage directions that establish place and time for a reader. Scenery is a production decision, not a feature of the script a reader holds. In a play's text, place and time are established through what characters say and through the stage directions, so the dialogue is precisely where the setting lives for a reader.","plays are written to be performed, so their meaning cannot be studied by reading them":"Ties a play's meaning to performance, denying that the script works as a text to be read and studied, which is exactly what the classroom activity does. Performance is one realization of a play, not a precondition for understanding it. Readers routinely study scripts and reach well-supported interpretations, and the activity described here is built entirely on reading rather than on staging."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-011","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade class will read a demanding informational article about the water cycle. Which of the following practices would best support the students' comprehension of that text? Select all that apply.","options":["Preteaching a small set of academic terms such as condensation and evaporation before students read","Leading a brief discussion of the key concept that water changes state but is not lost","Having students turn each heading into a question and check it as they read","Assigning the article as homework so students can work at their own pace without time pressure","Giving students a simpler article on a related topic in place of the assigned text"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how to support students with complex text. Preteaching a small, carefully chosen set of academic terms removes word-level obstacles before they arise, so students can spend their attention on the ideas the text builds rather than stalling on unfamiliar labels. The key word is small: preteaching everything would leave no strategy for the words that were not covered.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how to support students with complex text. Establishing the key concept beforehand gives students a framework to attach details to as they read, which is what allows them to hold the text together. Without that framework, accurate facts about evaporation and condensation remain unconnected.  [C] The question requires an understanding of how to support students with complex text. Converting headings into questions gives students a purpose for reading each section and a self-check when it ends, which sustains attention across a text longer than they could hold in one pass. It also makes the text's own structure do some of the work.","dex":{"Assigning the article as homework so students can work at their own pace without time pressure":"Blames the difficulty on time pressure and answers with pace and setting instead, leaving the technical vocabulary and unfamiliar concepts untouched. Removing time pressure does nothing about the sources of difficulty in this text, which are technical vocabulary and an unfamiliar conceptual framework. A student reading alone at home has less support, not more, and any confusion goes unnoticed until the next day.","Giving students a simpler article on a related topic in place of the assigned text":"Confuses swapping in an easier text with scaffolding one; substitution removes the demanding text rather than supporting students through grade-level content. Substituting an easier text avoids the difficulty rather than scaffolding it, and students never gain access to the grade-level ideas and vocabulary the assignment was chosen to teach. Scaffolding keeps the demanding text and adds support; substitution removes the text."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-012","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A second-grade student retells a story by listing many small events but leaves out the problem the main character faced. Which instruction would best help the student?","options":["Asking the student to copy the first and last sentence of each page as evidence","Teaching the student to use a retelling frame that asks who the character was, what the problem was, and how it was solved","Encouraging the student to include more descriptive details about the setting and the characters","Having the student reread the story to build fluency so that fewer details are lost along the way"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach students to identify central ideas and key details. The student's difficulty is not recall but selection: the events are there, and what is missing is a sense of which ones carry the story. A retelling frame supplies the structure that distinguishes load-bearing events from incidental ones, and once the student can name the problem, the small details have something to attach to.","dex":{"Asking the student to copy the first and last sentence of each page as evidence":"Reduces citing evidence to copying whatever sits at a fixed spot on the page, skipping the judgment about which parts actually support a point. Citing evidence means selecting the parts of the text that support a point, not transcribing whatever sits at fixed positions on the page. Copying first and last sentences trains a positional habit and leaves the judgment of relevance unexercised.","Encouraging the student to include more descriptive details about the setting and the characters":"Diagnoses the thin retelling as a shortage of details, when what the student lacks is a way to rank events by importance. The student already produces too many details, so adding more makes the retelling longer without making it more coherent. The gap is hierarchical, not quantitative: the student has no way to rank details by importance.","Having the student reread the story to build fluency so that fewer details are lost along the way":"Attributes a problem of selection and organization to weak fluency, sending rate practice after a difficulty that is really one of judgment. The student recalls the events well enough to list many of them, so the breakdown is not one of retrieval while reading. Rereading for fluency targets rate and accuracy, and neither of those determines whether a reader can identify what matters."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-013","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Ms. Okonjo teaches a combined second- and third-grade class. Her students read the same grade-level folktale, but several multilingual learners and students with reading difficulties struggle to identify the central idea. She prepares a graphic organizer with fewer boxes and sentence starters for those students while other groups use a blank organizer. Which of the following best describes the purpose of creating two versions of the organizer?","options":["It lets the teacher identify which students can complete work without help","It reduces how much students must read, leaving more time for class discussion","It adjusts the amount of scaffolding so that every student works toward the same comprehension goal at a level of support each can manage","It lowers the comprehension expectation for struggling students so they are not held to grade-level standards"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to modify graphic representations to differentiate instruction. Both versions of the organizer ask students to do the same cognitive work, which is to identify the central idea of the same grade-level folktale. What differs is how much of the surrounding structure the teacher supplies. Sentence starters and fewer boxes reduce the organizational and language load, so students who would otherwise spend their attention on figuring out what the task wants can spend it on the comprehension goal itself.","dex":{"It lets the teacher identify which students can complete work without help":"Recasts a support tool as a sorting device, as though the point of two versions were to see who manages without help. Observing who needs help is something a teacher can learn from almost any task, and it is not why the two versions exist. The purpose of the adaptation is to make the comprehension goal reachable during the lesson, not to sort students by independence afterward.","It reduces how much students must read, leaving more time for class discussion":"Assumes differentiation means less reading and more time to talk, when the organizer acts on how students process the text, not on how much of it they read. Neither version changes how much text students read, since both groups read the same folktale. The organizer works on what students do with the text after reading, so the claim about saving reading time misdescribes what was actually modified.","It lowers the comprehension expectation for struggling students so they are not held to grade-level standards":"Takes added scaffolding as a lowered expectation, when the adapted organizer changes the amount of support, not the grade-level comprehension goal. This confuses the level of support with the level of the task. Every student here is working on the same text and the same standard; the adapted organizer changes how much structure is provided, not what students are expected to understand. Differentiation that lowers the goal rather than the support leaves struggling readers permanently behind grade-level content."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-014","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher models reading by stopping at the end of a paragraph and saying, \"I just realized I cannot explain what happened in that paragraph, so I am going to go back and reread it.\" Which student behavior is the teacher most likely trying to develop?","options":["Reading more slowly so that fewer details are missed the first time","Answering questions about the text accurately after reading","Predicting what will happen next in the passage","Noticing when comprehension has broken down and applying a fix-up strategy such as rereading"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to model metacognitive monitoring. Metacognition has two parts, and the teacher's think-aloud performs both in sequence: first she registers that meaning has failed, then she selects a repair. Making that normally invisible inner speech audible is what lets students acquire it, since evaluating and repairing are precisely the parts of reading that leave no visible trace.","dex":{"Reading more slowly so that fewer details are missed the first time":"Flattens metacognitive monitoring into a blanket habit of reading slowly, replacing notice-then-repair with a pace adjustment applied before anything goes wrong. Reading slowly is a blanket adjustment applied before any problem is detected, so it requires no monitoring at all. The think-aloud is the opposite: she reads normally, notices a specific failure, and responds to it. A student who simply reads slowly has no way of knowing when to slow down.","Answering questions about the text accurately after reading":"Substitutes the visible outcome, right answers, for the invisible process of monitoring; a student can score well without ever noticing confusion. Getting questions right is an outcome that may follow from good monitoring, but it is not the behavior itself. A student can answer correctly without ever noticing confusion, and a student who notices every breakdown may still miss items. The think-aloud targets the process, not the score.","Predicting what will happen next in the passage":"Confuses prediction with monitoring, though one looks ahead at unread text and the other looks back at a failure that has already occurred. Prediction looks forward at unread text, while the teacher's comment looks backward at text already read and found wanting. The two strategies also respond to different problems: prediction builds anticipation, whereas monitoring responds to a failure that has already occurred."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-015","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A teacher plans writing instruction that allocates time each week to handwriting and spelling practice, to generating and organizing ideas, and to planning and revising extended pieces. Which statement best explains why this range of components is necessary?","options":["Writing draws on transcription, text generation, and self-regulation at the same time, so each must be taught explicitly rather than assumed to develop from reading alone","Writing is the reverse of reading, so students who read well will become strong writers without separate writing instruction","Handwriting and spelling must be fully automatic before students are asked to generate any ideas in writing","Planning and revising are best postponed to the upper grades, once students have something worth saying"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of systematic, explicit writing instruction. The Not So Simple View of Writing holds that competent writing demands transcription, text generation, and executive self-regulation simultaneously, and that these compete for the same limited working memory. Because the components are partly independent, strength in one does not produce the others, which is why each needs its own instructional time rather than being left to develop incidentally.","dex":{"Writing is the reverse of reading, so students who read well will become strong writers without separate writing instruction":"Pictures writing as reading mirrored, assuming ability transfers on its own and that composition therefore needs no explicit instruction of its own. Reading and writing share knowledge, but the relationship is not symmetrical and the transfer is not automatic. Many students comprehend well above the level at which they can compose, because transcription and the executive demands of composing are not exercised by reading. Assuming transfer is exactly what leaves writing undertaught.","Handwriting and spelling must be fully automatic before students are asked to generate any ideas in writing":"Makes transcription a strict prerequisite for composing, a sequence that would keep beginning writers from writing at all for years. Delaying all composing until transcription is flawless would keep beginning writers from writing for years and would stall the development of text generation. In practice the components develop together, with instruction temporarily reducing the transcription load so that ideas can be generated at all.","Planning and revising are best postponed to the upper grades, once students have something worth saying":"Defers planning and revising to the upper grades as a luxury, mistaking having something to say for the process that teaches students to say it well. Planning and revising are demanding precisely because they call on executive function, which is why they need early, supported practice rather than later introduction. Waiting until students have something worth saying mistakes the product for the process: students learn to have something worth saying by planning and revising."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-016","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Midway through a unit, Ms. Reyes has each student complete a one-minute oral reading sample and a brief written retell. She uses the results to regroup students for the following week. How should these assessments be used?","options":["As a screening tool for deciding which students should be referred for special education evaluation","As formative information for adjusting grouping and instruction, interpreted alongside students' backgrounds and other evidence rather than as a final judgment of ability","As a formal measure for placing students into ability groups that stay fixed for the rest of the year","As the sole basis for reporting each student's reading level to families"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how assessment guides and differentiates instruction. A one-minute sample and a short retell are quick, narrow, and easily affected by the day, which makes them useful for deciding what to teach next week and unsuited to conclusions about a student's capacity. Reading them alongside students' linguistic and cultural experiences and other evidence is what keeps a brief snapshot from hardening into a verdict.","dex":{"As a screening tool for deciding which students should be referred for special education evaluation":"Promotes routine classroom monitoring to a special education screening decision, blurring the line between instructional adjustment and a formal referral process. A referral rests on a documented pattern of insufficient response to effective instruction gathered through a formal process, not on a single classroom sample. Treating routine monitoring as a screening decision confuses everyday instructional adjustment with a special education procedure.","As a formal measure for placing students into ability groups that stay fixed for the rest of the year":"Turns formative progress-monitoring data into a permanent placement decision, using a measure meant to move instruction to lock groups in place. Progress-monitoring data are meant to be revisited, and that is incompatible with groups that never change. Fixed placement also treats a single short sample as a stable trait, when the whole point of monitoring is that instruction should respond as performance shifts.","As the sole basis for reporting each student's reading level to families":"Offers one narrow sample as a full account of a student's reading to families, skipping the triangulation such a report demands. Reporting to families calls for a fuller picture than two brief measures can provide, and families are entitled to evidence that reflects a student's range rather than one minute of performance. Using a narrow sample alone would likely misrepresent students, especially those whose oral reading is stronger or weaker than their comprehension."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-017","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A third-grade teacher wants students to treat writing as a recursive process rather than as a single draft. Which of the following classroom practices would best support that goal? Select all that apply.","options":["Conferring with students between drafts and asking each to make one meaningful change based on the conference","Having students use a simple checklist to evaluate their own writing against the criteria before turning it in","Assigning a different writing topic each day so that students get practice across many genres","Collecting papers at the end of the period and returning them the following week with a grade"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how to teach writing as a recursive process. A conference held between drafts puts feedback where it can still be used, and asking for one meaningful change keeps the revision substantive rather than cosmetic. The student then experiences drafting, response, and revision as one continuous activity instead of as three separate events.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how to teach writing as a recursive process. A criteria-based checklist hands the evaluative move to the student, who checks the draft against the target and decides what to fix before anyone else responds. That self-evaluation is what makes revision something a writer initiates rather than something a teacher requests.","dex":{"Assigning a different writing topic each day so that students get practice across many genres":"Mistakes volume and genre variety for process writing, when recursion means returning to the same draft instead of always starting the next one. Moving to a new topic each day increases the volume of writing but guarantees that no piece is ever revisited. Recursion means returning to the same draft, so a schedule that always moves forward teaches the opposite habit.","Collecting papers at the end of the period and returning them the following week with a grade":"Counts delayed grading as evidence of a process approach, missing that feedback arriving after the piece is closed leaves no room to revise. A grade returned a week later closes the piece before any revision can happen, and by then the student has moved on. What makes a process recursive is feedback arriving while the draft is still open to change, which delayed grading removes."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-018","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fifth-grade teacher is introducing opinion writing. Which instructional approach best reflects how this genre is typically taught?","options":["Teaching students to organize information by topic using headings and other text features","Teaching students to describe a person or place with vivid adjectives and precise nouns","Teaching students to state a claim, support it with reasons and evidence, and acknowledge a counterclaim","Teaching students to present events in time order using sensory details and dialogue"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how common writing genres are taught. Opinion writing is organized around a position rather than around time or topic, so its structure is a claim, the reasons and evidence that support it, and a response to what someone might argue on the other side. Teaching the counterclaim is what separates opinion writing from a bare list of reasons, and that is why it belongs in the genre's instruction.","dex":{"Teaching students to organize information by topic using headings and other text features":"Imports the architecture of informative writing, headings and all, into a genre that exists to persuade rather than to explain a topic. Headings and text features belong to informative writing, where the purpose is to convey information about a topic. Opinion writing does use evidence, but it selects and arranges that evidence to support a position rather than to cover a subject.","Teaching students to describe a person or place with vivid adjectives and precise nouns":"Reaches for the word-choice tools of description and offers them as genre structure, though a richly worded passage can still take no position. Vivid adjectives and precise nouns are word-choice tools that serve descriptive writing and are useful in any genre. They are not genre structure: a passage can be richly described and still take no position, which is the defining requirement here.","Teaching students to present events in time order using sensory details and dialogue":"Borrows narrative's structure for opinion writing, trading time order, sensory detail, and dialogue for a genre whose purpose is arguing a position. Chronological order, sensory detail, and dialogue are the tools of narrative writing, where the purpose is to recount events. An opinion piece organized as a timeline would have no place for a claim or for reasons, which are the elements that do the work in this genre."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-019","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"During word study, Mr. Ellis has students use letter tiles to build words ending in -ed, then read each word aloud and record it in a writing notebook for use later that day. Which analysis best explains why this routine supports both reading and writing?","options":["Manipulating tiles adds a hands-on component that helps students memorize the spellings of individual words","Recording words in a notebook builds students' store of sight words, which makes reading more automatic","Reading words aloud builds oral reading fluency, and that fluency is what allows students to spell accurately","Building the word requires mapping phonemes to graphemes, and reading it back runs that same mapping in reverse, so one body of letter-sound knowledge is exercised in both directions"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how decoding and encoding function as reciprocal skills. Building a word with tiles forces the student to analyze the spoken word into phonemes and select a grapheme for each one; reading it back forces the reverse operation on the identical spelling. Because both directions draw on the same letter-sound knowledge, each one strengthens the representations the other depends on, which is why the routine develops reading and writing together rather than in sequence.","dex":{"Manipulating tiles adds a hands-on component that helps students memorize the spellings of individual words":"Locates the learning in the hands-on medium itself and the outcome in memorized spellings, when the value lies in transferable phoneme-grapheme mapping. The tiles are a convenient medium, but the learning comes from the phoneme-grapheme decisions students make while using them. Framing the benefit as memorizing individual spellings reduces a generative skill to rote recall and would not transfer to words never built with tiles.","Recording words in a notebook builds students' store of sight words, which makes reading more automatic":"Treats copying word lists as building a store of sight words, though these words are regular and being analyzed rather than recognized as wholes. Sight words are recognized as whole units and are typically irregular, whereas these words are regular and are being analyzed rather than memorized. Copying words into a notebook is transcription practice, not the mechanism that makes reading automatic.","Reading words aloud builds oral reading fluency, and that fluency is what allows students to spell accurately":"Makes fluent oral reading the foundation of spelling, inverting a reciprocal relationship in which both draw on the same letter-sound knowledge. This reverses the direction of the relationship. Fluent oral reading does not produce accurate spelling; accurate spelling rests on the same phoneme-grapheme knowledge that reading uses, and the routine is valuable because it exercises that shared knowledge from both sides."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-020","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fifth-grade teacher reads the opening section of a complex informational text aloud while students follow along silently in their own copies, then asks them to summarize what they heard. Which of the following best accounts for the benefit of pairing listening with the students' own reading?","options":["It lets students construct meaning from language they can understand but could not yet decode on their own, so comprehension work is not held back by word recognition","It models accurate decoding, and hearing the words pronounced correctly is what enables students to understand them","It frees students from having to monitor their own understanding, since the teacher's phrasing carries the meaning for them","It builds interest in the topic, and motivation rather than instruction is what carries students through difficult text"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how listening comprehension supports reading comprehension. Complex informational text often sits above students' decoding level but within their listening comprehension level, so hearing it lets them do genuine comprehension work on language they could not read independently. The teacher's pacing and phrasing supply the prosody that decoding would otherwise consume attention on, and the summarization afterward makes the meaning-building explicit.","dex":{"It models accurate decoding, and hearing the words pronounced correctly is what enables students to understand them":"Assumes accurate pronunciation delivers comprehension, collapsing word recognition into meaning-making as though saying a word supplied what it means. Hearing a word pronounced does supply its spoken form, but understanding depends on whether the student already knows what the word means. Decoding and comprehension are distinct: a student can pronounce every word correctly and still take no meaning from the sentence.","It frees students from having to monitor their own understanding, since the teacher's phrasing carries the meaning for them":"Reads teacher modeling as comprehension done for students, when support is meant to sustain their effort, not excuse them from making it. Fluent oral reading by the teacher does provide phrasing, but the point of the lesson is for students to build meaning themselves, which the summarization task requires them to do. Removing the need to monitor would remove the very comprehension work the routine is designed to produce.","It builds interest in the topic, and motivation rather than instruction is what carries students through difficult text":"Installs motivation as the mechanism carrying students through hard text, in place of instruction that actually builds their capacity to comprehend it. Interest in a topic is worth building, but it does not teach students how to summarize or how to hold a complex structure together. Treating motivation as the mechanism mistakes a condition that helps students engage for the instruction that makes them more capable."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-021","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fifth-grade teacher displays the sentence \"The porch light was still on, but no one answered the door\" and asks, \"What can you figure out here that the author never tells you?\" Which instructional purpose does this question best serve?","options":["It encourages students to evaluate whether the author's argument is convincing","It requires students to combine text evidence with prior knowledge to generate an inference","It asks students to locate explicitly stated information and restate it in their own words","It checks whether students understand the literal meanings of the words in the sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how questioning develops inference. An inference exists precisely where the text stops: the sentence gives a lit porch and an unanswered door, and the conclusion that someone was expected but is not coming comes from the reader's knowledge of how households work. Naming the gap with \"what the author never tells you\" is what pushes students past the literal level and makes the combining of evidence and prior knowledge explicit.","dex":{"It encourages students to evaluate whether the author's argument is convincing":"Carries over the evaluative moves suited to argument and applies them to a narrative sentence that takes no position at all. Evaluating an argument applies to texts that take a position and offer support, which a narrative sentence does not. The sentence sets a scene, so the appropriate move is to infer what is happening, not to judge a case.","It asks students to locate explicitly stated information and restate it in their own words":"Hears an inference question as locate-and-restate, dropping the phrase that decides it, what the author never tells you, and staying on the literal level. The question rules out this reading outright by specifying information the author never states. Restating what is on the page is a useful skill, but it works entirely within the literal level, whereas this question asks students to go beyond it.","It checks whether students understand the literal meanings of the words in the sentence":"Downgrades the question to a vocabulary check, substituting word-level confirmation for the combining of evidence and prior knowledge the task calls for. Every word in the sentence is common and its literal meaning is unlikely to be the obstacle. The cognitive work here is combining two stated facts into something unstated, which happens after word meanings are settled."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-022","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A second-grade student reads the sentence \"The wind whispered through the trees\" and says, \"That is silly. Wind cannot talk.\" Which teacher response best develops the student's understanding of figurative language?","options":["Telling the student that authors occasionally make mistakes and that this sentence should have been written differently","Rewording the sentence into plainer language and having the student reread the new version for fluency","Explaining that the author compares the sound of wind to a whisper to create a sensory image, then asking what feeling the sentence gives the reader","Confirming that the sentence is not literally true and telling the student to skip unusual phrasing and attend to the facts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach figurative language. The student's objection is actually a correct literal reading, so the productive move is to build on it: acknowledge that wind does not speak, then show that the comparison was chosen to make the reader hear and feel something specific. Asking about the resulting feeling extends the lesson from recognizing the device to understanding why an author reaches for it.","dex":{"Telling the student that authors occasionally make mistakes and that this sentence should have been written differently":"Reads the figurative phrasing as an author's slip, swapping error-hunting for an explanation of what the comparison actually accomplishes. The phrasing is deliberate, and calling it an error misrepresents how published text works while also explaining nothing. The student needs to learn what the comparison accomplishes, not to doubt the author's competence.","Rewording the sentence into plainer language and having the student reread the new version for fluency":"Sidesteps the device by substituting plainer words, then mislabels the residual task as fluency practice, so the object of the lesson disappears. Substituting plainer language removes the device instead of teaching it, and the fluency reread addresses rate rather than meaning. The student would practice reading a sentence that no longer contains the thing worth learning about.","Confirming that the sentence is not literally true and telling the student to skip unusual phrasing and attend to the facts":"Judges the sentence by whether it is literally true and advises skipping unusual phrasing, casting figurative language as an obstacle rather than a source of meaning. Directing the student to skip unusual phrasing teaches avoidance of exactly the language that carries much of a text's impact. The student would leave the exchange with the literal objection intact and no way to read the next figurative sentence encountered."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-023","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher reads a poem aloud and then asks students why the poet broke a line in the middle of a phrase. Which aspects of poetry does this question address? Select all that apply.","options":["How line breaks control pacing and emphasis for the reader","How the physical arrangement of words on the page contributes to meaning","How the rhyme scheme identifies which genre a poem belongs to","How the number of stanzas determines the difficulty level of a poem","How the poet's biography explains the theme of the poem"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how structural elements of poetry contribute to meaning. A line break tells the reader where to pause, and a break placed inside a phrase withholds the completion of a thought, which creates emphasis and suspense. Asking why the break falls where it does directs attention to that control of pacing.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how structural elements of poetry contribute to meaning. Where a line ends is a visual and spatial decision, not merely a matter of reaching the right margin, and noticing the arrangement is what lets students see poetry's structure as meaningful rather than decorative.","dex":{"How the rhyme scheme identifies which genre a poem belongs to":"Promotes rhyme scheme into a genre label, demoting a structural feature from something that serves meaning to something that sorts poems. Rhyme scheme is a separate feature from line breaks and is not what the question asks about. Moreover, rhyme pattern does not determine genre; poems in many forms share rhyme schemes, and much contemporary poetry does not rhyme at all.","How the number of stanzas determines the difficulty level of a poem":"Converts a structural feature into a countable difficulty index, as though stanza totals measured what makes a poem hard. Stanza count is a quantitative feature and says nothing reliable about how hard a poem is to understand. Difficulty comes from language, imagery, and the ideas at stake, none of which is measured by counting blocks of lines.","How the poet's biography explains the theme of the poem":"Looks to the poet's biography for meaning, substituting outside context for the textual and structural evidence in the poem itself. Biographical context can enrich a reading but it cannot explain a specific textual choice such as where a line breaks. The question asks students to reason from evidence in the poem itself, which is the habit poetry instruction should build."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-024","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Ms. Vega plans to use an above-grade-level article on animal adaptation with her third graders. Which pre-reading activity would best prepare them to make sense of it?","options":["Having students read the text silently once for fluency before any discussion takes place","Asking students to copy the boldface vocabulary words and their dictionary definitions into a notebook","Showing a short video about the topic so that students do not need to read the text closely","Discussing the key concept that a physical feature helps an animal survive, and clarifying what adapt and survive mean in that context"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to support students with complex text. Combining a discussion of the key concept with clarification of the academic terms does two jobs at once: it gives students a framework to attach details to, and it removes the word-level obstacles that would otherwise interrupt that framework as they read. Discussing the concept in context, rather than defining words in isolation, is what makes the vocabulary usable during reading.","dex":{"Having students read the text silently once for fluency before any discussion takes place":"Treats a first silent read for fluency as preparation for hard text, expecting practice at speed to open up the meaning. A silent first read offers no support for the vocabulary or the concepts that make this text hard, and practicing fluency does not by itself build understanding of unfamiliar ideas. Students who struggle are left to struggle again, only faster.","Asking students to copy the boldface vocabulary words and their dictionary definitions into a notebook":"Equates copying dictionary definitions with vocabulary learning, trading a discussion of the concept for the shallow processing of transcription. Copying definitions produces little engagement with meaning and often yields definitions that do not fit the way the word is used in the text. It substitutes a clerical task for the discussion of concepts that actually prepares students to read.","Showing a short video about the topic so that students do not need to read the text closely":"Lets the video stand in for the reading, turning background activation into a substitute for engaging the text itself. A video can build background knowledge, but using it so students can skip close reading removes the very work the lesson exists to do. Background activation is a preparation for reading the text, not a replacement for it."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-025","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A third-grade teacher notices that students' written reading responses summarize whole articles but rarely point to specific sentences from the text. Which instructional change would best address this pattern?","options":["Asking students to record a statement about the text and then copy two sentences from the passage that support it","Asking students to reread the passage aloud one more time before writing their responses","Asking students to write longer summaries so that more of the text's details are included","Asking students to look up unfamiliar words after reading so that word meanings do not block comprehension"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to support students in identifying main ideas and key details with evidence. Pairing a claim about the text with specific sentences that justify it makes the evidence step explicit and repeatable, so students practice moving from what they think to what the text says, which is precisely the move that summarizing alone does not demand of them.","dex":{"Asking students to reread the passage aloud one more time before writing their responses":"Treating extra rereading as the remedy for any comprehension problem, so that more exposure to the text is assumed to produce better understanding Rereading strengthens accuracy and familiarity with the text, but it gives students no routine for connecting an idea back to particular sentences. This option reflects the common misconception that more exposure to the text automatically produces better comprehension. The missing skill is citation, not repetition.","Asking students to write longer summaries so that more of the text's details are included":"Equating a longer, more detailed summary with evidence-based support, as though completeness and justification were the same thing A longer summary still reports content without justifying it, so it reinforces the exact pattern the teacher observed. This option reflects the common misconception that completeness is the same as support. Adding details says more about what happened; citing evidence explains why a reader is entitled to claim it.","Asking students to look up unfamiliar words after reading so that word meanings do not block comprehension":"Assuming that comprehension failures always trace back to word meaning, which makes vocabulary the default explanation for any difficulty Vocabulary work addresses word-level meaning and has real value, but word knowledge does not teach students to locate and quote support for an idea. This option reflects the common misconception that comprehension failures usually originate in word meanings. These students comprehend well enough to summarize; they simply do not back up their thinking."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-026","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Ms. Alvarez's second-grade class includes several English learners and several students reading well above grade level. She wants every student to work on identifying the main idea of the same science article. Which approach best differentiates this instruction?","options":["Reading the article aloud to the whole class and having every student complete the same written summary","Providing the article at three reading levels and giving all students the same main-idea graphic organizer","Assigning the English learners a simpler topic while the advanced readers work on the science article","Having the English learners practice decoding during the lesson while the rest of the class works on main idea"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to differentiate instruction for learners with different abilities and backgrounds. Holding the target skill constant while varying the text level keeps English learners and advanced readers working on the same concept, and a shared graphic organizer gives everyone the same thinking routine regardless of which version they read.","dex":{"Reading the article aloud to the whole class and having every student complete the same written summary":"Equating identical tasks and identical products with fairness, so that equal treatment is mistaken for equal opportunity A read-aloud does give access to the text, but requiring an identical written summary ignores differences in students' language production. This option reflects the common misconception that treating everyone the same way is the same as being fair. Identical tasks do not produce equal opportunity when students' language and literacy levels differ.","Assigning the English learners a simpler topic while the advanced readers work on the science article":"Equating differentiation with assigning struggling students an easier task, which lowers the target instead of giving access to it Giving English learners a different task removes them from the grade-level concept instead of giving them access to it. This option reflects the common misconception that differentiation means assigning struggling students an easier objective. Differentiation adjusts the path, not the destination.","Having the English learners practice decoding during the lesson while the rest of the class works on main idea":"Treating remediation of foundational skills as the main form of differentiation, which removes English learners from grade-level comprehension work Pulling English learners out of comprehension instruction to work on decoding denies them the very thinking the lesson targets. This option reflects the common misconception that students who are still developing foundational skills must master those first before engaging with meaning. Comprehension instruction runs alongside foundational work, not after it."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-027","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"During a fourth-grade small-group lesson, the teacher stops at the end of each page and asks students to mark a sticky note with a check if the text made sense and a question mark if it did not, then to discuss what they did about it. This routine primarily develops students'","options":["Skill in stating the main idea of each page after reading","Academic vocabulary by collecting the unfamiliar words they meet","Ability to monitor their own comprehension and take action when meaning breaks down","Oral reading fluency through frequent short readings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to guide students in metacognitively monitoring their comprehension. The check-or-question-mark judgment forces a periodic decision about whether meaning is intact, and the follow-up conversation makes the repair step visible, so students build both an awareness of their own understanding and a repertoire of responses for when it fails.","dex":{"Skill in stating the main idea of each page after reading":"Substituting a comprehension product, such as stating a page's main idea, for the monitoring process that produces it Naming a page's main idea is a comprehension product, not a check on whether comprehension is happening. This option reflects the common misconception that a comprehension outcome can stand in for the monitoring process that produces it. Students can state main ideas and still fail to notice when a later page stops making sense.","Academic vocabulary by collecting the unfamiliar words they meet":"Assuming that any breakdown in meaning is caused by an unknown word, so all comprehension lapses are attributed to vocabulary Nothing in the routine directs attention to individual words. This option reflects the common misconception that a comprehension breakdown is usually a vocabulary problem. Loss of meaning often comes from unclear referents, thin background knowledge, or tangled sentence structure, none of which this judgment attributes to words.","Oral reading fluency through frequent short readings":"Treating pauses for reflection during reading as fluency practice, when fluency targets automaticity and prosody instead Stopping frequently interrupts continuous reading rather than building rate, and fluency is not the target of a judgment task. This option reflects the common misconception that any repeated reading activity is fluency instruction. Fluency work aims at automaticity and prosody; the pauses here serve reflection instead."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-028","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"The Simple View of Writing describes writing as the product of transcription and text generation. A teacher plans daily lessons based on the Not-So-Simple View of Writing instead. Which component does the Not-So-Simple View add that the Simple View does not explicitly include?","options":["Handwriting fluency and the automatic production of letters","Spelling and the mapping of phonemes onto graphemes","Sentence generation and the development of ideas for text","Executive functions such as planning, reviewing, and self-regulating the writing process"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of systematic, explicit writing instruction for all learners. The Not-So-Simple View of Writing keeps transcription and text generation but adds the executive functions that coordinate them, including planning, reviewing, revising, and self-regulation. This explains why students who can form letters and generate ideas may still produce weak papers.","dex":{"Handwriting fluency and the automatic production of letters":"Mistaking a component the Simple View already contains, handwriting automaticity, for something the expanded model adds Handwriting fluency belongs to transcription, which the Simple View already includes. This option reflects the common misconception that the expanded model introduces new transcription demands. Naming a component the earlier model already contains cannot answer a question about what was added.","Spelling and the mapping of phonemes onto graphemes":"Treating spelling and phoneme-grapheme mapping as the point where the two models diverge, when both place them inside transcription Spelling and phoneme-grapheme mapping also sit inside transcription in the Simple View. This option reflects the common misconception that spelling instruction is where the two models part ways. The models agree on transcription; they differ in what surrounds and coordinates it.","Sentence generation and the development of ideas for text":"Treating text generation as an advanced addition to the model rather than as one of its two original components Generating sentences and ideas is text generation, the second of the Simple View's two components. This option reflects the common misconception that idea generation is an advanced addition rather than one of the two original pillars. A writer can generate ideas well and still fail for want of planning or self-regulation."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-029","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Ms. Chen gives her fifth graders a short written retell every Friday and uses the results to decide which students need reteaching on identifying key details. This use of the retell is best described as","options":["A formative assessment that guides the next steps in instruction","A summative assessment that measures achievement at the end of a unit","A screening measure that identifies students who should be evaluated for special services","A standardized assessment that allows comparison of students across classrooms"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how formal and informal assessments guide differentiated instruction and monitor progress. The retell is collected frequently, examined for one specific skill, and used to change what happens next week, which is the defining feature of formative assessment: the information feeds back into teaching while there is still time to act on it.","dex":{"A summative assessment that measures achievement at the end of a unit":"Classifying an assessment by its form, a recorded score, rather than by the use that is made of its results A summative measure reports what students have learned after instruction ends rather than deciding what to reteach. This option reflects the common misconception that any assessment producing a record or a score is summative. What classifies an assessment is the use made of its results, not the form those results take.","A screening measure that identifies students who should be evaluated for special services":"Confusing frequent progress monitoring with screening for risk, so weekly instructional adjustments are treated as a path to referral Screening is designed to flag students who may be at risk and to prompt further evaluation, not to plan next week's small groups. This option reflects the common misconception that frequent progress monitoring is a route to referral. A weekly retell used to decide on reteaching stays entirely inside the instructional cycle.","A standardized assessment that allows comparison of students across classrooms":"Treating uniform administration to the whole class as standardization, which also requires normed and comparable scoring Standardized assessment requires fixed administration, normed scoring, and evidence supporting comparison across groups; a teacher-made weekly retell has none of these. This option reflects the common misconception that giving every student the same task makes an assessment standardized. Uniform administration is not standardization."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-030","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher wants students to treat their writing folders as a record of decisions they can return to rather than as a place where finished papers are kept. Which of the following practices would best support that goal? Select all that apply.","options":["Having students choose a piece they wrote several weeks earlier, revise it, and attach a note explaining what they changed and why","Having students keep drafts with dates on them and compare an early draft with a later one to describe how their writing has changed","Having students rewrite a finished piece for a different audience, deciding which details to keep and which to leave out","Having students trade papers and make every change a partner suggests before turning the piece in","Having the teacher mark every error on a draft and having students correct the marked errors before filing the piece"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how to teach writing as a recursive process. Reaching back to a piece written weeks earlier shows students that a text is never finally finished, and the attached note makes the reason behind each change explicit. Revisiting old work is what turns a folder into a record of thinking rather than a stack of completed assignments.  [B] This option is also correct because dated drafts let students see their own development instead of being told about it. Comparing an early draft with a later one makes change over time visible, and describing that change pushes students to name the decisions behind it, which is the self-evaluation a recursive process depends on.  [C] This option is also correct because changing the audience forces writers to reopen decisions they had already settled: which details serve this reader, which ones distract, and what needs reordering. That is substantive revision applied to a piece thought to be complete, exactly the habit of returning to a text the teacher wants to build.","dex":{"Having students trade papers and make every change a partner suggests before turning the piece in":"Treating a partner's suggestions as changes the writer must accept, so revision becomes compliance instead of a decision the author owns Letting a partner's suggestions dictate the changes removes the one thing recursion requires, which is the writer's own judgment about what the text needs. Students who apply every suggestion are following directions rather than revising. This option reflects the common misconception that peer response arrives as authoritative feedback.","Having the teacher mark every error on a draft and having students correct the marked errors before filing the piece":"Equating editing for surface conventions with revision of meaning, and treating a teacher-supplied list of fixes as the writer's own rethinking Correcting errors someone else has located is editing, a one-directional cleanup performed on the surface of a finished text. It does not send students back to reconsider meaning, and because the teacher supplies the list, it leaves no decision for the writer to make. This option reflects the common misconception that error correction is revision."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-031","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Before her third graders plan a piece of writing, Ms. Ortiz displays three anchor charts: one lists characters, setting, and problem and solution; one lists a topic, facts, and definitions; one lists a claim, reasons, and evidence. Which explanation best helps students understand how the charts differ?","options":["The charts describe the order in which students should learn to write, beginning with narrative and ending with opinion","Each chart describes a different genre, and the purpose of that genre determines which features belong in the piece","The charts describe steps in the writing process that every piece must follow in order","The charts describe three levels of quality, with the claim-and-evidence chart being the most advanced"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to explain and demonstrate concepts for students. Naming the three lists as genre features tied to purpose gives students a reason for the differences: a story needs a problem and solution because it entertains, while an opinion piece needs reasons and evidence because it persuades. Purpose explains the features rather than leaving them as arbitrary lists.","dex":{"The charts describe the order in which students should learn to write, beginning with narrative and ending with opinion":"Confusing genre categories with scope and sequence, as though the order in which genres are taught were part of the genres themselves The charts show alternatives available to a writer, not the sequence of units. This option reflects the common misconception that genre types are the same as scope and sequence. A class may study genres in any order, and students return to each one repeatedly across the school year.","The charts describe steps in the writing process that every piece must follow in order":"Confusing lists of genre features with the stages of the writing process, so attributes of a finished text are read as steps a writer takes These lists are features of finished texts, not sequential actions a writer performs. This option reflects the common misconception that any set of classroom lists represents the writing process. Planning, drafting, revising, and publishing are stages; characters, facts, and claims are attributes of genres.","The charts describe three levels of quality, with the claim-and-evidence chart being the most advanced":"Treating genres as a hierarchy of difficulty in which the genre taught later is the more advanced one The charts differ in kind, not in quality, and each genre has demanding versions appropriate to this grade. This option reflects the common misconception that text types form a hierarchy of difficulty. A weak opinion piece is not more advanced than a strong narrative, even though opinion writing is often taught later."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-032","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"During a first-grade word study block, the teacher says a word, students repeat it and tap out each sound, write the letters that represent those sounds, and then read back what they wrote. This routine primarily promotes literacy development by","options":["Developing reading fluency through repeated oral reading of connected text","Strengthening phonemic awareness by keeping the work at the level of spoken sounds","Engaging students in both encoding and decoding over the same phoneme-grapheme connections","Building oral vocabulary by connecting new words to students' background knowledge"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how decoding and encoding support each other as reciprocal skills. Moving from spoken sounds to written letters is encoding; reading the written word back is decoding. Because both directions travel the same phoneme-grapheme links, each reinforces the other and students see that one set of connections works both ways.","dex":{"Developing reading fluency through repeated oral reading of connected text":"Treating repeated oral reading of isolated words as fluency practice, when fluency needs automatic recognition carried across connected text Fluency is built on continuous, connected text, and this routine uses isolated words with no sentence context for phrasing or expression. This option reflects the common misconception that repeating words aloud is fluency practice. Fluent reading requires automatic word recognition carried across a passage.","Strengthening phonemic awareness by keeping the work at the level of spoken sounds":"Equating phonemic awareness, which is purely oral, with word study that requires students to write and read letters The routine moves beyond spoken sounds as soon as students write letters and read print. This option reflects the common misconception that tapping sounds and working with letters are the same task. Phonemic awareness can be done with eyes closed; adding print makes the work phonics and word study.","Building oral vocabulary by connecting new words to students' background knowledge":"Treating any activity that uses words as vocabulary instruction, which conflates print-sound structure with word meaning The routine works on the sound and spelling structure of words, not on their meanings or their relation to experience. This option reflects the common misconception that any work with words is vocabulary instruction. Word study at this level builds the print-sound system that later makes independent word learning possible."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-033","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher has students meet in small groups to discuss a text they have all read, explaining their thinking, questioning one another's interpretations, and working out the parts that confused them, before they read a comparable text on their own. Which statement best explains why this practice improves students' comprehension of the new text?","options":["Discussing a topic gives students background knowledge, which lets them rely on what they already know instead of monitoring their understanding as they read","Explaining their thinking to classmates strengthens students' speaking skills, which helps them answer questions about a text fluently","Working in groups increases students' engagement, which leads them to read with greater accuracy and rate","Talking through a text lets students practice constructing meaning and monitoring their understanding in oral language, which they then apply to a text they read independently"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how oral language work supports reading comprehension. Comprehending is not a set of print-specific routines; it is the same act of building meaning and noticing when that meaning breaks down. In discussion students perform that act out loud, with partners who question and repair it, and at no cost in attention to decoding. What carries over to the independent text is the process itself, now rehearsed and easier for a student to run alone.","dex":{"Discussing a topic gives students background knowledge, which lets them rely on what they already know instead of monitoring their understanding as they read":"Treating background knowledge as a substitute for the comprehension processes a reader has to carry out while reading Background knowledge does support comprehension, but it is not what this practice builds, and nothing in the stem supplies knowledge of the new text. This option reflects the common misconception that knowledge can stand in for the work of comprehending. The practice targets how students make meaning, not what they happen to already know.","Explaining their thinking to classmates strengthens students' speaking skills, which helps them answer questions about a text fluently":"Locating the benefit of text discussion in speaking performance rather than in the meaning students construct Speaking well and understanding well are different outcomes. A student who talks easily about a text may still build a thin understanding of it, and the gain here shows up in what students comprehend rather than in how smoothly they respond. This option reflects the common misconception that the payoff of talk about text is better talk.","Working in groups increases students' engagement, which leads them to read with greater accuracy and rate":"Attributing comprehension growth to engagement and to fluent word reading, which are motivational and word-level outcomes rather than meaning-making Engagement is worth having, but accuracy and rate are word-level outcomes, and improving them does not explain a better grasp of meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that motivation plus fluent word reading is what produces understanding."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-034","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"While reading a chapter of historical fiction aloud, a fifth-grade teacher pauses and says, 'The author never tells us why she left the house, but I know she had just read the letter and her hands were shaking, so I think she was frightened and went to find her brother.' Which strategy is the teacher primarily modeling?","options":["Making an inference by combining text evidence with what the reader already knows to explain something the author left unstated","Making a prediction about what will happen later in the story","Summarizing the events of the passage to determine the main idea","Using context clues to work out the meaning of an unfamiliar word"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies that improve comprehension. The teacher names two details from the page, adds a general understanding of what shaking hands can signal, and reaches a conclusion the text never states. That is exactly what an inference requires and exactly what a think-aloud should make visible.","dex":{"Making a prediction about what will happen later in the story":"Conflating an inference about an event that has already occurred with a prediction about what happens next The teacher explains an action that has already occurred rather than anticipating what comes next. This option reflects the common misconception that any reasoning about a character counts as predicting. Inference fills in what the author left out; prediction forecasts what the author has not yet written.","Summarizing the events of the passage to determine the main idea":"Equating thinking aloud while reading with summarizing, so the form of the activity is mistaken for the strategy being modeled The teacher is adding meaning rather than condensing events, and no attempt is made to reduce the passage to its gist. This option reflects the common misconception that talking while reading is summarizing. A think-aloud can model any strategy; the content of the talk determines which one.","Using context clues to work out the meaning of an unfamiliar word":"Narrowing the use of context to word-meaning clues, when surrounding text also supports conclusions about characters and events Nothing in the think-aloud concerns a word's meaning, and the shaking hands serve as evidence about a character's state rather than as a source of definition. This option reflects the common misconception that context clues means any use of surrounding text. Context clues resolve word meaning; here surrounding text supports a conclusion about events."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-035","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A third-grade teacher asks students to compare how a story reads when it is told by a child who is lost in a museum and when it is told by the security guard who finds her. This lesson primarily helps students understand","options":["How the setting establishes where and when the story takes place","How a narrator's point of view shapes what the reader knows and feels about the events","How figurative language such as similes and metaphors makes description more vivid","How the sequence of events determines the plot of a story"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach literary devices to deepen comprehension. The same events retold by two different narrators show students that whoever tells a story controls what information reaches the reader and whose feelings the reader shares, which is the practical meaning of point of view.","dex":{"How the setting establishes where and when the story takes place":"Identifying the most concrete story element, the setting, as the variable under study when only the narrator differs The museum setting is identical in both tellings and therefore cannot explain the difference students are asked to notice. This option reflects the common misconception that the most concrete story element is the one under study. When two versions differ only in narrator, the narrator is the variable.","How figurative language such as similes and metaphors makes description more vivid":"Attributing a change in how vivid a text feels to figurative language rather than to the narrator who tells the story The lesson changes the teller of the story, not the wording of the descriptions. This option reflects the common misconception that any change in how vivid a text feels comes from figurative language. Neither version needs a single simile for the comparison to work.","How the sequence of events determines the plot of a story":"Confusing point of view with plot sequence, so that who reports the events is mistaken for the order in which they happen Both versions recount the same events in the same order, so plot is held constant while perspective changes. This option reflects the common misconception that point of view concerns the order of happenings. Plot is what happens; point of view is who reports it."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-036","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade class reads a short play. The teacher asks students to note what characters say to one another, what the stage directions tell the actors to do, and where the scenes break. This work primarily helps students understand","options":["How a character's decisions drive the events of the plot","How to write a summary that includes only the most important events","How the structural features of drama, including dialogue, stage directions, and scenes, carry meaning that prose paragraphs do not","How the rhyme and rhythm of a poem contribute to its mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how the structural elements of different genres contribute to a text's overall meaning. In drama, meaning lives in what is spoken, in the directions that convey action and tone, and in the division of scenes that signals shifts in time or place, so reading those features is what allows students to interpret the play at all.","dex":{"How a character's decisions drive the events of the plot":"Confusing structural features of a text with elements of story content, so character motivation is studied in place of genre form Following what characters decide is interpretation of content, not attention to the structural elements named in the activity. This option reflects the common misconception that any discussion of a literary text counts as structural analysis. The teacher pointed students at dialogue, directions, and scene breaks, which are formal features.","How to write a summary that includes only the most important events":"Reducing drama to a summary of main events, which discards the stage directions and scene breaks that carry meaning Summarizing reduces a text to its gist and leaves out the very details this activity asks students to notice. This option reflects the common misconception that the purpose of reading any text is to extract the main events. In drama, stage directions and scene breaks are essential and are lost in a summary.","How the rhyme and rhythm of a poem contribute to its mood":"Applying the sound features of poetry to a play, so that rhyme and rhythm are sought where dialogue carries the meaning Rhyme and rhythm belong to poetry, not to the play the class is reading. This option reflects the common misconception that the features of one genre transfer to another. Teaching students to look for sound patterns in dialogue will not help them read a script."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-037","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Mr. Reyes confers with Jalen, a fifth grader reading a complex article about coral reefs. Jalen reads the page fluently but says, 'I read the whole thing and I do not remember anything.' Which two conference moves would best develop Jalen's thinking? Select all that apply.","options":["Asking Jalen to say what each section was mostly about and to point to the sentences that support his statement","Discussing three or four key concept words such as reef, algae, and bleaching before Jalen rereads the section","Moving Jalen to an easier text so that he can read without frustration","Having Jalen copy the boldfaced vocabulary words and their definitions into a notebook","Telling Jalen the main idea of the section so that he can move on to the next one"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how to use conversation to develop a student's thinking about reading content. Requiring a statement plus the sentences that support it gives Jalen a concrete way to hold meaning while he reads, and talking it through lets Mr. Reyes hear exactly where the construction of meaning fails and respond in the moment.  [B] Preteaching a small set of concept words and discussing what they mean before rereading supplies the knowledge Jalen needs to connect sentences to one another. Complex text often defeats readers through the density of unfamiliar concepts rather than through the words themselves, and a short oral discussion restores his ability to build meaning.","dex":{"Moving Jalen to an easier text so that he can read without frustration":"Treating a lower text level as the default support for comprehension difficulty, rather than teaching strategies for handling complex text Fluent reading with poor recall is not a text-level problem, so an easier article would not address it. This option reflects the common misconception that the remedy for comprehension difficulty is always a lower text. Jalen can read these words; what he lacks is a way to hold and connect ideas, which instruction should supply.","Having Jalen copy the boldfaced vocabulary words and their definitions into a notebook":"Equating copying definitions with prereaching vocabulary, so recording words replaces the talk that builds concepts Copying definitions produces a list of words without the conceptual understanding that makes a dense article cohere. This option reflects the common misconception that vocabulary instruction means recording definitions. Discussing a few concept words and using them in talk is what builds the knowledge that supports comprehension.","Telling Jalen the main idea of the section so that he can move on to the next one":"Treating the teacher's supplying of an answer as scaffolding, when scaffolding leaves the thinking with the student Supplying the main idea closes the conversation instead of opening it and leaves Jalen dependent on the teacher for meaning. This option reflects the common misconception that giving an answer is a form of scaffolding. Scaffolding keeps the thinking with the student while making it possible; handing over the conclusion removes the thinking entirely."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-038","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"After a read-aloud, Ms. Boyd asks her first graders to retell the story. One student says, 'The dog ran away, and then he found the boy, and I liked the part with the mud puddle.' Which statement best characterizes this retell?","options":["The student comprehends the story well but has weak decoding skills","The student has confused the narrator's point of view with the author's","The student is summarizing too briefly because of limited working memory","The student recalls a central event and adds a personal reaction but does not yet sort central events from interesting details"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to support students in identifying central ideas and key details. The student produces one event that matters to the plot and then drifts to a favorite moment, which is typical of early retelling. The instructional need is a routine for deciding which events the story cannot do without.","dex":{"The student comprehends the story well but has weak decoding skills":"Attributing a weakness in oral retelling to decoding, even though retelling a read-aloud involves no print at all The retell is oral, so decoding never enters into it. This option reflects the common misconception that any reading difficulty signals a decoding problem. Because the student is retelling a story that was read aloud, the evidence points to comprehension and selection, not to word attack.","The student has confused the narrator's point of view with the author's":"Reading a student's personal reaction to a story as confusion about point of view or about who is speaking Adding a personal reaction is not an error about who is speaking. This option reflects the common misconception that student involvement with a text signals confusion about perspective. The student knows the story was told to her; she is simply reporting enjoyment alongside recall.","The student is summarizing too briefly because of limited working memory":"Attributing poor selection of events to limited working memory, when the missing skill is judging which events matter most Length is not the problem; the retell contains enough content, but that content is not weighted by importance. This option reflects the common misconception that a thin retell indicates a capacity limit. Teaching students to rank events by importance addresses the selection problem, which more memory would not solve."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-039","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Ms. Nakamura's fourth graders are reading a folktale from a culture unfamiliar to most of the class, and several students misinterpret the characters' motives. Which combination of steps best supports comprehension while respecting students' cultural backgrounds?","options":["Building relevant background knowledge before reading and using a graphic organizer to track characters' motives during reading","Replacing the folktale with a story set in the students' own community","Explaining to students that characters in traditional tales act for reasons modern readers cannot understand","Having students read the folktale silently twice and then write a summary"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to differentiate instruction for learners from varied cultural backgrounds. Supplying the knowledge the tale assumes and giving students a structure for recording what characters want and why supports comprehension without lowering the task or replacing the text, so students keep their access to a tradition not their own.","dex":{"Replacing the folktale with a story set in the students' own community":"Treating unfamiliar cultural content as a reason to replace the text rather than to supply the knowledge the text assumes Swapping the text removes the opportunity to learn about another culture rather than making the original comprehensible. This option reflects the common misconception that the respectful response to unfamiliar content is to avoid it. The difficulty here is missing knowledge, and knowledge can be taught.","Explaining to students that characters in traditional tales act for reasons modern readers cannot understand":"Treating cultural distance as making a text unreadable, which closes off the inference the lesson should invite Declaring the motives inaccessible tells students that inference is impossible here and shuts down the thinking the lesson should provoke. This option reflects the common misconception that cultural distance makes interpretation inappropriate. Readers can learn to reason about unfamiliar values once they have enough information about them.","Having students read the folktale silently twice and then write a summary":"Treating repeated silent reading as differentiation for comprehension difficulty, when the gap is missing context from outside the text Silent rereading adds exposure but no new knowledge or structure, so students will most likely reproduce the same misreadings. This option reflects the common misconception that repetition compensates for missing information. Comprehension fails here for lack of context, which must come from outside the text."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-040","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A third-grade teacher teaches students to listen for clunks, places where the text stops making sense, and to respond by rereading the sentence, reading on two more sentences, or breaking a long word apart. Which two outcomes does this instruction primarily target? Select all that apply.","options":["Students' awareness of whether they are understanding what they read","Students' ability to choose and apply a repair strategy when comprehension breaks down","Students' automatic recognition of high-frequency words","Students' ability to read grade-level text at an appropriate rate"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how to guide students in metacognitively monitoring their comprehension. Naming the feeling of lost meaning as a clunk gives students a label for an internal state, which turns vague confusion into something they can notice and report, and noticing is the prerequisite for doing anything about it.  [B] Teaching a short list of repair moves and when each one fits gives students something to do at the moment of breakdown. Awareness on its own leaves a reader stuck; pairing the clunk with specific strategies is what makes monitoring useful rather than merely diagnostic.","dex":{"Students' automatic recognition of high-frequency words":"Generalizing one fix-up move, breaking a word apart, into the lesson's main target, so monitoring becomes word-recognition practice Word recognition is a foundational skill taught through phonics and high-frequency word practice, not through comprehension monitoring. This option reflects the common misconception that because one fix-up move involves breaking a word apart, the lesson targets word recognition. Breaking a word apart is a rescue tactic used after meaning is already lost.","Students' ability to read grade-level text at an appropriate rate":"Conflating comprehension monitoring with fluency, as though reading at an appropriate rate guaranteed that meaning was intact Rate is a fluency outcome, and nothing in the routine asks students to read faster or more smoothly. This option reflects the common misconception that monitoring and fluency are the same component. A student can read at an appropriate rate and still not notice that the text has stopped making sense."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-041","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A second-grade teacher devotes fifteen minutes daily to writing instruction: she models a sentence pattern, writes one sentence with the class, then has students write several independently while she checks letter formation and spelling. Which principle best explains why this routine is effective?","options":["Writing improves fastest when teachers comment only on ideas, because mechanics take care of themselves over time","Writing develops through systematic, explicit instruction, and transcription skills must be taught directly so that handwriting and spelling become automatic enough to free attention for composing","Writing develops naturally from wide reading, so the teacher's main job is to give students time to write","Writing instruction should begin with whole-text composition, because young children already form letters fluently"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of systematic, explicit writing instruction for all learners. Modeling, shared writing, and guided practice make the sentence pattern visible before students attempt it alone, and attending to letter formation and spelling builds the transcription fluency that keeps young writers from spending all their working memory on forming letters instead of on what they want to say.","dex":{"Writing improves fastest when teachers comment only on ideas, because mechanics take care of themselves over time":"Treating feedback on ideas alone as the way to protect young writers, on the assumption that attention to conventions harms expression Mechanics do not resolve themselves for many students, and ignoring them leaves struggling writers without the feedback they need. This option reflects the common misconception that attention to conventions damages young writers' ideas. Explicit attention to both is what allows ideas to reach the page legibly.","Writing develops naturally from wide reading, so the teacher's main job is to give students time to write":"Assuming that writing is absorbed naturally from reading and practice time, so explicit teaching of its components is unnecessary Writing is not absorbed from reading; its components must be taught. This option reflects the common misconception that literacy skills transfer from input alone. Reading supplies models and knowledge, but sentence construction, spelling, and planning each require explicit teaching and practice.","Writing instruction should begin with whole-text composition, because young children already form letters fluently":"Treating transcription as a prerequisite young writers already have, rather than as a skill that competes with ideas for attention Most second graders are still building handwriting and spelling automaticity, which is exactly why this teacher checks them. This option reflects the common misconception that transcription is a prerequisite already in place by the time composing begins. When transcription is slow, it competes with ideas for attention."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-042","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fifth-grade teacher uses weekly one-minute oral reading probes, monthly written retells, and daily exit tickets. Which two purposes do these assessments serve that a single end-of-unit test cannot? Select all that apply.","options":["Showing whether students are making progress over time so the teacher can judge whether instruction is working","Allowing the teacher to regroup students and adjust instruction in response to what students show this week","Assigning students a final grade for the grading period","Ranking students so that reading groups can be fixed for the entire year"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how assessment guides differentiated instruction and monitors progress. Repeated measures taken the same way across weeks produce a trend, and a trend tells the teacher whether a student's response to current instruction is adequate, which is information a single end-of-unit score cannot supply.  [B] This option is also correct because frequent, brief measures give the teacher current information about what students can do now, so groups can be formed around today's needs and reformed as those needs change. This is the instructional use of assessment: the results change what happens next.","dex":{"Assigning students a final grade for the grading period":"Using frequent formative measures as the source of a report-card grade, which confuses informing teaching with certifying achievement Grading is exactly what an end-of-unit test does well, so it cannot be the distinguishing purpose. This option reflects the common misconception that accumulating formative scores is the best way to produce a grade. Frequent low-stakes measures are designed to inform teaching, and averaging them for a report card misuses them.","Ranking students so that reading groups can be fixed for the entire year":"Treating reading groups as a fixed ranking of ability for the year, when progress monitoring assumes groups change as students change Fixed year-long ranking contradicts the point of progress monitoring, which assumes that students change and that instruction should follow. This option reflects the common misconception that assessment places students into stable ability categories. Groups formed from current data are meant to be temporary and responsive."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-043","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Ms. Alvarez's third graders draft personal narratives, conference with her, and then use a short checklist to decide what to change before writing a new version. Which feature of this routine best reflects writing as a recursive process?","options":["Students receive a score after each draft so they know how well they performed","Students start a brand-new topic each week, so they gain practice with many kinds of writing","Students return to an existing draft to revise it based on their own evaluation instead of treating a finished paper as final","Students copy a corrected version of their draft in their best handwriting for display"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach writing as a recursive process. Revision, editing, and rewriting are not a cleanup stage that happens once at the end; writers move back and forth among planning, drafting, and revising as their ideas develop. Having students evaluate their own draft with a checklist and then re-enter it to make changes puts the loop into the routine rather than leaving it as advice.","dex":{"Students receive a score after each draft so they know how well they performed":"Assuming that grading motivates revision; evaluation that supports recursion must be criterion-referenced and student-directed, not a summary judgment. A score reports how a performance was judged but does not tell students what to change or invite them back into the text. Self-evaluation against criteria, not the mark itself, is what drives the next draft.","Students start a brand-new topic each week, so they gain practice with many kinds of writing":"Treating writing production as the goal and equating a high output of finished pieces with recursive process writing. Producing a new piece each week increases volume but keeps the process linear: each text is started, finished, and abandoned. Nothing leads students back into a text they have already written.","Students copy a corrected version of their draft in their best handwriting for display":"Confusing neat handwriting and a clean final copy with the intellectual work of revising; this reduces writing to handwriting and spelling. Copying a corrected draft is transcription, not revision. It changes the appearance of the paper without asking students to reconsider meaning, organization, or word choice."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-044","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A second-grade teacher plans a unit in which students write about something that happened to them, including what they felt and why it mattered. Which genre is the teacher introducing?","options":["Descriptive, because students use sensory details about people and places","Opinion/argumentative, because students state what they think about their experience","Informative/explanatory, because students convey information about an event","Narrative, because students recount an experience and convey its personal significance"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how common writing genres are taught. Narrative writing recounts a sequence of events and conveys what the experience meant to the writer. Asking students to include what they felt and why the event mattered is what separates a narrative recount from a bare report of what happened.","dex":{"Descriptive, because students use sensory details about people and places":"Identifying genre from a local surface feature instead of the overall communicative purpose of the piece. Sensory detail is a technique that appears inside many genres, including narrative. The presence of description does not make description the genre, and the unit described asks for an event and its significance, not a still portrait of a person or place.","Opinion/argumentative, because students state what they think about their experience":"Treating any statement of personal preference or feeling as argument; argument requires a contestable claim plus reasons directed at an audience. Opinion writing asks students to take a position on a debatable question and support it with reasons aimed at a reader who may disagree. Expressing feeling about a personal experience is not the same as arguing a claim.","Informative/explanatory, because students convey information about an event":"Judging genre by subject matter rather than by the writer's purpose; an event can appear in both an explanation and a story, and purpose is what distinguishes them. Informative writing exists to explain or inform a reader about a topic, using facts, definitions, and examples. Telling what happened to the writer, with the writer's reaction at the center, serves a different purpose."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-045","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A first-grade teacher is teaching the -ck spelling for /k/ at the end of a short-vowel word. Which pairing of activities best uses decoding and encoding as reciprocal skills?","options":["Students read a list of -ck words aloud, then reread the same list to build speed","Students read -ck words in a decodable passage, then use sound boxes to spell those same words and check their spelling against the text","Students match pictures to printed -ck words, then draw a picture for one of the words","Students listen for final /k/ in spoken words, then sort pictures by whether the word ends in /k/"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how decoding and encoding reinforce each other. Reading the words in print and then spelling those very same words sends students over identical letter sequences in both directions, and checking the spelling against the text makes the correspondence explicit. Each process strengthens the mental representation the other depends on, which is why the two taught together produce more growth than either taught alone.","dex":{"Students read a list of -ck words aloud, then reread the same list to build speed":"Mistaking repeated reading in one direction for a reciprocal routine; fluency practice does not exercise encoding at all. Both halves of this pairing run in the reading direction. Rereading for speed builds automaticity, which is valuable, but no part of it asks students to go from sound to print.","Students match pictures to printed -ck words, then draw a picture for one of the words":"Assuming that any activity involving printed words exercises encoding; encoding requires segmenting and mapping sounds to letters, not recognizing a word. Matching pictures to words and drawing require students to connect print to meaning, not to analyze the sound structure and reconstruct the spelling. The phoneme-by-grapheme work that constitutes encoding is absent.","Students listen for final /k/ in spoken words, then sort pictures by whether the word ends in /k/":"Selecting an activity at the wrong level of the literacy hierarchy; oral sound work precedes and does not substitute for print-based reading and writing. Listening for a sound and sorting pictures is phonemic awareness: it operates entirely on speech with no print. It is a sensible precursor, but it sits below both decoding and encoding and cannot link them."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-046","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher devotes part of each week to helping students learn new words well enough to use them in their own talk and classroom discussion, before the students meet those words in print. Which statement best explains why building oral vocabulary in this way supports reading comprehension?","options":["A word already known in speech becomes meaningful the moment it is decoded in print, so oral vocabulary sets the ceiling on what an accurate decoder can understand","Using new words in spoken sentences gives students more practice sounding out unfamiliar print, and decoding remains the main barrier to comprehension in fourth grade","Hearing words used in conversation teaches students to work out meanings from context, so the oral work mainly builds a strategy for handling unfamiliar words","Once students decode accurately, comprehension takes care of itself, so oral vocabulary work mainly helps students who still struggle with word reading"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how oral language comprehension supports reading comprehension. Reading comprehension depends on both accurate word reading and language comprehension. A word a student already knows when hearing it carries its meaning into print, so decoding it delivers meaning at once; a word that is decoded but has no entry in the student's oral vocabulary yields sound without sense. Oral vocabulary therefore sets the ceiling on how much of a text accurate decoding can deliver.","dex":{"Using new words in spoken sentences gives students more practice sounding out unfamiliar print, and decoding remains the main barrier to comprehension in fourth grade":"Treating oral vocabulary work as decoding practice and assuming word reading is still the primary barrier to comprehension in the upper elementary grades Saying and hearing new words gives students practice with spoken language, not with print-to-sound mapping, so it does not build decoding. By fourth grade, students who already read accurately are usually limited by word meaning rather than by word reading, which is exactly why the teacher turned to oral vocabulary.","Hearing words used in conversation teaches students to work out meanings from context, so the oral work mainly builds a strategy for handling unfamiliar words":"Mistaking a by-product of vocabulary instruction, such as using context clues, for the reason vocabulary supports comprehension, rather than direct knowledge of word meanings Students may indeed become better at guessing unknown words from context, but that is a side effect rather than the mechanism at work. The gain comes from knowing more words outright, so that fewer words are unknown in the first place. A strategy for coping with unfamiliar words cannot substitute for knowing them.","Once students decode accurately, comprehension takes care of itself, so oral vocabulary work mainly helps students who still struggle with word reading":"Assuming comprehension follows automatically once decoding is accurate, so that language comprehension places no limit on reading comprehension Accurate decoding does not produce understanding on its own; a reader who sounds out every word still has to bring language comprehension to the page. This statement also wrongly confines oral vocabulary work to struggling readers, when it is precisely what allows accurate decoders to make sense of the words they read."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-047","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher models pausing at the end of each section of an informational text to jot down one question the section raised for her, then has students jot down their own questions and check whether later sections answer them. Which comprehension strategy is the teacher primarily teaching?","options":["Clarifying, because students stop where the text stops making sense and use a fix-up strategy to restore meaning","Summarizing, because students reduce each section to its most important ideas and set aside minor details","Questioning, because students generate their own questions about the text and look for answers as they read on","Making connections, because students link what they are reading to their own experiences and to other texts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies that improve comprehension. The teacher is showing students how to turn their own curiosity about a text into questions and then to keep reading with those questions in mind. Generating questions and hunting for answers keeps readers actively building meaning instead of moving passively through the text, which is why self-questioning is taught as a strategy in its own right.","dex":{"Clarifying, because students stop where the text stops making sense and use a fix-up strategy to restore meaning":"Confusing a pause to record a question with a pause because comprehension has broken down; clarifying responds to lost meaning, while questioning responds to curiosity Clarifying is triggered by a breakdown in understanding: the reader notices that meaning has been lost and applies a fix such as rereading or reading ahead. Nothing in this lesson signals confusion. Students are asking about content they do understand in order to find out more, so the demand is curiosity rather than repair.","Summarizing, because students reduce each section to its most important ideas and set aside minor details":"Treating any written response to a section of text as summarizing; summarizing is defined by selecting and condensing content, not by raising questions about it Summarizing asks students to decide what matters across a stretch of text and condense it, dropping the rest. Students here are producing questions, not condensed statements, and they read forward to see whether an answer appears rather than looking back over what they have already read.","Making connections, because students link what they are reading to their own experiences and to other texts":"Assuming that any active response to reading involves relating the text to personal experience or other books; questioning keeps students working with the text's own content Making connections asks students to bring something from outside the text, such as a personal experience or another book, and link it to what they are reading. The questions in this lesson are answered from the text itself, so students are working within the text rather than connecting it to anything beyond it."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-048","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade class reads the sentence: \"The old house groaned as the wind pushed against its loose shutters.\"\n\nWhich question best develops students' understanding of why the author wrote that the house \"groaned\"?","options":["\"What is the dictionary definition of groaned, and what part of speech is it?\"","\"Who is speaking in this sentence, and what do we know about that character?\"","\"What sound does groaned describe, and how would you say it out loud?\"","\"Can a house really groan? What is the author comparing the house to, and what feeling does that comparison create?\""],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to teach figurative language. Having students notice that a house cannot literally groan, then identify what is being compared and what mood the comparison creates, moves them from a literal reading to the author's purpose. That three-part move — notice the impossibility, name the comparison, explain the effect — is the core of instruction in figurative language.","dex":{"\"What is the dictionary definition of groaned, and what part of speech is it?\"":"Treating figurative language as a vocabulary problem; teaching the denotation does not explain the comparison or its effect. A literal definition cannot account for the author's choice, because the point of the word here is precisely that it does not apply literally. Treating it as an unknown vocabulary word misses the device.","\"Who is speaking in this sentence, and what do we know about that character?\"":"Applying a different literary lens to the passage; point of view and character analysis do not address how the comparison builds meaning. This question asks about speaker and characterization, which is a different literary element. The sentence's figurative force comes from the comparison, not from who is talking.","\"What sound does groaned describe, and how would you say it out loud?\"":"Substituting performance of the text for analysis of it; expressive reading is a fluency outcome, not figurative language comprehension. Asking students to produce the sound addresses oral reading and expression. It keeps attention on how the word is performed rather than on what the comparison means."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-049","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Before reading a short play, a fourth-grade teacher introduces the terms dialogue, scene, and stage directions with student-friendly definitions, several examples and non-examples, and a wall chart students will use while reading. Which evaluation of this approach is most accurate?","options":["It is misguided, because vocabulary of this kind is best acquired incidentally through wide reading rather than taught directly","It gives students direct instruction in the labels they need to notice, discuss, and analyze the structural features that carry meaning in a play","It will work only if students memorize the definitions before reading, because knowing definitions is what determines comprehension","It removes the need to teach comprehension strategies, because once students know the terms they will understand the play"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how direct vocabulary instruction supports comprehension of genre features. Terms such as scene and stage directions name the structures that carry a play's meaning: stage directions tell readers what a spectator would see, and scene boundaries signal shifts in time and place. Teaching these words directly with examples and non-examples gives students the language to talk about the structure, and explicit teaching of this kind is more efficient than leaving such low-frequency academic terms to incidental acquisition.","dex":{"It is misguided, because vocabulary of this kind is best acquired incidentally through wide reading rather than taught directly":"Assuming all vocabulary is best acquired incidentally through wide reading; direct instruction is indicated for academic and text-structure terms. Incidental acquisition from wide reading is too slow and too uneven for low-frequency academic and genre-specific terms, and students who most need the words encounter them least. Direct instruction is the recommended route for precisely this vocabulary.","It will work only if students memorize the definitions before reading, because knowing definitions is what determines comprehension":"Equating direct vocabulary instruction with definition drill; robust word knowledge requires depth of processing, not verbatim recall. Memorized definitions produce knowledge of a label without the ability to use it. Effective direct instruction goes beyond the definition to multiple examples, non-examples, and repeated use in reading and discussion.","It removes the need to teach comprehension strategies, because once students know the terms they will understand the play":"Treating vocabulary knowledge as a replacement for comprehension strategies; vocabulary supports meaning construction but does not substitute for it. Words are tools that let students talk about and attend to structure; they do not do the work of making inferences, tracking events, or integrating ideas. Comprehension strategy instruction is still required."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-050","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Before reading a complex informational article about animal migration with her second graders, Ms. Reyes previews three key terms, shows photographs, and leads a brief discussion of why animals move from place to place. Which statement best explains the purpose of this routine?","options":["It lowers the cognitive load of the text so students can spend attention on constructing meaning and using comprehension strategies","It guarantees that students will not meet any unfamiliar words while they read","It converts the lesson from reading instruction into science instruction, which is more concrete for young students","It lets the teacher skip comprehension strategy instruction because the content has already been explained"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how prereading supports work with complex text. Preteaching a small set of high-utility words and activating the key concept supplies part of the knowledge the text assumes, so less of the reader's limited working memory is spent on word recognition and unfamiliar ideas. The attention freed up can go to integrating information and applying strategies such as questioning and summarizing.","dex":{"It guarantees that students will not meet any unfamiliar words while they read":"Expecting prereading vocabulary work to eliminate unknown words; its function is to build the knowledge base the text presupposes, not to exhaust the vocabulary. Previewing three words cannot remove every unfamiliar word, and that is not the aim. The aim is to equip students with the concepts the text leans on and with strategies for handling words that remain unknown.","It converts the lesson from reading instruction into science instruction, which is more concrete for young students":"Mistaking background-knowledge building for a change of subject; concept discussion is a comprehension support, not an abandonment of reading instruction. Discussing the concept serves the reading: it supplies the background knowledge the article assumes. The lesson remains a reading lesson, and the content knowledge is mobilized in service of comprehension.","It lets the teacher skip comprehension strategy instruction because the content has already been explained":"Treating prereading preparation as a substitute for strategy instruction; scaffolding before reading reduces load but does not replace guidance during reading. Preliminary support prepares students to read but does not do the reading for them. Students still need strategy instruction during and after reading to handle the parts of the text that remain demanding."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-051","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A third-grade teacher asks students to retell a story in exactly four sentences: who the character was, what the character wanted, what went wrong, and how it ended. Which skill does this activity primarily develop?","options":["Reading fluency, because students practice saying the story aloud","Phonemic awareness, because students think about the sounds in words","Identifying the main idea and key details, because students must choose the most important elements and leave the rest out","Decoding multisyllabic words, because students meet new words while they retell"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to support students in identifying central ideas and key details. A constrained retell forces a decision about what matters: with only four sentences and four specified elements, students cannot include everything, so they must rank information and cite the specific details that carry the story. That act of selecting and supporting is the same judgment required to state a main idea with evidence.","dex":{"Reading fluency, because students practice saying the story aloud":"Equating oral production with fluency; retelling requires reconstructing meaning, whereas fluency concerns accuracy, rate, and prosody in reading visible text. Fluency practice means reading text accurately and expressively from the page. A retell asks students to put the text away and generate their own sentences, which is a comprehension task, not a reading-rate task.","Phonemic awareness, because students think about the sounds in words":"Placing a meaning-level comprehension task at the word-sound level; the activity makes no demand on attention to individual phonemes. Phonemic awareness operates on the sound structure of spoken words and is typically developed well before third grade. A retell addresses meaning at the level of the whole text.","Decoding multisyllabic words, because students meet new words while they retell":"Confusing an incidental demand of an activity with its instructional target; the task is defined by the decisions about content it requires. Decoding may occur incidentally, but nothing in the task directs attention to print. Retelling works from memory of meaning rather than from words on the page."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-052","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"In a fifth-grade class reading the same work of historical fiction, several students are losing track of the large cast of characters and their relationships. Which adaptation best supports their comprehension?","options":["Have the struggling students take turns reading the novel aloud to build fluency","Give the struggling students a list of comprehension questions to answer on their own after reading","Assign the struggling students a shorter, simpler text on a different topic so that they have less to read","Have the whole class use a character relationship graphic organizer, and complete part of it together with the struggling students before they work on their own"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to differentiate instruction for diverse learners. A graphic organizer externalizes the relationships that the students are failing to hold in mind, and completing part of it with the teacher supplies scaffolding that is faded as students take over. Keeping the whole class on the same text preserves access to the shared discussion and to grade-level content, which is what makes the support differentiated rather than simply reduced.","dex":{"Have the struggling students take turns reading the novel aloud to build fluency":"Applying a fluency routine to a comprehension problem; the diagnosis points to holding and organizing information, not to reading accuracy. Oral reading turns do not address the stated difficulty, which is tracking relationships among characters. Round-robin reading also gives each student little actual reading practice.","Give the struggling students a list of comprehension questions to answer on their own after reading":"Mistaking independent practice for scaffolding; assigning harder work without support is the opposite of differentiation. Independent questions after reading remove support at the point where these students need it most and provide no information until the reading is over. The students need guidance while they construct meaning, not a check afterward.","Assign the struggling students a shorter, simpler text on a different topic so that they have less to read":"Equating differentiation with an easier text; differentiating means changing support and representation, not lowering the content ceiling. Moving these students to a simpler text on another topic lowers both the challenge and the content. They lose the vocabulary, knowledge, and participation in class discussion that the novel provides, and the gap widens."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-053","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fourth-grade teacher models reading a difficult paragraph by saying aloud, \"This sentence doesn't make sense to me. I'm going to reread the sentence before it to see what 'it' refers to.\" Which statement best describes the purpose of this think-aloud?","options":["It models fluent oral reading so that students can imitate the teacher's expression","It makes the normally invisible process of monitoring comprehension and choosing a fix-up strategy visible to students","It supplies background knowledge about the topic of the paragraph","It assesses whether students can answer literal questions about the text"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to model and guide metacognitive monitoring. Skilled readers notice when meaning breaks down and act to repair it, but both steps happen silently. By voicing the moment of confusion and the specific repair chosen, the teacher makes an internal process observable, and repeated modeling of this kind is what allows students to take over the strategy for themselves.","dex":{"It models fluent oral reading so that students can imitate the teacher's expression":"Reducing a think-aloud to a reading model; the point is to externalize cognition, not to demonstrate prosody. Expression and phrasing are fluency outcomes. The content of this think-aloud is a comprehension problem and a repair, and its value lies in exposing thinking rather than in providing a reading performance to copy.","It supplies background knowledge about the topic of the paragraph":"Confusing strategy modeling with content scaffolding; think-alouds teach a process that transfers across topics rather than knowledge of this one. The teacher says nothing about the topic. What is being demonstrated is what to do when comprehension fails, which applies to any topic and any paragraph.","It assesses whether students can answer literal questions about the text":"Mistaking a demonstration for an assessment opportunity; modeling precedes and supports performance rather than measuring it. No student performance is being sampled here. Modeling comes before independent practice and is designed to build the strategy, whereas assessment would call for students to demonstrate it while the teacher observes."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-054","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"According to the Not-So-Simple View of Writing, which statement best explains why systematic, explicit writing instruction is necessary?","options":["Ideas come first; transcription skills should be taught only after students can generate text freely","Writing is speech written down, so once students can talk about a topic they can write about it","Writing draws on transcription, on idea generation, and on self-regulation at the same time, and these compete for limited working memory, so each needs explicit teaching","Writing develops naturally out of reading, so explicit instruction is needed only for handwriting and spelling"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of systematic, explicit writing instruction. The Not-So-Simple View holds that writing is constrained by transcription, by text generation, and by the executive functions that plan, draft, and revise, all drawing on the same limited working memory. Because unautomated transcription consumes capacity that would otherwise go to ideas, and because planning and revision are not picked up incidentally, each component must be taught explicitly and systematically.","dex":{"Ideas come first; transcription skills should be taught only after students can generate text freely":"Sequencing writing components strictly rather than developing them together; unautomated transcription constrains composition from the start. Delaying transcription instruction leaves students struggling with letter formation and spelling at exactly the moment they are trying to compose, which drains working memory. Automating transcription early is what frees capacity for ideas.","Writing is speech written down, so once students can talk about a topic they can write about it":"Holding the Simple View that writing is spoken language written down; this ignores the planning and self-regulation demands unique to writing. This is the Simple View, which treats writing as the transcription of speech and has been superseded. Writing places demands that speech does not, including spelling, punctuation, and the management of a longer, unsupported stretch of language.","Writing develops naturally out of reading, so explicit instruction is needed only for handwriting and spelling":"Assuming writing is a by-product of reading and that only handwriting and spelling need teaching; this neglects idea generation and self-regulation. Reading supports writing but does not teach it automatically. Transcription is one component of a larger set, and text generation and self-regulated revision require their own instruction."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-055","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"After a small-group lesson on making inferences, Ms. Ortiz asks each student to write one sentence naming an inference they made and the sentence from the text that led to it. She reads the slips before forming the next day's groups. Which evaluation of this activity is most accurate?","options":["It is an informal formative assessment that makes students' thinking visible, so she can see whether they are grounding inferences in text evidence and regroup accordingly","It is an informal assessment, but it measures writing mechanics rather than comprehension, so it cannot guide reading instruction","It is a formal summative assessment because it produces a written product that can be scored","It is mainly a comprehension strategy lesson, and its usefulness ends once students have filled in the slip"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to evaluate instructional activities for the thinking they reveal. Asking for both the inference and the supporting sentence requires students to show their reasoning rather than only their answer, which lets the teacher distinguish a student who guessed from one who used evidence. Because the slips are read immediately and used to form the next day's groups, the activity functions formatively: it is ungraded, quick, and directly changes what happens next.","dex":{"It is an informal assessment, but it measures writing mechanics rather than comprehension, so it cannot guide reading instruction":"Judging an assessment by the format of the response instead of by the construct it samples; the response mode does not determine what is measured. Writing is the vehicle here, not the object. The teacher reads the slips for the relationship between claim and evidence, and a slip with weak mechanics can still show sound reasoning, or the reverse.","It is a formal summative assessment because it produces a written product that can be scored":"Treating any written, scoreable product as formal and summative; the distinguishing feature is how the results are used, not their physical form. Formal summative assessment uses standardized administration and scoring to report achievement after instruction. This exit slip has no scoring rubric, is administered by the classroom teacher, and is used to adjust tomorrow's teaching, all of which make it informal and formative.","It is mainly a comprehension strategy lesson, and its usefulness ends once students have filled in the slip":"Seeing an assessment encounter only as practice; the same activity can both develop thinking and yield data, and the data should drive instruction. The activity does give students practice, but the teacher collects and reads the slips for a reason. Overlooking the information they carry discards the evidence that should shape the next lesson."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-056","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fifth-grade teacher wants to strengthen students' listening comprehension as a route to stronger reading comprehension. Which of the following practices are supported by evidence? Select all that apply.","options":["Reading texts aloud that are more complex than what students can read themselves, and leading structured discussion of their meaning","Teaching students to build a mental representation of what they hear by retelling, visualizing, and asking questions as they listen","Explicitly teaching the academic words and complex sentence structures that appear in the read-aloud texts","Relying on silent independent reading as the main way to build listening comprehension","Replacing teacher read-alouds with audio recordings so that students hear fluent reading without needing teacher interaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of how listening comprehension is developed through evidence-based practice. Reading aloud texts beyond students' independent reading level lets them encounter vocabulary, syntax, and ideas they could not access on their own. Structured discussion then requires them to construct and defend meaning, which is the activity that builds comprehension.  [B] The question requires an understanding of how listening comprehension is developed through evidence-based practice. Retelling, visualizing, and questioning while listening are practices that build and check a mental model of the text. These are the same meaning-construction processes used in reading, which is why practicing them with spoken language transfers.  [C] The question requires an understanding of how listening comprehension is developed through evidence-based practice. Word meanings and sentence structures encountered in listening become resources for reading, because comprehension is limited by the same vocabulary and syntax in both modalities. Explicit teaching of these forms is more reliable than hoping students infer them from context.","dex":{"Relying on silent independent reading as the main way to build listening comprehension":"Assuming that any reading activity builds listening comprehension; listening comprehension requires comprehension of language presented to the ear, and develops ahead of reading comprehension through spoken input. Silent reading gives no auditory input, so it cannot develop listening comprehension. It is valuable for fluency and for practicing reading, but it exercises a different route to meaning.","Replacing teacher read-alouds with audio recordings so that students hear fluent reading without needing teacher interaction":"Equating exposure to fluent reading with comprehension instruction; the benefit of read-alouds comes from the interaction around the text, not from hearing the words. A recording supplies fluent speech but removes the interactive talk that makes read-alouds effective. Without discussion, students are not required to construct, check, or revise meaning, and the teacher loses the chance to scaffold."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1,2]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-057","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A second-grade teacher wants to help students make inferences during read-alouds. Which of the following instructional moves would best support that goal? Select all that apply.","options":["Asking students what the text says and what they already know, then helping them combine the two","Using picture clues and a character's actions to figure out a feeling the author does not state","Having students chorally reread the passage to improve their accuracy","Having students copy the sentences that contain the answer to the teacher's question"],"answer":0,"explain":"[A] The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies that improve comprehension. Naming what the text says and what the student already knows, then combining them, is the structure of an inference made visible. Young students need this routine made explicit because they otherwise treat story events as unrelated to their own knowledge.  [B] The question requires an understanding of instructional strategies that improve comprehension. Feelings are rarely stated directly in texts for this age group, so students must read them from pictures and from what characters do. Working from those clues to an unstated feeling is exactly the text-plus-background-knowledge combination that defines inference.","dex":{"Having students chorally reread the passage to improve their accuracy":"Substituting a fluency routine for a comprehension strategy; accurate reading of the words does not produce the unstated meaning. Choral rereading builds accuracy and confidence with print. It asks students to reproduce the words, not to go beyond them, so it does not require the combination of evidence and knowledge that inference demands.","Having students copy the sentences that contain the answer to the teacher's question":"Confusing locating and copying information with constructing meaning; inference requires generating an idea the text does not state. Copying sentences is a transcription task that can be completed without understanding what the sentences imply. Locating a stated answer is the opposite of inferring something that is not stated."},"multi":true,"answers":[0,1]},{"id":"8006-comprehension-058","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A fifth-grade class reads two poems about the same school playground: one written from the point of view of a student who has just transferred to the school, and one written from the point of view of the oldest tree on the grounds.\n\nWhich statement best explains why teaching point of view supports comprehension?","options":["Point of view appears only in fiction, so it has little bearing on understanding most texts","Identifying point of view is mainly a way to sort texts into genres before reading","Point of view matters chiefly because comprehension questions ask students to name the narrator","Point of view establishes who is speaking and what that speaker is in a position to know, so recognizing it lets students judge the limits and reliability of what is presented"],"answer":3,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how teaching literary devices improves comprehension of text. Point of view fixes the position from which events are filtered, and that position determines what the speaker can observe and what interests shape the telling. Once students identify who is speaking, they can ask what this speaker would not know or would report differently, which is what allows them to read the same event critically in two poems.","dex":{"Point of view appears only in fiction, so it has little bearing on understanding most texts":"Restricting point of view to fiction; perspective and stance shape the presentation of information in nonfiction as well. Point of view operates in nonfiction as well, where the author's stance and sources shape what is reported and what is omitted. Dismissing it as a fiction-only feature leaves students without a tool for evaluating informational texts.","Identifying point of view is mainly a way to sort texts into genres before reading":"Treating a literary device as a labeling exercise; the value of point of view lies in explaining how the telling is constrained, not in classifying texts. Genre classification may be a by-product, but the reason to teach point of view is that it explains how information is filtered. Sorting texts does not require students to use the concept while reading.","Point of view matters chiefly because comprehension questions ask students to name the narrator":"Justifying literature instruction by test formats; devices are taught for the interpretive work they enable. Naming the narrator is a recall task that can be done without any effect on interpretation. The device is taught because it changes what the reader takes the text to mean."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-059","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"A second-grade teacher is preparing students to read a short play. Which structural feature most directly helps students understand where and when the events take place?","options":["The cast of characters listed at the beginning","The scene headings and stage directions that describe the setting","The dialogue spoken by the characters","The punctuation at the end of each line of dialogue"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how the structural elements of a genre contribute to a text's meaning. In a play there is no narration to establish time and place, so that information is carried by the scene headings and stage directions. Teaching students to read these parts tells them where to look for the setting that the characters' words take for granted.","dex":{"The cast of characters listed at the beginning":"Expecting one structural element to do the work of another; the cast list identifies participants, not circumstances. The cast list tells students who appears and sometimes how characters are related, but it does not establish the time or place of the action.","The dialogue spoken by the characters":"Overlooking that in drama the setting is supplied outside the spoken lines; dialogue presumes a setting rather than establishing it. Dialogue advances the plot and reveals characters, and characters may mention where they are, but the play's own signal of time and place is the stage direction rather than the speech.","The punctuation at the end of each line of dialogue":"Confusing cues for oral delivery with cues for setting; punctuation guides expression, while stage directions carry the scene information. Punctuation at the end of a line indicates how the line should be delivered, such as whether it is a question or an exclamation. It carries performance information, not information about time or place."}},{"id":"8006-comprehension-060","code":"8006","subtest":"Comprehension and Written Expression","q":"Before a fifth-grade class reads a complex persuasive editorial, the teacher leads a discussion of the issue, has students list what they already know and what they question about it, and previews the signal words that mark a claim and its supporting reasons. Which statement best explains why this approach supports comprehension of the complex text?","options":["It activates and builds background knowledge and gives students a purpose for reading, which reduces the demands the text places on working memory","It ensures that students will agree with the author's opinion before they begin reading","It allows students to read the text without using any comprehension strategies","It effectively shortens the text, and length is the main reason complex texts are difficult"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question requires an understanding of how to support students' reading of complex text. Discussing the issue and recording what students know and question activates relevant knowledge and sets a purpose, so readers meet the text with a framework to attach new information to. Previewing the signal words for claims and reasons adds a structural roadmap, and together these reduce the load on working memory enough for students to attend to the argument itself.","dex":{"It ensures that students will agree with the author's opinion before they begin reading":"Confusing comprehension of a position with agreement with it; understanding an argument does not require accepting its conclusion. The goal is for students to understand the position and how it is supported, not to adopt it. Good readers can summarize and evaluate an argument they reject, and instruction protects that distinction.","It allows students to read the text without using any comprehension strategies":"Treating scaffolding as a replacement for strategy use; supports before reading reduce load but do not perform the comprehension work. Preliminary work prepares students to use strategies more effectively; it does not make strategies unnecessary. Students still need to question, monitor, and summarize as they work through the editorial's reasoning.","It effectively shortens the text, and length is the main reason complex texts are difficult":"Attributing text complexity to length; difficulty is driven mainly by concepts, vocabulary, and structure. Nothing about the text's length changes. What makes it complex is concept density, unfamiliar vocabulary, and the structure of the argument, and the teacher's moves target those rather than the number of words."}}];
